(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.09b1df46ee.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Sequential development of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs, including branching morphogenesis and bronchopulmonary segments","batch":1,"stem":"The respiratory diverticulum first appears as an outgrowth from the ventral wall of the foregut at approximately which gestational age?","choices":{"A":"2 weeks","B":"16 weeks","C":"8 weeks","D":"12 weeks","E":"4 weeks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Langman places the appearance of the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) at about 4 weeks, as an outgrowth from the ventral wall of the foregut; its site is set by retinoic acid from the adjacent mesoderm, which turns on TBX4 in the gut endoderm. At 2 weeks the embryo is still a bilaminar disc with no gut tube to bud from. By 8 weeks the lung is already in the pseudoglandular period (5–16 weeks), branching toward the terminal bronchioles, and 12 and 16 weeks fall later in that same branching phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) buds from the ventral wall of the foregut at about week 4, and retinoic acid from the adjacent mesoderm upregulates TBX4 in the gut endoderm to fix its site. COMLEX: Because trachea and esophagus come from the same foregut tube, faulty partitioning at this stage causes esophageal atresia with or without tracheoesophageal fistula (about 1 in 3,000 births).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — congenital lung anomalies traced to errors in foregut budding and partition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Sequential development of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs, including branching morphogenesis and bronchopulmonary segments","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman undergoes a routine prenatal ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. The fetus is developing normally. At this stage, the lungs are in which phase of development?","choices":{"A":"Embryonic period","B":"Pseudoglandular period","C":"Terminal sac period","D":"Canalicular period","E":"Alveolar period"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At 20 weeks the fetus is in the canalicular period (16–26 weeks), when each terminal bronchiole divides into two or more respiratory bronchioles, each ending in three to six alveolar ducts, and the vascular supply increases steadily (Langman Table 14.1). The pseudoglandular period (5–16 weeks) is already over, and the embryonic period ends with the lung bud and early bronchial branching. The terminal sac period begins at about 26 weeks, and the alveolar period, with mature alveoli, runs from about 36 weeks into childhood. First Aid 2025 draws the boundaries slightly differently (pseudoglandular weeks 5–17, canalicular 16–25, saccular 24 weeks–birth, alveolar 36 weeks–8 years).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the canalicular period (Langman 16–26 weeks; First Aid 16–25 weeks) each terminal bronchiole divides into respiratory bronchioles and then alveolar ducts, while the vascular supply rises steadily. COMLEX: First Aid marks the fetus as respiration-capable only at about week 25, which sets the practical lower limit of viability for a preterm delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — respiratory bronchioles as the transitional zone where conduction meets gas exchange","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Sequential development of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs, including branching morphogenesis and bronchopulmonary segments","batch":1,"stem":"During lung development, the tracheoesophageal ridges fuse to form the tracheoesophageal septum, dividing the foregut into which two structures?","choices":{"A":"Pharynx and larynx","B":"Esophagus and trachea","C":"Trachea and primary bronchi","D":"Larynx and esophagus","E":"Right and left primary bronchial buds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"As the respiratory diverticulum expands caudally, two longitudinal tracheoesophageal ridges separate it from the foregut; when they fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, the foregut is divided into a dorsal esophagus and a ventral trachea (Langman Ch 14). The larynx is not split off by the septum — the respiratory primordium keeps its communication with the pharynx through the laryngeal orifice. The right and left bronchial buds form later from the end of the tracheal tube, not from partition of the foregut. Faulty partition by this septum is what produces esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The tracheoesophageal ridges fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, splitting the foregut into a dorsal esophagus and a ventral trachea; the lung bud stays open to the pharynx through the laryngeal orifice. COMLEX: In 90% of tracheoesophageal defects the upper esophagus ends blindly and the lower segment joins the trachea, so the newborn chokes on feeds and may aspirate gastric contents; look for other VACTERL defects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — tracheoesophageal fistula and other foregut-derived congenital anomalies","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Key events and timing of lung maturation during prenatal and postnatal periods","batch":1,"stem":"During which period of lung development do terminal bronchioles first appear, and respiratory bronchioles and alveoli are notably absent?","choices":{"A":"Embryonic period","B":"Pseudoglandular period","C":"Canalicular period","D":"Terminal sac period","E":"Alveolar period"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the pseudoglandular period (5–16 weeks), branching continues until terminal bronchioles form, but no respiratory bronchioles or alveoli are present yet (Langman Table 14.1). In the canalicular period (16–26 weeks) terminal bronchioles divide into respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts. Terminal sacs (primitive alveoli) appear from about 26 weeks, and mature alveoli only from about 36 weeks into childhood. The embryonic period covers the lung bud and the first bronchial divisions, before terminal bronchioles exist. First Aid 2025 draws the boundaries slightly differently (pseudoglandular weeks 5–17, canalicular 16–25, saccular 24 weeks–birth, alveolar 36 weeks–8 years).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the pseudoglandular period (Langman 5–16 weeks; First Aid 5–17 weeks) the endodermal tubules branch only as far as the terminal bronchioles, with no respiratory bronchioles or alveoli, so respiration is impossible. COMLEX: First Aid ties pulmonary hypoplasia (a poorly developed bronchial tree) to congenital diaphragmatic hernia and bilateral renal agenesis (Potter sequence).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — pulmonary hypoplasia: small lungs with fewer airways and alveoli","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Key events and timing of lung maturation during prenatal and postnatal periods","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant is born at 28 weeks of gestation. Gas exchange in this infant is possible because sufficient primitive alveoli and capillaries have formed. Which period of lung maturation corresponds to this gestational age?","choices":{"A":"Pseudoglandular period","B":"Canalicular period","C":"Terminal sac period","D":"Alveolar period","E":"Embryonic period"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At 28 weeks the lung is in the terminal sac period (26 weeks to birth): terminal sacs (primitive alveoli) form and capillaries come into close contact with them, so that by the end of the seventh month there are enough alveolar sacs and capillaries for gas exchange and a premature infant can survive (Langman Ch 14). The canalicular period (16–26 weeks) has respiratory bronchioles but not yet enough sacs for survival, and the pseudoglandular and embryonic periods have no gas-exchanging units at all. The alveolar period, with mature alveoli, begins at about 36 weeks. First Aid 2025 draws the boundaries slightly differently (pseudoglandular weeks 5–17, canalicular 16–25, saccular 24 weeks–birth, alveolar 36 weeks–8 years).","boardPrep":"Step 1: From the end of month 6, cuboidal cells thin into type I cells in close contact with capillaries, and by the end of month 7 there are enough alveolar sacs and capillaries for gas exchange, so the premature infant can survive. COMLEX: First Aid dates this saccular stage from week 24 to birth, slightly earlier than Langman's 26 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — the respiratory membrane formed where type I cells meet capillary endothelium","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Key events and timing of lung maturation during prenatal and postnatal periods","batch":1,"stem":"Postnatal growth of the lungs is primarily due to which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Increase in the size of existing alveoli","B":"Increase in the number of respiratory bronchioles and alveoli","C":"Formation of new secondary bronchi","D":"Thickening of the alveolar walls","E":"Expansion of the bronchial cartilage rings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Langman states that although alveoli increase somewhat in size after birth, growth of the lungs after birth is due primarily to an increase in the number of respiratory bronchioles and alveoli. Only about one-sixth of the adult number of alveoli is present at birth; the rest form during the first 10 years by continuous formation of new primitive alveoli. Enlargement of existing alveoli is the minor contribution, and the bronchial tree (including secondary bronchi) and its cartilage are laid down long before birth. Alveolar walls actually thin as type I cells flatten — that thinness is what forms the blood–air barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Only about one-sixth of the adult number of alveoli is present at birth; the rest form over the first 10 years, so postnatal lung growth comes mainly from new respiratory bronchioles and alveoli rather than bigger alveoli. COMLEX: First Aid places the alveolar stage from week 36 to about 8 years, when secondary septation turns terminal sacs into adult alveoli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — surface area as a determinant of gas diffusion rate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Functional and clinical importance of surfactant, including mechanism, timing, and physiological rationale","batch":1,"stem":"Surfactant is produced by which cell type in the developing lung?","choices":{"A":"Type II alveolar epithelial cells","B":"Type I alveolar epithelial cells","C":"Clara cells","D":"Goblet cells","E":"Endothelial cells of pulmonary capillaries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surfactant, a phospholipid-rich fluid that lowers surface tension at the air–alveolar interface, is made by type II alveolar epithelial cells, which appear at the end of about 24 weeks (Langman Ch 14; First Aid 2025 has pneumocytes starting at week 20). Type I cells are the thin squamous cells that form the blood–air barrier with the capillary endothelium; they do not secrete surfactant. Club (Clara) cells line the bronchioles and goblet cells secrete mucus in the conducting airways, and capillary endothelial cells contribute only the vascular side of the barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type II alveolar cells appear at the end of about 24 weeks (Langman; First Aid has pneumocytes from week 20) and secrete a phospholipid-rich fluid, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, from lamellar bodies; they are also the stem cells for type I and type II cells. COMLEX: Fetal lung maturity is screened in amniotic fluid by the lecithin–sphingomyelin ratio (≥ 2 healthy, < 1.5 predicts neonatal RDS) and lamellar body count.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type I vs. type II alveolar cells and their roles in the alveolar wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Functional and clinical importance of surfactant, including mechanism, timing, and physiological rationale","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 25 weeks develops progressive respiratory failure shortly after birth. Chest imaging reveals diffuse bilateral opacification. The alveoli contain a protein-rich fluid with hyaline membranes. Which of the following best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Meconium aspiration from in utero breathing movements","B":"Congenital cystic malformation of the lung","C":"Insufficient surfactant leading to alveolar collapse","D":"Tracheoesophageal fistula with aspiration pneumonia","E":"Incomplete separation of the pleural cavities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is respiratory distress syndrome (formerly hyaline membrane disease) of prematurity: with insufficient surfactant, surface tension at the air–blood interface stays high and alveoli collapse during expiration, and the partly collapsed alveoli contain protein-rich fluid, hyaline membranes and lamellar bodies (Langman Ch 14). At 25 weeks type II cells have only just appeared, so surfactant is scarce. Meconium aspiration is a problem of term and post-term infants, a cystic malformation produces a localized mass, a tracheoesophageal fistula presents with feeding problems and aspiration, and a failure of pleural partitioning does not produce diffuse hyaline membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In respiratory distress syndrome the partly collapsed alveoli contain protein-rich fluid, many hyaline membranes and lamellar bodies; Langman says it causes about 20% of newborn deaths. COMLEX: Risk rises with prematurity, maternal diabetes (fetal insulin) and cesarean delivery; give the mother glucocorticoids before a preterm birth and the infant exogenous surfactant, and watch for retinopathy, intraventricular hemorrhage and bronchopulmonary dysplasia with oxygen therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — neonatal atelectasis among the types and causes of atelectasis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl1_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Functional and clinical importance of surfactant, including mechanism, timing, and physiological rationale","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary function of pulmonary surfactant at the time of the first breath?","choices":{"A":"Facilitates oxygen binding to hemoglobin","B":"Stimulates the production of prostaglandins for labor","C":"Reduces surface tension to prevent alveolar collapse","D":"Increases chloride concentration in the lung fluid","E":"Promotes resorption of lung fluid by lymph capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When lung fluid is resorbed at birth, surfactant stays behind as a thin phospholipid coat on the alveolar cells; with the first breath this coat prevents a high-tension air–water interface, so the alveoli do not collapse during expiration (atelectasis) (Langman Ch 14). Surfactant has no role in hemoglobin's binding of O2. Langman does note that surfactant entering the amniotic fluid late in gestation may activate macrophages that raise prostaglandin production and help start labor, but that is a prenatal signal, not its function at the first breath. The high chloride of fetal lung fluid is not produced by surfactant, and the fluid is cleared at birth by blood and lymph capillaries, not by surfactant action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At birth the lung fluid is resorbed by blood and lymph capillaries, leaving surfactant as a thin phospholipid coat, so the first breath meets no high-tension air–water interface and the alveoli stay open in expiration. COMLEX: Surfactant also lowers lung recoil and raises compliance; per the law of Laplace, collapsing pressure = 2 × surface tension / radius, so small alveoli collapse first without it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — mechanics of inspiration and expiration that surfactant makes possible","isHighYield":false}],"2":[{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trachea Structure and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following cell types in the tracheal epithelium serves as the progenitor population for epithelial cell replacement?","choices":{"A":"Small granule cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Basal cells","D":"Brush cells","E":"Ciliated columnar cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Basal cells are stem cells located near the basement membrane that maintain individual cell replacement in the tracheal epithelium. Their nuclei form a prominent row in close proximity to the basal lamina. While nuclei of other cells may reside at this level, they are relatively sparse, so most nuclei near the basement membrane belong to basal cells. These cells are capable of differentiating into the other epithelial cell types, making them the progenitor population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Basal cells, whose nuclei form the row next to the basal lamina, are the stem cells of the tracheal epithelium; ciliated cells (up to 70%), goblet cells (5–15%), brush cells and small granule cells make up the rest. COMLEX: Basal-cell proliferation is what drives squamous metaplasia in smokers, an adaptive but less functional change that can progress to squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 5 — squamous cell carcinoma, which arises from squamous metaplasia of the airway epithelium","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trachea Structure and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic bronchitis undergoes bronchoscopy with biopsy. Histologic examination reveals a thickened basement membrane beneath the tracheal epithelium. This structure primarily consists of which component?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle bundles","B":"Hyaline cartilage","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Densely packed collagenous fibers","E":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The thick \"basement membrane\" characteristic of the tracheal epithelium appears as a glassy or homogeneous light-staining layer approximately 25-40 μm thick. Electron microscopy reveals it consists of densely packed collagenous fibers that lie immediately under the epithelial basal lamina. Structurally, it represents an unusually thick and dense reticular lamina and is part of the lamina propria. In smokers and individuals with asthma, this layer becomes even thicker in response to chronic irritation. Smooth muscle bundles (the trachealis) and hyaline cartilage lie deeper in the wall — the trachealis bridges the free ends of the C-shaped cartilages posteriorly, and the cartilages form their own layer beneath the submucosa — so neither makes up the subepithelial \"basement membrane.\"","boardPrep":"Step 1: The tracheal \"basement membrane\" is a glassy layer of densely packed collagen under the epithelial basal lamina, really an unusually thick reticular lamina belonging to the lamina propria. COMLEX: It thickens in smokers with chronic cough and in asthma, where the thickening is most marked at the bronchioles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — airway remodeling and morphologic findings in asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trachea Structure and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old chronic smoker presents with difficulty clearing airway secretions. A biopsy reveals decreased numbers of cells with approximately 250 cilia per cell that normally provide coordinated sweeping motion. Which structural feature would you expect to see at the apical surface of these cells on electron microscopy?","choices":{"A":"Blunt microvilli with chemosensory receptors","B":"Aggregated ciliary basal bodies appearing as a dark line","C":"Secretory granules filled with mucinogen","D":"Dense-core granules containing catecholamines","E":"Lamellar bodies with phospholipids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ciliated cells are the most numerous tracheal cell type and extend through the full thickness of the epithelium. Each cell has approximately 250 cilia (Pawlina Ch 19; Guyton Ch 38 gives about 200 cilia per airway epithelial cell). Immediately below the cilia is a dark line formed by aggregated ciliary basal bodies, which anchor the cilia to the cell. These cells function as a \"mucociliary escalator\" that serves as an important protective mechanism for removing small inhaled particles from the lungs through coordinated sweeping movements. Blunt microvilli with chemosensory receptors mark brush cells, mucinogen-filled granules mark goblet cells, and dense-core secretory granules mark small granule (Kulchitsky) neuroendocrine cells, so none of these is the apical feature of the ciliated cell.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Each tracheal ciliated cell carries about 250 cilia, anchored by basal bodies that line up as a dark band under the apical surface, and their coordinated sweep moves mucus toward the pharynx. COMLEX: Smoke first desynchronizes ciliary beating and then reduces the number of ciliated cells, so mucus clearance fails and the patient relies on cough.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 15 — the cough reflex that takes over when mucociliary clearance fails","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Bronchi Functional Importance and Histology","batch":1,"stem":"During a pulmonary function test, a medical student observes that bronchial smooth muscle contraction regulates airway diameter. In the intrapulmonary bronchi, this smooth muscle is organized as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Irregularly distributed cells in the submucosa","B":"Scattered bundles within the cartilage plates","C":"Isolated clusters at branching points only","D":"A complete circumferential layer (muscularis)","E":"A longitudinal layer parallel to the airway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A key change in the intrapulmonary bronchus wall is the addition of smooth muscle forming a complete circumferential layer called the muscularis. Initially, the smooth muscle is arranged in interlacing bundles forming a continuous layer. In smaller bronchi, the smooth muscle may appear discontinuous due to its spiral course. Because the smooth muscle forms a separate layer, the bronchial wall has five distinct layers: mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, cartilage layer, and adventitia. Contraction of this muscle regulates the appropriate diameter of the airway. The muscle is a continuous layer around the whole circumference rather than isolated clusters at branch points; any gaps seen in smaller bronchi reflect its spiral course.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intrapulmonary bronchi add a complete circumferential layer of smooth muscle, the muscularis, giving five wall layers (mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, cartilage, adventitia); in smaller bronchi it may look discontinuous because of its spiral course. COMLEX: Bronchospasm in asthma is contraction of this airway smooth muscle on top of mucosal inflammation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — autonomic control of bronchiolar smooth muscle tone","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Bronchi Functional Importance and Histology","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes surgical resection of a lung segment containing a tumor. The surgeon identifies the appropriate bronchopulmonary segment for resection. At the level of the segmental bronchus, which histologic feature distinguishes this structure from the trachea?","choices":{"A":"Cartilage plates arranged around the wall","B":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","C":"Simple cuboidal epithelium lining the lumen","D":"Absence of smooth muscle in the wall","E":"Presence of C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the point where bronchi enter the lungs to become intrapulmonary bronchi, the C-shaped cartilage rings of the trachea are replaced by cartilage plates of irregular shape. These plates are distributed in a linear array around the entire circumference of the wall, giving the bronchi a circular or cylindrical shape, in contrast to the ovoid shape with a flattened posterior wall of the trachea. As bronchi decrease in size due to branching, the cartilage plates become smaller and less numerous, ultimately disappearing when the airway reaches approximately 1 mm in diameter. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium does not distinguish the two, because bronchi keep the same epithelial cell composition as the trachea, and simple cuboidal epithelium belongs to the bronchioles, not to segmental bronchi.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Where bronchi enter the lung, the C-shaped tracheal rings give way to irregular cartilage plates around the whole circumference, so intrapulmonary bronchi are round rather than flattened posteriorly; the plates shrink and vanish at the bronchiole. COMLEX: Each segmental bronchus supplies a bronchopulmonary segment, the unit a surgeon resects; Langman counts 10 segmental bronchi on the right and 8 on the left.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — branching morphogenesis and formation of the bronchopulmonary segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Bronchi Functional Importance and Histology","batch":1,"stem":"An autopsy specimen from a 70-year-old patient shows a main bronchus with the following layers visible on cross-section: mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, cartilage layer, and adventitia. As the bronchi progressively branch and decrease in diameter, which change occurs in the epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Decrease in the height of pseudostratified columnar cells","B":"Transformation from simple cuboidal to stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Replacement of goblet cells with club cells","D":"Increase in thickness of the basement membrane","E":"Complete loss of all ciliated cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The mucosa of bronchi is composed of pseudostratified epithelium with the same cellular composition as the trachea. However, the height of the cells decreases as the bronchi decrease in diameter. The \"basement membrane\" that is conspicuous in primary bronchi quickly diminishes in thickness and disappears as a discrete structure in secondary bronchi. The pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium is maintained throughout the bronchial tree, though it becomes progressively shorter with branching.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial mucosa keeps the tracheal cell mix, but cell height falls as the bronchi narrow, and the thick \"basement membrane\" fades out by the secondary bronchi. COMLEX: In chronic bronchitis and bronchiectasis, patches of this epithelium can change to stratified squamous (metaplasia), which resists injury but clears mucus poorly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis: pathogenesis and morphology of damaged bronchi","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Bronchioles and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A pathology resident examines a lung biopsy and identifies a small airway measuring 0.8 mm in diameter. The airway lacks cartilage plates and submucosal glands. Which cell type would be most abundant in the terminal portion of this airway?","choices":{"A":"Type I pneumocytes","B":"Club cells","C":"Type II pneumocytes","D":"Ciliated columnar cells","E":"Goblet cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This describes a terminal bronchiole, which is the smallest conducting bronchiole and is lined with simple cuboidal epithelium. In terminal bronchioles, club cells are interspersed among ciliated cells and increase in number as ciliated cells decrease along the length of the bronchiole. Club cells are nonciliated cells with a characteristic rounded or dome-shaped apical surface projection. Goblet cells are absent in terminal bronchioles unless the patient is a smoker or exposed to irritants. Pneumocytes are found in alveoli, not bronchioles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchioles (about 1 mm or less) lack cartilage plates and glands; terminal bronchioles have simple cuboidal epithelium in which club cells grow more numerous as ciliated cells thin out, and goblet cells are absent except in smokers. COMLEX: First Aid notes club cells also detoxify inhaled toxins with cytochrome P-450 and act as progenitors for club and ciliated cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 14 — local factors that constrict bronchioles, the airways with no cartilage support","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Bronchioles and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with asthma has a bronchiolar biopsy showing increased smooth muscle thickness. Club cells in her terminal bronchioles secrete a surface-active lipoprotein that prevents luminal adhesion. These cells are characterized by which ultrastructural features?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 250 cilia and prominent basal bodies","B":"Mucinogen granules and lack of cilia at the apical surface","C":"Lamellar bodies containing pulmonary surfactant","D":"Dense-core granules and synaptic contact with nerve fibers","E":"Basal rER, supranuclear Golgi, and secretory granules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Club cells display electron microscopic characteristics of protein-secreting cells. They have well-developed basal rough-surfaced endoplasmic reticulum, a lateral or supranuclear Golgi apparatus, secretory granules that stain for protein, and numerous cisternae of smooth ER in the apical cytoplasm. They secrete a surface-active lipoprotein that prevents luminal adhesion during expiration and produce CC16 protein, an abundant component of airway secretion. The rounded, dome-shaped apical surface lacks cilia, distinguishing them from ciliated cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Club cells look like protein-secreting cells on electron microscopy, and they secrete a surface-active lipoprotein that keeps bronchiolar walls from sticking together in expiration plus a 16-kDa club cell secretory protein (CC16). COMLEX: In COPD and asthma, CC16 falls in airway fluid as club cells are damaged and may rise in serum, so it serves as a measurable lung-injury marker.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — major obstructive lung diseases, in which club-cell injury occurs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Bronchioles and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student studying a histology slide observes respiratory bronchioles with scattered thin-walled outpocketings extending from the lumen. These structures represent the first location in the bronchial tree where which process occurs?","choices":{"A":"Particle filtration","B":"Air humidification","C":"Gas exchange","D":"Mucus secretion","E":"Air conduction only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Respiratory bronchioles constitute a transitional zone in the respiratory system and are involved in both air conduction and gas exchange. Scattered, thin-walled outpocketings called alveoli extend from the lumen of respiratory bronchioles. These alveoli are the sites at which air leaves and enters the bronchiole to allow gas exchange. Respiratory bronchioles are thus the first part of the bronchial tree that allows gas exchange, marking the beginning of the respiratory portion of the system. Particle filtration, humidification, and mucus secretion all belong to the conducting portion (air conditioning happens mainly in the nasal cavities, and mucus comes from goblet cells and glands of the larger passages), so none of them begins at the respiratory bronchiole.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Respiratory bronchioles are a transitional zone: thin-walled alveoli open off their walls, so they both conduct air and exchange gas; distally club cells predominate over ciliated cells. COMLEX: First Aid counts all airways down to the terminal bronchioles as anatomic dead space, and cilia stop in the respiratory bronchioles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space vs. shunt and how dead space is defined","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Alveoli and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn infant develops respiratory distress syndrome due to insufficient production of surfactant. Which cell type is primarily responsible for synthesizing and secreting this substance?","choices":{"A":"Type I pneumocytes","B":"Alveolar macrophages","C":"Type II pneumocytes","D":"Brush cells","E":"Club cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type II alveolar cells, also called type II pneumocytes or septal cells, are secretory cells whose apical cytoplasm is filled with lamellar bodies. These lamellar bodies appear on electron microscopy as stacks of parallel membrane lamellae and are rich in a mixture of phospholipids, neutral lipids, and proteins. These are secreted by exocytosis to form surfactant, an alveolar-lining surface-active agent that reduces surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse. Type II cells account for 60% of alveolar-lining cells by number but cover only 5% of the alveolar surface.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type II alveolar cells make up about 60% of alveolar-lining cells but cover only about 5% of the surface; their apical lamellar bodies hold phospholipids, neutral lipids and proteins secreted by exocytosis as surfactant. COMLEX: Surfactant synthesis begins about week 20 and is mature about week 35 (First Aid), so maternal glucocorticoids are given before a preterm delivery to speed it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant timing, mechanism and clinical importance in the newborn","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Alveoli and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old patient with interstitial lung disease undergoes lung biopsy. Electron microscopy reveals cells that line 95% of the alveolar surface and are joined by occluding junctions to form an effective barrier between air space and the septal wall. These extremely thin squamous cells are incapable of cell division. Which statement best describes these cells?","choices":{"A":"They serve as progenitor cells for alveolar epithelium","B":"They account for 60% of alveolar-lining cells by number","C":"They comprise only 40% of alveolar-lining cells by number","D":"They contain lamellar bodies with phospholipids","E":"They bulge into the air space with a dome-shaped surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type I alveolar cells are extremely thin squamous cells that line most (95%) of the alveolar surface but comprise only 40% of alveolar-lining cells by number. They are joined to one another and to other alveolar epithelial cells by occluding junctions, forming an effective barrier between air space and septal wall components. Type I alveolar cells are not capable of cell division. In contrast, type II pneumocytes account for 60% by number but cover only 5% of the surface and serve as progenitor cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type I alveolar cells are only about 40% of alveolar-lining cells by number yet line about 95% of the surface; they are joined by occluding junctions into a tight air–septum barrier and cannot divide. COMLEX: Because type I cells cannot replace themselves, alveolar repair after injury depends on proliferation of type II cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — layers of the respiratory membrane, including the type I epithelium","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl2_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Alveoli and Cell Types","batch":1,"stem":"Following acute lung injury from smoke inhalation, a patient's lung tissue shows cellular proliferation and restoration of the alveolar epithelium. Which cell type serves as the progenitor cell capable of regenerating both major alveolar cell populations?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar macrophages","B":"Brush cells","C":"Type I alveolar cells","D":"Club cells from terminal bronchioles","E":"Type II alveolar cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In addition to secreting surfactant, type II alveolar cells serve as progenitor cells for type I alveolar cells. Following lung injury, type II pneumocytes proliferate and restore both types of alveolar cells within the alveolus. This regenerative capacity is crucial for lung repair. Hyperplasia of type II alveolar cells is an indicator of alveolar injury and repair. Type I cells cannot divide, and club cells are found in bronchioles, not alveoli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Besides secreting surfactant, type II alveolar cells are the progenitors of type I cells; after lung injury they proliferate and restore both alveolar cell types. COMLEX: Type II cell hyperplasia on a lung biopsy therefore signals alveolar injury with ongoing repair rather than a primary disease of its own.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — pathogenesis of ARDS and diffuse alveolar damage","isHighYield":false}],"3":[{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn infant is noted to have respiratory distress immediately after birth. Imaging reveals bilateral small lungs. Further evaluation shows the infant has a congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Which embryological mechanism best explains the pulmonary findings?","choices":{"A":"Impeded lung expansion in utero due to compression","B":"Abnormal detachment of primitive foregut tissue","C":"Defective branching of the bronchial tree","D":"Abnormal vascular supply from the aorta","E":"Deficiency in surfactant production"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pulmonary hypoplasia is a defect in the development of both lungs that results in decreased lung size. It is caused by abnormalities that compress the lung or impede lung expansion in utero. The most common causes are congenital diaphragmatic hernia, which allows abdominal contents to compress the developing lungs, and oligohydramnios, which reduces the fluid volume necessary for normal lung expansion. Severe hypoplasia is fatal in the early neonatal period. The congenital diaphragmatic hernia physically prevents normal lung expansion during critical developmental periods. Surfactant deficiency is the defect of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, which comes from pulmonary immaturity in preterm infants and produces collapsed alveoli with hyaline membranes, not small lungs from compression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary hypoplasia (both lungs small, one often worse) comes from anything that compresses the lung or blocks its expansion in utero, chiefly congenital diaphragmatic hernia and oligohydramnios; severe hypoplasia is fatal in the early neonatal period. COMLEX: First Aid notes the hernia is usually left-sided and also lists bilateral renal agenesis (Potter sequence) as a cause through lack of amniotic fluid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — key events and timing of prenatal lung maturation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-month-old infant presents with a mass lesion in the left hemithorax. Imaging shows a discrete area of lung tissue that is external to the lung parenchyma and is not connected to the tracheobronchial tree. Angiography reveals an aberrant blood supply from the descending aorta. Which embryological origin best explains this abnormality?","choices":{"A":"Faulty fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges","B":"Failure of lobar bronchi to develop properly","C":"Extra foregut lung bud apart from the main tree","D":"Incomplete recanalization of the bronchial tree","E":"Defective cartilage formation in the bronchi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This describes extralobar pulmonary sequestration, a discrete area of lung tissue characterized by two key features: it is not connected to the airways and has an abnormal blood supply arising from the aorta or its branches. Extralobar sequestration is external to the lung and most commonly presents in infants as a mass lesion. It may be associated with other congenital anomalies. Langman attributes ectopic lung tissue arising from the trachea or esophagus to additional respiratory buds of the foregut that develop independently of the main respiratory system, which is why the tissue never joins the bronchial tree. Faulty fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges instead causes esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula. Failed lobar bronchial development, incomplete recanalization, and defective bronchial cartilage do not produce a separate mass with its own systemic artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pulmonary sequestration is lung tissue with no airway connection and a systemic arterial supply from the aorta or its branches; the extralobar form lies outside the lung, usually presents in infancy as a mass and may come with other congenital anomalies. COMLEX: Before resecting any sequestration, the surgeon must map the anomalous aortic feeding vessel so it can be ligated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — systemic (aortic) origin of the lung's bronchial arterial supply","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"During a routine chest CT scan, a 35-year-old asymptomatic patient is found to have a 3 cm cystic lesion in the middle mediastinum near the hilum. Histologic examination after surgical resection reveals the cyst is lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium and has a wall containing cartilage, smooth muscle, and bronchial glands. From which embryological structure did this lesion most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Mesodermal lung bud","B":"Lateral plate mesoderm","C":"Neural crest cells","D":"Primitive foregut","E":"Pharyngeal pouch"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This describes a bronchogenic cyst, which is the most common type of foregut cyst. Foregut cysts arise from abnormal detachments of primitive foregut during embryonic development and are most often located in the hilum or middle mediastinum. Bronchogenic cysts recapitulate normal airway microanatomy with ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium, bronchial glands, cartilage, and smooth muscle in the wall. Although commonly incidental findings, they may cause symptoms from compression of nearby structures or superimposed infection. The respiratory system develops as a ventral outgrowth from the foregut, and abnormal detachment during this process leads to foregut cyst formation. The lung bud is an endodermal outgrowth of the foregut, not a mesodermal structure, and lateral plate (splanchnic) mesoderm supplies only the cartilage, muscle, and connective tissue around it; neural crest forms PNS ganglia and nearby non-neural structures, and the pharyngeal pouches form the middle ear, tonsil, thymus, and parathyroids, so none of these gives rise to a detached airway-type cyst.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Foregut cysts come from abnormal detachments of the primitive foregut and sit mostly in the hilum or middle mediastinum; a bronchogenic cyst copies a bronchus wall (ciliated pseudostratified columnar lining, bronchial glands, cartilage, smooth muscle). COMLEX: Usually incidental, these cysts come to attention through compression of nearby structures or superimposed infection; First Aid adds they are sharply defined fluid-filled densities on chest x-ray.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — normal bronchial wall histology that a bronchogenic cyst recapitulates","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy has a history of recurrent pneumonias affecting the same area of the left lower lobe. CT imaging reveals a discrete area of lung parenchyma within the left lower lobe that does not appear to communicate with the bronchial tree. Angiography demonstrates arterial blood supply from a branch of the descending thoracic aorta. Which gross pathologic finding would most likely be present in the affected area?","choices":{"A":"Multiple thin-walled cysts filled with clear fluid","B":"Dilated bronchi with cartilage plate deformities","C":"Honeycomb fibrosis with cystic airspaces","D":"Overinflated lung segment with compressed adjacent tissue","E":"Area of lung tissue with recurrent infection and bronchiectasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This describes intralobar pulmonary sequestration, which occurs within the lung parenchyma. Unlike extralobar sequestration, intralobar sequestration usually presents in older children or adults with recurrent localized infections or bronchiectasis in the affected segment. Because the sequestered tissue is not connected to the bronchial tree but is within the lung, secretions cannot drain properly, leading to repeated infections. The abnormal arterial supply from the aorta instead of the pulmonary artery is a defining feature. The recurrent infections cause chronic inflammation and bronchiectasis in the sequestered segment. Multiple thin-walled cysts filled with clear fluid are not how Robbins describes intralobar sequestration, which presents with recurrent localized infection or bronchiectasis, and honeycomb fibrosis is the cystic end-stage scarring of diffuse interstitial fibrosis such as idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, not a focal congenital lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intralobar sequestration sits within the visceral pleura of a lobe, with no bronchial connection and an aortic arterial supply, so secretions cannot drain and the segment becomes repeatedly infected and bronchiectatic. COMLEX: Recurrent pneumonia in the same lower-lobe segment of a child or young adult should prompt imaging for a systemic feeding artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis from conditions that cause chronic localized infection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"A mediastinal cyst is removed from a 28-year-old woman during thoracic surgery. Microscopic examination reveals a cyst lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium. The wall contains mucous glands, smooth muscle bundles, and islands of hyaline cartilage. The cyst is not connected to the tracheobronchial tree. These histologic features are most consistent with which diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal duplication cyst","B":"Pericardial cyst","C":"Thymic cyst","D":"Bronchogenic cyst","E":"Lymphangioma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bronchogenic cysts are characterized by their histologic recapitulation of normal airway microanatomy. They are lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium and have walls containing bronchial glands, cartilage, and smooth muscle. These structures mirror those found in normal bronchi. Bronchogenic cysts arise from abnormal detachments of primitive foregut and are the most common type of foregut cyst. They are rarely connected to the tracheobronchial tree and are most often located in the hilum or middle mediastinum. While commonly discovered incidentally, they may become symptomatic due to compression of nearby structures or superimposed infection. A lymphangioma is a benign lymphatic lesion made of networks of endothelium-lined spaces, with no airway-type epithelium, glands, or cartilage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A mediastinal cyst whose wall contains bronchial glands, hyaline cartilage and smooth muscle under a ciliated pseudostratified columnar lining is bronchogenic, the most common foregut cyst; it is rarely connected to the tracheobronchial tree. COMLEX: First Aid explains such cysts drain poorly, so they can compress the airway and cause recurrent respiratory infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — tracheal and bronchial epithelium and the glands of the submucosa","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"An autopsy of a stillborn infant reveals markedly small lungs bilaterally. The mother's pregnancy was complicated by severe oligohydramnios throughout the second and third trimesters. Microscopic examination of the lungs would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Multiple cartilaginous nodules in lung parenchyma","B":"Cystic spaces lined by bronchiolar epithelium","C":"Intralobar masses with systemic arterial supply","D":"Numerous dilated airways filled with mucopurulent material","E":"Decreased number of alveoli with reduced lung weight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pulmonary hypoplasia results in decreased lung size with reduced numbers of alveoli and airways. The condition is caused by abnormalities that compress the lung or impede lung expansion in utero. Oligohydramnios is a major cause because amniotic fluid provides the mechanical forces necessary for normal lung expansion during fetal development. Without adequate fluid, the lungs cannot expand properly, leading to underdevelopment of the pulmonary parenchyma with fewer alveoli and smaller overall lung size. Severe pulmonary hypoplasia is fatal in the early neonatal period because the infant has insufficient lung tissue to support adequate gas exchange. Cystic spaces lined by bronchiolar epithelium are the honeycomb change of advanced interstitial fibrosis, and intralobar masses with a systemic arterial supply define pulmonary sequestration; neither results from oligohydramnios.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In pulmonary hypoplasia the lungs are small and light with fewer airways and alveoli, because compression or too little amniotic fluid deprives them of the expansion they need to grow. COMLEX: Langman explains why amniotic fluid matters: fetal breathing movements aspirate it, and those movements stimulate lung development and condition the respiratory muscles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — branching morphogenesis that hypoplastic lungs fail to complete","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man develops left lower lobe atelectasis 24 hours after abdominal surgery. Physical examination reveals decreased breath sounds in the left lower lung field. Chest X-ray shows volume loss in the left hemithorax with the mediastinum shifted toward the left. Which mechanism most likely caused this patient's atelectasis?","choices":{"A":"Accumulation of pleural fluid compressing the lung","B":"Airway obstruction with resorption of distal air","C":"Fibrosis preventing full lung expansion","D":"Pneumothorax with pleural air accumulation","E":"Loss of surfactant with alveolar collapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has resorption atelectasis, which stems from obstruction of an airway. Over time, air is resorbed from the distal alveoli, which subsequently collapse. The key diagnostic clue is that the mediastinum shifts toward the atelectatic lung because lung volume is diminished. In the postoperative setting, airway obstruction is most often caused by excessive secretions or mucus plugs within smaller bronchi. Postoperative patients are at high risk due to decreased cough effectiveness from pain, sedation, and immobility. Other causes of resorption atelectasis include bronchial asthma, chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, aspiration of foreign bodies, and intrabronchial tumors. Air in the pleural space from a pneumothorax causes compression atelectasis instead, and with compression atelectasis the mediastinum shifts away from the affected lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Resorption atelectasis follows airway obstruction: trapped air is absorbed and the distal alveoli collapse, lung volume falls, and the mediastinum shifts toward the collapsed side; mucus plugs after surgery are a classic cause. COMLEX: First Aid lists incentive spirometry, and PEEP during mechanical ventilation, to prevent or reverse postoperative atelectasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — how lung collapse alters pulmonary function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer develops progressive dyspnea. Chest X-ray reveals a large right-sided pleural effusion with collapse of the right lung. Physical examination shows the trachea deviated to the left. Which type of atelectasis is most consistent with these findings?","choices":{"A":"Neonatal atelectasis","B":"Contraction atelectasis","C":"Resorption atelectasis","D":"Adhesive atelectasis","E":"Compression atelectasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Compression atelectasis results whenever significant volumes of fluid, tumor, or air accumulate within the pleural cavity, physically compressing the lung and preventing expansion. The key distinguishing feature from resorption atelectasis is that with compression atelectasis, the mediastinum shifts away from the affected lung because the pleural space is occupied by the compressing material. In this case, the large pleural effusion from metastatic disease compresses the right lung, causing the trachea and mediastinum to shift to the left (away from the affected side). Pleural effusions can result from transudates, exudates, or blood. Pneumothorax and tumors are other causes of compression atelectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compression atelectasis occurs when fluid (transudate, exudate or blood), tumor or air fills the pleural cavity, and the mediastinum shifts away from the affected lung. COMLEX: First Aid's physical-exam table matches this: a large effusion is dull to percussion with reduced breath sounds and fremitus, and the trachea deviates away from it; thoracentesis relieves it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 15 — distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions form","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with a history of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis presents with progressive dyspnea and decreased exercise tolerance. Pulmonary function tests show a restrictive pattern. Chest CT demonstrates extensive interstitial fibrosis with volume loss in both lungs. Which type of atelectasis is most likely contributing to this patient's symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Resorption atelectasis","B":"Compression atelectasis","C":"Contraction atelectasis","D":"Adhesive atelectasis","E":"Rounded atelectasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Contraction atelectasis occurs when focal or generalized pulmonary or pleural fibrosis prevents full lung expansion. In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, the progressive scarring and fibrosis of lung tissue physically restricts the ability of the lungs to expand fully, even when the airways are patent and there is no external compression. This leads to chronic volume loss and contributes to the restrictive pattern seen on pulmonary function testing. Unlike resorption and compression atelectasis, which are reversible when the underlying cause is addressed, contraction atelectasis caused by fibrosis is generally irreversible because the fibrotic tissue permanently limits lung expansion. This patient's progressive interstitial fibrosis directly causes contraction atelectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Contraction atelectasis occurs when focal or generalized pulmonary or pleural fibrosis keeps the lung from fully expanding even though the airways are open. COMLEX: First Aid calls it cicatrization atelectasis and gives sarcoidosis as an example; the patient's PFTs show a restrictive pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other major restrictive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn infant presents with respiratory distress and feeding difficulties immediately after birth. Physical examination reveals excessive salivation and coughing with feeding attempts. A nasogastric tube cannot be passed into the stomach. Imaging studies show air in the gastrointestinal tract. Which embryological structure is most likely affected in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Primitive foregut","B":"Lateral plate mesoderm","C":"Neural crest cells","D":"Pharyngeal arch mesenchyme","E":"Mesodermal somites"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This clinical presentation describes tracheoesophageal fistula with esophageal atresia, a congenital tracheobronchial anomaly (Robbins Ch 15). The inability to pass a nasogastric tube indicates esophageal atresia, while the presence of air in the gastrointestinal tract indicates a connection between the trachea and esophagus. Both the trachea and esophagus develop from the primitive foregut. The respiratory system develops as a ventral outgrowth from the foregut, and abnormal separation during embryonic development leads to tracheoesophageal fistula. This is distinct from bronchogenic cysts, which also arise from foregut abnormalities but represent detached portions rather than incomplete separation. The other options do not form the trachea and esophagus: lateral plate (splanchnic) mesoderm supplies only the cartilage, muscle, and connective tissue around the tubes, pharyngeal arch mesenchyme forms the laryngeal cartilages and muscles, neural crest forms PNS ganglia and nearby non-neural structures, and somites are mesoderm that forms muscle, bone, and connective tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trachea and esophagus both arise from the primitive foregut, separated by the tracheoesophageal septum; esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula (about 90% of these defects) explains a failed nasogastric tube plus air in the gut. COMLEX: Polyhydramnios may precede delivery because swallowed amniotic fluid cannot reach the stomach, and after birth gastric contents can reflux into the trachea and cause pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut partition by the tracheoesophageal septum","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 28 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress shortly after birth. The mother's pregnancy was complicated by prolonged rupture of membranes and severe oligohydramnios for several weeks before delivery. Autopsy of the infant reveals bilateral small lungs with markedly reduced weight. Which mechanism best explains the pathogenesis of this infant's pulmonary findings?","choices":{"A":"Genetic mutation affecting surfactant production","B":"Abnormal branching of the bronchial tree during embryogenesis","C":"Lack of mechanical forces necessary for normal lung expansion","D":"Inflammatory damage to developing alveoli","E":"Vascular malformation preventing adequate blood supply"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oligohydramnios is a major cause of pulmonary hypoplasia because amniotic fluid provides the mechanical forces necessary for normal lung expansion during fetal development. The fetus makes breathing movements in utero that draw amniotic fluid into the lungs, creating expansion forces that stimulate lung growth. Without adequate amniotic fluid volume, the lungs cannot expand properly during critical developmental periods, leading to underdevelopment with decreased numbers of alveoli and airways and reduced overall lung size. Severe pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios is often fatal in the early neonatal period because the infant has insufficient lung tissue to support adequate gas exchange. A surfactant defect causes neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, a disease of immature lungs with collapsed alveoli and hyaline membranes; it does not explain small, light lungs after weeks of oligohydramnios.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oligohydramnios causes pulmonary hypoplasia because amniotic fluid supplies the mechanical expansion the developing lung needs; the result is small, light lungs with fewer alveoli. COMLEX: First Aid groups this with bilateral renal agenesis (Potter sequence), where absent fetal urine causes the oligohydramnios.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — prenatal lung maturation, including fetal breathing movements","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Congenital Lung Anomalies and Embryological Associations","batch":1,"stem":"A 2-year-old child presents with recurrent respiratory infections affecting the same region of the right lung. CT imaging reveals a discrete mass of lung tissue in the right lower lobe that appears to be supplied by an aberrant systemic artery arising from the descending thoracic aorta. The lesion is within the visceral pleura of the right lung. Which characteristic most likely distinguishes this lesion from other pulmonary sequestrations?","choices":{"A":"Connection to the tracheobronchial tree","B":"External location relative to normal lung parenchyma","C":"Association with other congenital anomalies","D":"Presence of normal respiratory epithelium","E":"Presentation in later childhood with recurrent infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is intralobar pulmonary sequestration: a segment of lung tissue within the visceral pleura of the lung, with no connection to the airways and an arterial supply from a systemic vessel, usually the aorta (Robbins Ch 15). What distinguishes it from extralobar sequestration is presentation in older children with recurrent localized infection, because secretions cannot drain from tissue that has no bronchial connection. Extralobar sequestration lies outside the lung with its own pleural covering, usually presents in infancy as a mass, and is the type associated with other congenital anomalies. Lack of a bronchial connection and systemic arterial supply are shared by both types, so they do not distinguish this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intralobar sequestration lies inside a lobe's visceral pleura, while extralobar sequestration is external to the lung with its own pleura; the intralobar form usually shows up in older children with recurrent localized infection or bronchiectasis. COMLEX: The systemic artery supplying a sequestration usually arises from the aorta, often the descending thoracic aorta, and must be identified before resection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — dual pulmonary and systemic (bronchial) blood supply of the lung","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has a 5 cm cystic mass resected from the middle mediastinum just below the carina. The pathologist reports that it arose from an abnormal detachment of the primitive foregut and must now assign it to one of three subtypes: bronchogenic, esophageal, or enteric. Which feature of the specimen is the basis for this subclassification?","choices":{"A":"Tissue makeup of the cyst wall","B":"Position within the mediastinum","C":"Size of the cyst at resection","D":"Character of the fluid it contains","E":"Presence of an airway connection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins groups bronchogenic, esophageal, and enteric cysts together as foregut cysts, all arising from abnormal detachments of the primitive foregut, and separates them by the structure of the cyst wall. A wall that recapitulates a bronchus (ciliated pseudostratified columnar lining with bronchial glands, cartilage, and smooth muscle) makes the cyst bronchogenic, the most common type. Position is not the discriminator, because foregut cysts as a group sit mostly in the hilum or middle mediastinum. Cyst size and the character of the fluid vary within each subtype and do not define it. A connection to the airway is not the basis either: a bronchogenic cyst is rarely connected to the tracheobronchial tree, and esophageal and enteric cysts have no reason to be.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins sorts foregut cysts into bronchogenic (most common), esophageal and enteric according to the structure of the cyst wall, not their position. COMLEX: Most are found incidentally on imaging; resection is prompted by compression of mediastinal structures or superimposed infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — bronchial wall layers used to recognize a bronchogenic wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"A fetus at 32 weeks gestation undergoes prenatal ultrasound that reveals a discrete mass of echogenic lung tissue outside the normal right lung with its own pleural covering. The mass appears to be supplied by a systemic artery. If this infant survives to delivery, which gross pathologic finding would most likely be present?","choices":{"A":"Cystic mass connected to the trachea","B":"Mass with its own pleura and systemic arterial supply","C":"Bilateral reduction in lung size and weight","D":"Area of lung parenchyma with permanently dilated bronchi","E":"Mediastinal cyst with mucoid contents"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is extralobar pulmonary sequestration: a discrete mass of lung tissue outside the normal lung, invested by its own pleura, with no connection to the airways and an arterial supply from a systemic artery, usually the aorta or a branch (Robbins Ch 15). It usually presents in infancy as a mass lesion and is often associated with other congenital anomalies. A cyst connected to the trachea is not a sequestration (sequestrations lack an airway connection), and a mediastinal cyst with mucoid contents describes a bronchogenic cyst. Bilateral small lungs describe pulmonary hypoplasia, and dilated bronchi within lung parenchyma describe bronchiectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extralobar sequestration is lung tissue outside the normal lung, invested by its own pleura, with no airway connection and a systemic arterial supply; it usually presents in infancy as a mass and may be associated with other congenital anomalies. COMLEX: Prenatal ultrasound showing an echogenic thoracic mass fed by a systemic artery should prompt a postnatal search for associated anomalies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — bronchial (systemic) arteries and their origin from the aorta","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Gross and Microscopic Findings of Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has increasing tachypnea and retractions. Chest radiograph shows a markedly hyperlucent, distended left upper lobe that compresses the left lower lobe. The lobe is resected. Its bronchus is patent, with no intraluminal mass, mucus plug, or foreign material, and the lobe has a normal pulmonary arterial supply. Which structural abnormality most likely underlies this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Systemic artery arising from the aorta","B":"No connection to the tracheobronchial tree","C":"Protease destruction of alveolar walls","D":"Hypoplasia of the bronchial wall cartilage","E":"Reduced alveolar number from compression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is congenital lobar overinflation (congenital lobar emphysema), which Robbins lists among the congenital lung anomalies and attributes most often to hypoplasia of the bronchial cartilage. The poorly supported bronchus behaves as a ball valve, letting air in on inspiration but not out on expiration, so the lobe overdistends; the overinflation becomes dangerous when the distended lobe compresses the adjacent uninvolved lung. A systemic artery arising from the aorta defines pulmonary sequestration, which presents as a mass of nonaerated tissue, and this lobe has a normal pulmonary supply. Lack of any connection to the tracheobronchial tree describes sequestration or a bronchogenic cyst, neither of which could trap inspired air. Protease destruction of alveolar walls is the mechanism of acquired emphysema such as that of α1-antitrypsin deficiency, not a lesion of a 3-week-old. Reduced alveolar number from compression in utero is pulmonary hypoplasia, which produces small lungs rather than a distended lobe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Congenital lobar overinflation most often results from hypoplasia of the bronchial cartilage, so the floppy bronchus acts as a ball valve that lets air in on inspiration but not out on expiration. COMLEX: The danger is the overdistended lobe compressing the remaining lung, which causes tachypnea and retractions in a young infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — other forms of emphysema associated with lung overinflation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"Five hospitalized patients each have a partially collapsed lung on chest imaging. Assuming the underlying process is appropriately treated, which patient's collapsed lung is least likely to return to full expansion?","choices":{"A":"Postoperative man with a mucus plug in a lower-lobe bronchus","B":"Child with an aspirated peanut in the right main bronchus","C":"Woman with a large malignant pleural effusion against the lung","D":"Man with dense pleural fibrosis encasing the lower lobe","E":"Young man with a large spontaneous pneumothorax"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dense pleural fibrosis causes contraction atelectasis: focal or generalized pulmonary or pleural fibrosis prevents full lung expansion, and Robbins states that atelectasis is reversible except in cases caused by fibrosis. Removing a mucus plug after surgery or an aspirated peanut from the main bronchus relieves resorption atelectasis, and the alveoli that collapsed as air was absorbed behind the obstruction can reinflate. Draining a malignant effusion or evacuating a pneumothorax removes the fluid or air that was causing compression atelectasis, so the lung re-expands once the pleural space is cleared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atelectasis is a reversible disorder except when fibrosis causes it, because scar in the lung or pleura physically blocks re-expansion, whereas removing an obstruction or draining the pleural space lets the lung reinflate. COMLEX: Rarely, asbestos causes diffuse visceral pleural fibrosis that in advanced cases binds the lung to the chest wall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — asbestos-related disease, including pleural plaques and diffuse pleural fibrosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a right lower lobe that stays collapsed for several days after upper abdominal surgery because thick secretions block its bronchus. His oxygen saturation has fallen to 90% on room air. If the lobe is not re-expanded, which further complication is it most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Permanent fibrous contraction of the lobe","B":"Bulla formation with rupture into the pleura","C":"Superimposed infection of the airless lobe","D":"Hyaline membranes from loss of surfactant","E":"Interstitial emphysema of the mediastinum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins notes that significant atelectasis reduces oxygenation and predisposes to infection; secretions trapped in a collapsed, unventilated lobe after surgery are a setting for postoperative pneumonia. Permanent fibrous contraction is wrong because atelectasis from airway obstruction is reversible; only atelectasis caused by fibrosis is not. Bullae are large subpleural air spaces of emphysema, and a collapsed lobe holds less air, not more. Hyaline membranes mark diffuse alveolar damage or neonatal surfactant deficiency, not an adult lobe collapsed by a mucus plug. Interstitial emphysema follows alveolar tears from surges in intraalveolar pressure, the opposite of an airless lobe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Significant atelectasis lowers oxygenation and predisposes to infection, since secretions are trapped in an unventilated lobe. COMLEX: Postoperative mucus-plug atelectasis is prevented and treated with incentive spirometry and airway clearance; First Aid also lists PEEP for ventilated patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 2 — pneumonia classification, including hospital-acquired pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl3_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Atelectasis Types and Causes","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 30 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress syndrome shortly after birth despite receiving surfactant replacement therapy. Chest X-ray shows diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities. Which type of atelectasis is most likely contributing to this infant's respiratory distress?","choices":{"A":"Compression atelectasis from pleural effusion","B":"Resorption atelectasis from airway obstruction","C":"Contraction atelectasis from pulmonary fibrosis","D":"Rounded atelectasis from asbestos exposure","E":"Neonatal atelectasis from surfactant deficiency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome is caused by surfactant deficiency in premature infants. Robbins defines neonatal atelectasis as incomplete expansion of the lungs; in RDS, surfactant deficiency leaves the alveoli unable to stay open. Surfactant reduces surface tension in the alveoli, preventing their collapse during expiration. When surfactant is deficient, as occurs in premature infants whose type II pneumocytes have not yet matured enough to produce adequate surfactant, the alveoli tend to collapse, leading to neonatal atelectasis. This differs from other forms of atelectasis: resorption requires airway obstruction, compression requires pleural space occupation, and contraction requires fibrosis. Although surfactant replacement therapy is given, it may not completely prevent atelectasis in very premature infants with severe surfactant deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins defines neonatal atelectasis as incomplete expansion of the lungs; in respiratory distress syndrome, too little surfactant leaves alveoli unable to stay open. First Aid calls this adhesive atelectasis. COMLEX: On chest x-ray the lung fields look ground-glass, and the risk is highest with prematurity, maternal diabetes and cesarean delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant timing and the physiology of the newborn's first breath","isHighYield":false}],"6":[{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman undergoes spirometry testing as part of a pulmonary function assessment. During normal quiet inspiration, her alveolar pressure decreases to approximately -1 cm H₂O. Which mechanism best explains how this negative alveolar pressure facilitates gas exchange?","choices":{"A":"Creates a pressure gradient that draws air into the alveoli","B":"Increases surfactant production by type II pneumocytes","C":"Enhances oxygen diffusion across the alveolar-capillary membrane","D":"Reduces dead space ventilation in conducting airways","E":"Stimulates carbon dioxide release from pulmonary capillaries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"During inspiration, alveolar pressure must fall below atmospheric pressure to cause inward flow of air into the alveoli. When the glottis is open and no air is flowing, pressures throughout the respiratory tree equal atmospheric pressure, which is zero reference pressure. The decrease in alveolar pressure to approximately -1 cm H₂O during normal quiet inspiration creates a pressure gradient between the atmosphere and the alveoli, pulling 0.5 liter of air into the lungs in the 2 seconds required for normal quiet inspiration. This pressure-driven flow is the fundamental mechanism that brings fresh air containing oxygen into the alveoli where gas exchange occurs. Without this negative pressure relative to atmosphere, air would not flow into the lungs. The pressure drop only moves air: it does not drive surfactant secretion by type II alveolar cells, it does not shrink the dead space (the air left in the nose, pharynx, and trachea that never reaches gas-exchange areas), and CO2 leaves the capillaries by diffusing down its own partial pressure gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With the glottis open and no airflow, airway and alveolar pressures equal atmospheric (0 cm H2O); in quiet inspiration alveolar pressure falls to about −1 cm H2O, enough to draw 0.5 L in about 2 seconds, and in expiration it rises to about +1 cm H2O. COMLEX: The OMM course frames the same chain clinically: diaphragm down and ribs up enlarge the thorax and lower intrathoracic pressure, so restricted rib or diaphragm motion blunts this gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — step-by-step movement of O2 and CO2 from atmosphere to tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is studying the elastic properties of lungs using a compliance diagram. When comparing air-filled lungs to saline-filled lungs at the same transpulmonary pressure, the air-filled lungs require approximately three times greater pressure to achieve the same volume expansion. Which component accounts for approximately two-thirds of the total elastic forces in air-filled lungs?","choices":{"A":"Elastin fibers interwoven among lung parenchyma","B":"Collagen fibers stretched during lung expansion","C":"Cartilage rings providing airway structural support","D":"Fluid-air surface tension in the alveoli","E":"Smooth muscle tone in bronchiolar walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The compliance diagram comparison demonstrates that transpulmonary pressures required to expand air-filled lungs are about three times as great as those required to expand lungs filled with saline solution. This difference reveals that the tissue elastic forces tending to cause collapse of air-filled lungs represent only about one-third of the total lung elasticity, whereas the fluid-air surface tension forces in the alveoli represent about two-thirds. When lungs are filled with saline solution, there is no air-fluid interface and therefore no surface tension effect; only tissue elastic forces are operative. In air-filled lungs, both tissue elastic forces from elastin and collagen fibers and surface tension forces contribute to compliance, with surface tension being the dominant component. Airway cartilage and bronchiolar smooth muscle tone are not part of this split; Guyton divides lung elasticity only into tissue elastic forces and surface tension forces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Air-filled lungs need about three times the transpulmonary pressure of saline-filled lungs to expand, so tissue elastic forces (elastin and collagen) are only about one-third of lung elasticity and alveolar fluid–air surface tension is about two-thirds. COMLEX: First Aid notes surfactant lowers surface tension, lung recoil and the tendency to collapse while raising compliance, which is why its absence stiffens the newborn lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant's mechanism and its importance at the first breath","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 28 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress syndrome. The infant's alveoli have an average radius of 50 micrometers instead of the normal 100 micrometers, and surfactant production is severely deficient. Compared to normal alveoli with adequate surfactant, approximately how much greater is the pressure required to keep these smaller alveoli from collapsing?","choices":{"A":"1.5 times greater","B":"2 times greater","C":"3 times greater","D":"4.5 times greater","E":"9 times greater"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pressure caused by surface tension is inversely related to alveolar radius, according to the formula: Pressure = (2 × Surface tension) / Radius. When alveoli have half the normal radius (50 instead of 100 micrometers), the pressure doubles. Additionally, for normal-sized alveoli with adequate surfactant, the pressure is about 4 cm H₂O, but without surfactant, the pressure would be about 18 cm H₂O, or 4.5 times as great. Combining both effects: halving the radius doubles the pressure, and lacking surfactant increases it 4.5 times, resulting in approximately 9 times greater pressure (2 × 4.5 = 9). This explains why premature infants with surfactant deficiency have extreme difficulty expanding their lungs, as their small alveoli and lack of surfactant create tremendous collapsing pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collapsing pressure = 2 × surface tension / radius: a 100-µm alveolus with normal surfactant needs about 4 cm H2O, the same alveolus lined with pure water about 18 cm H2O (4.5×), and halving the radius doubles the pressure. COMLEX: Premature infants have small alveoli and little surfactant (secretion begins months 6–7), so Guyton describes respiratory distress syndrome as fatal without strong measures such as continuous positive pressure breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — neonatal atelectasis from surfactant deficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a tidal volume of 500 mL and a physiological dead space of 300 mL. His respiratory rate is 12 breaths per minute. What is his alveolar ventilation rate, and how does the increased dead space affect systemic homeostasis?","choices":{"A":"6000 mL/min; adequate oxygen delivery but impaired CO₂ elimination","B":"2400 mL/min; impaired gas exchange leading to hypoxemia and hypercapnia","C":"3600 mL/min; normal homeostasis maintained through compensatory mechanisms","D":"4200 mL/min; minimal impact on arterial blood gases","E":"1200 mL/min; severe respiratory acidosis and tissue hypoxia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alveolar ventilation per minute equals respiratory rate times the difference between tidal volume and physiological dead space: V̇A = Frequency × (VT - VD) = 12 × (500 - 300) = 2400 mL/min. This is significantly below the normal alveolar ventilation of approximately 4200 mL/min (calculated as 12 × [500 - 150]). The increased physiological dead space means that a larger portion of each breath does not participate in gas exchange. This reduction in alveolar ventilation directly impairs the ability to bring fresh oxygen to the alveoli and remove carbon dioxide, leading to hypoxemia and hypercapnia. These disturbances disrupt systemic homeostasis by reducing oxygen delivery to tissues and causing respiratory acidosis from CO₂ retention, which affects cellular metabolism and pH balance throughout the body.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar ventilation = rate × (tidal volume − physiologic dead space); normally 12 × (500 − 150) = 4200 mL/min. Physiologic dead space adds alveoli with absent or poor blood flow to the anatomic dead space and can reach 10 times anatomic (1–2 L) in disease. COMLEX: First Aid estimates it with the Bohr equation, VD = VT × (PaCO2 − PECO2)/PaCO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space vs. physiologic shunt in COPD and other diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"During heavy exercise, a healthy 25-year-old athlete increases her respiratory rate to 40 breaths per minute and her tidal volume to 3000 mL. Assuming normal dead space of 150 mL, approximately how much has her minute respiratory volume increased compared to rest, and what is the primary homeostatic benefit?","choices":{"A":"5-fold increase; maintains adequate tissue oxygenation during increased metabolic demand","B":"10-fold increase; prevents lactic acid accumulation in muscles","C":"30-fold increase; maximizes ATP production through oxidative phosphorylation","D":"20-fold increase; supports increased oxygen consumption and CO₂ elimination","E":"50-fold increase; enables sustained aerobic metabolism at peak performance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Minute respiratory volume equals respiratory rate times tidal volume. At rest: 12 breaths/min × 500 mL = 6000 mL/min or 6 L/min. During this heavy exercise: 40 breaths/min × 3000 mL = 120,000 mL/min or 120 L/min. This represents a 20-fold increase (120/6 = 20). The primary homeostatic benefit is maintaining systemic homeostasis during the dramatically increased metabolic demand of exercise. During heavy exercise, oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production can increase to about 20 times normal, and the respiratory system must match this demand to maintain arterial blood gas levels. The increased ventilation ensures adequate oxygen delivery to working muscles and efficient removal of metabolically produced CO₂, thereby maintaining pH balance and preventing metabolic acidosis despite the intense metabolic activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minute respiratory volume = tidal volume × rate, about 6 L/min at rest (500 mL × 12/min); rate can reach 40–50/min and tidal volume can approach vital capacity, giving more than 200 L/min. COMLEX: Most people cannot sustain more than half to two-thirds of these maximal values for longer than a minute, so ventilatory reserve shrinks quickly once lung disease limits it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 12 — neurogenic control of ventilation during exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with pulmonary fibrosis has reduced lung compliance requiring greater work of breathing. During normal quiet breathing, approximately what percentage of total body energy expenditure is typically devoted to pulmonary ventilation in a healthy person, and how might this change in disease?","choices":{"A":"1-2% at rest; can increase to 15-20% in severe pulmonary disease","B":"3-5% at rest; can rise as much as 50-fold in heavy exercise or lung disease","C":"10-15% at rest; can reach 70% during respiratory disease exacerbations","D":"20-25% at rest; minimal change even in advanced disease","E":"30-40% at rest; decreases with disease as breathing becomes shallower"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During normal quiet respiration, only 3% to 5% of the total energy expended by the body is required for pulmonary ventilation. This low energy cost reflects the efficiency of the respiratory system under normal conditions. However, during heavy exercise or in patients with decreased pulmonary compliance or increased airway resistance, the energy required can increase as much as 50-fold. This can become a major limitation on exercise intensity, as the person's ability to provide enough muscle energy for the respiratory process alone may be exceeded. In patients with pulmonary fibrosis or other conditions that decrease compliance, the work required to expand the lungs against increased elastic forces is substantially greater, so the energy cost of breathing can become a major limit on exercise. A resting cost of 20% to 25% with little change in disease contradicts Guyton's figures of 3% to 5% at rest and up to a 50-fold rise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton puts the energy cost of quiet breathing at 3–5% of total body expenditure, rising as much as 50-fold in heavy exercise, especially with high airway resistance or low compliance; the pulmonary OMM course quotes about 2–3% at baseline and 30–35% in significant lung disease. COMLEX: OMT that improves rib and diaphragm mechanics (rib raising, diaphragmatic release) aims to lower this work of breathing as an adjunct to medical care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other restrictive diseases that stiffen the lung","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a spinal cord injury has paralysis of his intercostal muscles but preserved diaphragmatic function. During quiet breathing, which statement best describes his respiratory mechanics?","choices":{"A":"Inspiration primarily achieved by diaphragmatic contraction; expiration by elastic recoil","B":"Both inspiration and expiration require active muscle contraction","C":"Inspiration requires rib cage elevation; expiration requires abdominal muscle contraction","D":"Neither inspiration nor expiration can occur without intercostal muscle function","E":"Accessory muscles must compensate for all respiratory movements"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal quiet breathing is accomplished almost entirely by movement of the diaphragm. During inspiration, contraction of the diaphragm pulls the lower surfaces of the lungs downward, causing expansion and air inflow. During expiration, the diaphragm simply relaxes, and the elastic recoil of the lungs, chest wall, and abdominal structures compresses the lungs and expels the air. Thus, under resting conditions, expiration is almost entirely a passive process caused by elastic recoil, with no active muscle contraction required. While the intercostal muscles normally assist by raising the rib cage during inspiration, the diaphragm alone can accomplish adequate ventilation for quiet breathing. During heavy breathing, however, the elastic forces are not powerful enough to cause the necessary rapid expiration, and extra force would normally be achieved by contraction of the abdominal muscles, which this patient could not perform.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Quiet breathing is almost entirely diaphragmatic: its contraction pulls the lung bases down in inspiration, and in expiration it simply relaxes while elastic recoil of lungs, chest wall and abdomen pushes air out. COMLEX: The diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), so a spinal injury below C5 spares it, while phrenic injury elevates the hemidiaphragm on x-ray.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — the ventral respiratory group, silent in quiet breathing when expiration is passive","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"During a pulmonary physiology lecture, a student learns that muscles elevating the chest cage are classified as muscles of inspiration while those depressing the chest cage are muscles of expiration. Which muscle group functions primarily to depress the rib cage during forced expiration?","choices":{"A":"External intercostals pulling the upper ribs forward","B":"Sternocleidomastoid lifting the sternum upward","C":"Anterior serrati elevating the ribs","D":"Scaleni muscles lifting the first two ribs","E":"Abdominal recti pushing abdominal contents upward"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The muscles that pull the rib cage downward during expiration are mainly the abdominal recti, which have the powerful effect of pulling downward on the lower ribs while simultaneously compressing the abdominal contents upward against the diaphragm. The internal intercostals also function as expiratory muscles. All the other muscles listed are muscles of inspiration: external intercostals raise the rib cage by pulling the upper ribs forward; sternocleidomastoid muscles lift upward on the sternum; anterior serrati lift many of the ribs; and scaleni lift the first two ribs. The classification is based on whether muscles elevate the chest cage (inspiration) or depress it (expiration), and the abdominal muscles are particularly important during forced expiration because they compress the lungs by pushing the diaphragm upward.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Muscles that depress the rib cage are expiratory: chiefly the abdominal recti, which pull the lower ribs down while compressing abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, plus the internal intercostals. COMLEX: In rib dysfunction diagnosis, an exhalation (depressed) rib dysfunction is treated with the upper rib of the group as the key rib, using muscle energy with the rib's attaching muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 15 — cough reflex, which uses forceful abdominal and internal intercostal contraction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher measures pleural pressure during normal breathing and notes that at the beginning of inspiration it is approximately -5 cm H₂O, and during inspiration it becomes more negative, reaching about -7.5 cm H₂O. Which mechanism best explains why pleural pressure is normally negative?","choices":{"A":"Active pumping of fluid out of the pleural space by lymphatic channels","B":"Continuous suction applied by respiratory muscles during breathing cycle","C":"Elastic recoil of lungs creating constant pull away from chest wall","D":"Surfactant production reducing surface tension in pleural space","E":"Atmospheric pressure compression of the thoracic cavity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal pleural pressure is a slight suction, meaning a slightly negative pressure. This negative pressure exists because the lung is an elastic structure that collapses like a balloon and expels all its air through the trachea whenever there is no force to keep it inflated. The elastic forces of the lungs therefore tend to pull the lung away from the chest wall. This creates a slight suction between the visceral pleura on the surface of the lung and the parietal pleura on the inner wall of the thoracic cavity. The lungs are held to the thoracic wall as if glued there, with a thin layer of pleural fluid providing lubrication. The continual suction (negative pressure) of about -5 cm H₂O is required to hold the lungs open to their resting level. During inspiration, expansion of the chest cage pulls outward on the lungs with greater force and creates more negative pleural pressure, averaging about -7.5 cm H₂O.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pleural pressure is a slight suction, about −5 cm H2O at the start of inspiration and about −7.5 cm H2O at the end, because the elastic lung keeps pulling away from the chest wall while lymphatics continually remove excess pleural fluid. COMLEX: First Aid notes this negative intrapleural pressure is what prevents atelectasis at FRC.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 14 — role of negative pleural pressure in keeping the lungs expanded","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"During a pulmonary physiology experiment, a researcher measures the transpulmonary pressure at different points in the respiratory cycle. At the end of normal expiration, the transpulmonary pressure is approximately +5 cm H₂O. Which statement best describes the relationship between transpulmonary pressure and lung volume?","choices":{"A":"Transpulmonary pressure equals atmospheric pressure minus alveolar pressure","B":"Transpulmonary pressure falls as lung volume increases during inspiration","C":"Transpulmonary pressure is the difference between alveolar and pleural pressure","D":"Transpulmonary pressure directly measures airway resistance during breathing","E":"Transpulmonary pressure indicates the work required by respiratory muscles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transpulmonary pressure is the difference between alveolar pressure and pleural pressure (Guyton Ch 38). At the end of normal expiration alveolar pressure is 0 and pleural pressure about −5 cm H₂O, so transpulmonary pressure is about +5 cm H₂O (Costanzo: alveolar minus intrapleural pressure, +5 cm H₂O at rest). It is a measure of the elastic recoil forces tending to collapse the lungs, and it rises — it does not fall — as the lungs inflate, reaching about +7.5 cm H₂O at the end of a normal inspiration; each unit rise expands the lungs by an amount set by compliance. The difference between atmospheric and alveolar pressure is what drives airflow, not transpulmonary pressure, and transpulmonary pressure measures neither airway resistance nor the work of the respiratory muscles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transpulmonary pressure = alveolar pressure − pleural pressure; it measures the lung's elastic recoil (\"recoil pressure\") at each moment and rises as the lung inflates. Lung compliance is the volume change per unit change in transpulmonary pressure, about 200 mL/cm H2O for both lungs. COMLEX: First Aid lists lower compliance in fibrosis, pneumonia, ARDS and pulmonary edema, and higher compliance in emphysema and older adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — principle of maximum expiratory flow and dynamic airway compression","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about the compliance of the lungs and thorax together. In a normal adult, the total compliance of the combined lung-thorax system is approximately 110 mL per cm H₂O pressure, which is about half that of the lungs alone. Which factor primarily accounts for the reduced compliance of the combined system compared to lungs alone?","choices":{"A":"Increased surface tension from alveolar fluid accumulation","B":"Elastic and viscous resistance of the thoracic cage","C":"Airway resistance from turbulent flow in bronchi","D":"Reduced surfactant production during chest wall movement","E":"Compression of pulmonary vasculature by chest expansion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thoracic cage has its own elastic and viscous characteristics, and even if the lungs were not present in the thorax, muscular effort would still be required to expand the thoracic cage. When measured in a totally relaxed or paralyzed subject, almost twice as much pressure is required to inflate the combined lung-thorax system compared with inflating the lungs alone after removal from the chest cage. Therefore, the compliance of the combined lung-thorax system is almost exactly half that of the lungs alone, measuring approximately 110 mL/cm H₂O pressure for the combined system compared with 200 mL/cm H₂O for the lungs alone. Furthermore, when the lungs are expanded to high volumes or compressed to low volumes, the limitations of the chest become even more extreme, and compliance can decrease to less than 20% of that of the lungs alone. Alveolar surface tension belongs to the lungs themselves, so it is already present when the lungs are inflated alone, and airway resistance is a separate fraction of the work of breathing (airway resistance work), not part of compliance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The thoracic cage has its own elastic and viscous resistance, so inflating lungs plus thorax takes almost twice the pressure of lungs alone: about 110 vs. 200 mL/cm H2O, and near the extremes of volume the combined compliance can fall below 20% of the lungs'. COMLEX: OMT for pulmonary patients targets this chest-wall component, improving rib and thoracic motion to reduce restrictions that add to the work of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 10 — functional residual capacity, where lung recoil and chest-wall recoil balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Mechanism of Pulmonary Ventilation and Role in Gas Exchange","batch":1,"stem":"A neonatologist is caring for a premature infant with respiratory distress syndrome. The infant's alveoli contain minimal surfactant. Quantitatively, approximately what is the surface tension of normal fluids lining the alveoli without surfactant, compared to the surface tension with normal amounts of surfactant present?","choices":{"A":"Pure water: 72 dynes/cm; without surfactant: 50 dynes/cm; with surfactant: 5-30 dynes/cm","B":"Pure water: 50 dynes/cm; without surfactant: 30 dynes/cm; with surfactant: 10-20 dynes/cm","C":"Pure water: 30 dynes/cm; without surfactant: 20 dynes/cm; with surfactant: 5-10 dynes/cm","D":"Pure water: 100 dynes/cm; without surfactant: 72 dynes/cm; with surfactant: 30-50 dynes/cm","E":"Pure water: 45 dynes/cm; without surfactant: 35 dynes/cm; with surfactant: 15-25 dynes/cm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The quantitative surface tension values are important for understanding the magnitude of surfactant's effect. Pure water has a surface tension of 72 dynes/cm. Normal fluids lining the alveoli but without surfactant have a surface tension of about 50 dynes/cm. With normal amounts of surfactant included, the surface tension ranges between 5 and 30 dynes/cm. This dramatic reduction in surface tension by surfactant is critical for normal lung function. At the lower end of the range, surfactant reduces surface tension to one-twelfth to one-half that of a pure water surface. This reduction is of great importance in reducing the work required by the respiratory muscles to expand the lungs and preventing alveolar collapse, especially in smaller alveoli where surface tension effects are magnified.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surface tension is about 72 dynes/cm for pure water, 50 dynes/cm for alveolar fluid without surfactant, and 5–30 dynes/cm with normal surfactant; dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine spreads over the surface to lower it. COMLEX: Surfactant comes from type II alveolar cells, so in a premature infant with too few of them, exogenous surfactant is given after birth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type II alveolar cells and their lamellar bodies","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with emphysema has both anatomical dead space of 150 mL and significant physiological dead space due to poorly perfused alveoli, bringing her total physiological dead space to 350 mL. Her tidal volume is 450 mL and respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute. Which statement best describes the impact on her systemic homeostasis?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar ventilation is 1600 mL/min; mild hypoxemia but normal CO₂ elimination","B":"Alveolar ventilation is 4800 mL/min; adequate gas exchange maintained","C":"Alveolar ventilation is 7200 mL/min; excessive CO₂ elimination causing alkalosis","D":"Alveolar ventilation is 3200 mL/min; normal homeostasis through compensatory tachypnea","E":"Alveolar ventilation is 1600 mL/min; severe impairment of gas exchange"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Alveolar ventilation equals respiratory rate times the difference between tidal volume and physiological dead space: V̇A = 16 × (450 - 350) = 16 × 100 = 1600 mL/min. This is severely reduced compared to normal alveolar ventilation of approximately 4200 mL/min. The large physiological dead space means that most of each breath does not participate in gas exchange because the alveoli are nonfunctional or only partially functional due to absent or poor blood flow through adjacent pulmonary capillaries. This severe reduction in alveolar ventilation profoundly impairs the ability to maintain normal arterial oxygen and carbon dioxide levels, disrupting systemic homeostasis. The patient would experience significant hypoxemia and hypercapnia, leading to respiratory acidosis and inadequate tissue oxygen delivery, which can affect cellular metabolism throughout the body.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveoli with absent or poor capillary blood flow count as dead space, so physiologic dead space exceeds anatomic dead space in diseased lungs and each breath delivers less fresh air to exchanging alveoli. COMLEX: In healthy lungs the two dead spaces are nearly equal; First Aid adds that the apex of a healthy lung is the largest contributor to alveolar dead space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — how emphysema leads to hypoxia and hypercapnia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is studying the work of breathing and divides it into three components. A patient with increased airway resistance due to bronchospasm would experience the greatest increase in which component of the work of inspiration?","choices":{"A":"Airway resistance work to overcome resistance to air movement","B":"Tissue resistance work to overcome viscosity of lung structures","C":"Compliance work required to expand lungs against elastic forces","D":"Surface tension work to overcome fluid-air interface forces","E":"Chest wall work to overcome thoracic cage rigidity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The work of inspiration can be divided into three fractions: compliance work or elastic work, which is required to expand the lungs against the lung and chest elastic forces; tissue resistance work, which is required to overcome the viscosity of the lung and chest wall structures; and airway resistance work, which is required to overcome airway resistance to movement of air into the lungs. In bronchospasm, the smooth muscle in the bronchioles contracts, narrowing the airways and dramatically increasing airway resistance. This requires substantially more work to move air through the constricted passages. While all three components exist during breathing, the airway resistance work would be disproportionately increased in this patient. During normal quiet breathing, airway resistance work is relatively small, but in conditions like asthma, bronchitis, or bronchospasm, this can become the dominant component and a major limitation on breathing capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Work of inspiration has three parts: compliance (elastic) work against lung and chest recoil, tissue resistance work against tissue viscosity, and airway resistance work against airflow; expiration at rest is passive. COMLEX: First Aid notes obstructive disease raises resistive work, so patients breathe slower and deeper, while restrictive disease raises elastic work, so they breathe faster and shallower.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 14 — local factors (histamine, slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) that constrict bronchioles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance of Pulmonary Ventilation in Systemic Homeostasis","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy person at rest has a tidal volume of 500 mL, dead space of 150 mL, and respiratory rate of 12 breaths per minute, giving a normal minute respiratory volume of approximately 6 L/min. If this person's respiratory rate increases to 40 breaths per minute but tidal volume can only increase to the vital capacity of 4600 mL, what would be the approximate maximum minute respiratory volume?","choices":{"A":"50 L/min","B":"100 L/min","C":"150 L/min","D":"184 L/min","E":"200 L/min"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The maximum minute respiratory volume equals the respiratory rate times the tidal volume. At maximum: 40 breaths/min × 4600 mL = 184,000 mL/min or 184 L/min. This represents approximately 30 times the normal minute respiratory volume of 6 L/min. This dramatic increase in minute respiratory volume is possible during maximal exercise and demonstrates the remarkable reserve capacity of the respiratory system. However, most people cannot sustain more than half to two-thirds of these maximum values for longer than 1 minute. This maximum ventilatory capacity is essential for maintaining systemic homeostasis during intense exercise when oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production can increase to about 20 times normal, requiring matching increases in ventilation to maintain normal arterial blood gas levels and prevent disruption of acid-base balance and tissue oxygen delivery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maximum minute volume is limited by how far rate and tidal volume can rise: rate up to 40–50/min and tidal volume up to the vital capacity (about 4600 mL in a young man) give more than 200 L/min, over 30 times the resting 6 L/min. COMLEX: Guyton notes a person can survive briefly on as little as 1.5 L/min at 2–4 breaths/min, the floor seen in severe respiratory depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 12 — minute respiratory volume and how it is calculated","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is teaching medical students about the muscles of respiration. Which set of muscles, when contracted, pulls the upper ribs forward and lifts upward on the sternum to increase the anteroposterior diameter of the chest during inspiration?","choices":{"A":"Internal intercostals and abdominal recti","B":"External intercostals and sternocleidomastoid muscles","C":"Diaphragm and internal intercostals","D":"Scaleni and abdominal obliques","E":"Posterior intercostals and transversus abdominis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most important muscles that raise the rib cage are the external intercostals. However, other muscles that help include the sternocleidomastoid muscles, which lift upward on the sternum; the anterior serrati, which lift many of the ribs; and the scaleni, which lift the first two ribs. During forced inspiration, particularly during exercise or respiratory distress, the sternocleidomastoid muscles become especially important as accessory muscles of inspiration. The external intercostals angle between the ribs in such a way that when they contract, they pull the upper ribs forward in relation to the lower ribs, causing leverage on the ribs to raise them upward, thereby increasing the anteroposterior diameter of the chest by about 20% greater during maximum inspiration than during expiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The external intercostals are the main rib elevators; the sternocleidomastoids lift the sternum, the anterior serrati lift many ribs and the scaleni lift the first two ribs, raising chest AP diameter by about 20% at maximal inspiration. COMLEX: Prominent accessory-muscle use means the primary respiratory muscles are failing to meet demand and may foreshadow respiratory failure, as in severe asthma or a COPD exacerbation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — status asthmaticus and its morphologic findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"During spirometry testing, a technician measures a patient's functional residual capacity using the helium dilution method. The patient has a measured FRC of 2300 mL. Using the standard equations relating pulmonary volumes and capacities, what is this patient's residual volume if their expiratory reserve volume is 1100 mL?","choices":{"A":"1200 mL","B":"1800 mL","C":"2300 mL","D":"3400 mL","E":"4600 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The functional residual capacity equals the expiratory reserve volume plus the residual volume: FRC = ERV + RV. Therefore, RV = FRC - ERV = 2300 - 1100 = 1200 mL. This is the volume of air remaining in the lungs at the end of normal expiration and is important because it represents the air that cannot be expired even with the most forceful expiration. The residual volume averages about 1200 mL in young adult men and about 1100 mL in young adult women. Understanding these relationships between pulmonary volumes and capacities is essential for interpreting pulmonary function tests. The FRC is particularly important clinically because changes in FRC can indicate various pulmonary diseases, and it represents the baseline volume from which tidal breathing occurs, affecting gas exchange efficiency. 2300 mL is the FRC itself, 3400 mL adds the ERV to the FRC instead of subtracting it, 4600 mL is double the FRC, and 1800 mL does not come out of FRC − ERV at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FRC = ERV + RV, so RV = FRC − ERV; Guyton's young-adult male values are FRC about 2300 mL, ERV about 1100 mL and RV about 1200 mL. COMLEX: Because RV cannot be exhaled into a spirometer, FRC must be measured indirectly (helium dilution) before RV and TLC can be calculated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 11 — spirometry limitations and the helium dilution method","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl6_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Anatomical Structures and Mechanics of Inspiration and Expiration","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is studying the pressure changes during the respiratory cycle. At the end of normal expiration, when no air is flowing and the respiratory muscles are relaxed, the alveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure. Which mechanism best explains why the lungs do not collapse completely at this point despite their elastic recoil?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant completely eliminates surface tension at end-expiration","B":"The diaphragm maintains slight contraction preventing collapse","C":"Residual volume prevents complete lung collapse","D":"Negative pleural pressure opposes elastic recoil of lungs","E":"Cartilage in airways provides structural support preventing collapse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lungs are held open at their resting level at the end of normal expiration by the balance between two opposing forces: the elastic recoil of the lungs tending to collapse them, and the negative pleural pressure pulling the lungs outward toward the chest wall. At the end of normal expiration, even though alveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure and no muscles are actively contracting, the pleural pressure remains about -5 cm H₂O. This negative pressure is necessary to keep the lungs inflated to the functional residual capacity level. The lung is suspended in the thoracic cavity by the hilum and is held to the chest wall by the negative pleural pressure, with pleural fluid providing lubrication. Without this continuous negative pleural pressure, the elastic forces would cause the lungs to collapse completely, as they do when air enters the pleural space in a pneumothorax.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At end-expiration with the muscles relaxed, alveolar pressure is 0 but pleural pressure stays about −5 cm H2O, the suction that just balances the lung's elastic recoil and holds it at its resting volume. COMLEX: If air enters the pleural space this suction is lost and the lung recoils; First Aid's exam table gives hyperresonance with absent breath sounds over a pneumothorax.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false}],"7":[{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q01_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist is measuring lung volumes in a healthy 25-year-old male at rest. The volume of air inspired or expired during each normal breath is measured. What is the typical tidal volume in a healthy adult?","choices":{"A":"500 mL","B":"1000 mL","C":"1500 mL","D":"2000 mL","E":"250 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tidal volume is the volume of air inspired or expired during each normal breath at rest. In a healthy adult, the typical tidal volume is approximately 500 mL. This volume represents the baseline ventilation needed to maintain normal gas exchange at rest and can increase significantly during exercise or increased metabolic demand. Understanding normal tidal volume is essential for interpreting pulmonary function tests and for setting appropriate parameters on mechanical ventilators. Tidal volume varies with body size, sex, and position, but 500 mL is the standard reference value for an average adult male. Volumes of 1000 to 2000 mL are far larger than a resting breath; in Guyton's normal values, amounts of that size belong to the reserve volumes (expiratory reserve volume about 1100 mL, residual volume about 1200 mL), not to tidal volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tidal volume is the air moved in or out with each normal breath, about 500 mL in a young adult man (Guyton) and about 400 mL in a woman; First Aid gives 6–8 mL/kg. COMLEX: Because First Aid expresses it as 6–8 mL/kg, a smaller patient's normal tidal volume is proportionally smaller than the textbook 500 mL.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — how pulmonary ventilation supports systemic homeostasis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q02_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is asked to inspire as deeply as possible beyond her normal tidal volume. The maximum additional volume of air that she can inspire is being measured. Which lung volume is being assessed?","choices":{"A":"Inspiratory reserve volume","B":"Tidal volume","C":"Expiratory reserve volume","D":"Residual volume","E":"Functional residual capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inspiratory reserve volume is the maximum additional volume of air that can be inspired beyond the normal tidal volume. This represents the extra ventilatory capacity available during increased metabolic demand or exercise. In healthy adults, the IRV is typically around 3000 mL in males and somewhat less in females. The ability to recruit this additional inspiratory capacity is important for maintaining adequate ventilation during physical activity or respiratory stress. Measurement of IRV helps assess respiratory muscle strength and chest wall compliance, and reductions in IRV can indicate restrictive lung disease or respiratory muscle weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inspiratory reserve volume is the extra air that can be inspired with full force above a normal tidal breath, about 3000 mL in men and 1900 mL in women; tidal volume plus IRV is the inspiratory capacity. COMLEX: Volumes vary with fitness, age, height, sex and altitude, and are about 20–30% lower in women than men, so results are read against matched predicted values.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — muscles of inspiration that generate the reserve inspiratory effort","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q03_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary function test measures the maximum volume of air that a patient can forcibly expire after completing a normal tidal expiration. The patient expires an additional 1100 mL beyond the normal tidal breath. What lung volume does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Tidal volume","B":"Inspiratory reserve volume","C":"Expiratory reserve volume","D":"Residual volume","E":"Vital capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Expiratory reserve volume is the maximum additional volume of air that can be expired by forceful expiration after the end of a normal tidal expiration. In healthy adults, ERV is typically around 1100 mL in males and approximately 700 mL in females. This volume represents the additional air that can be expelled from the lungs beyond normal quiet breathing. The ERV depends on the strength of the abdominal muscles and other expiratory muscles, as well as chest wall and lung compliance. Reductions in ERV can occur in conditions that increase functional residual capacity such as emphysema or air trapping, or in conditions that restrict lung expansion such as obesity or abdominal distension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Expiratory reserve volume is the maximum extra air expired by force after a normal tidal expiration, about 1100 mL in men and 700 mL in women; it sits between the end-tidal level (FRC) and the residual volume. COMLEX: Forced expiration below FRC uses the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals, the muscles that also power a cough.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — expiratory muscles that depress the rib cage in forced expiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q04_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man performs spirometry. His tidal volume is 500 mL and his inspiratory reserve volume is 3000 mL. What is his inspiratory capacity?","choices":{"A":"3500 mL","B":"4000 mL","C":"4500 mL","D":"2500 mL","E":"3000 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inspiratory capacity equals the tidal volume plus the inspiratory reserve volume. IC = TV + IRV = 500 mL + 3000 mL = 3500 mL. The inspiratory capacity represents the maximum amount of air a person can breathe in starting from the resting expiratory level. This measurement is clinically useful because it indicates the total amount of air available for inspiration and can be easily measured by spirometry. Reductions in inspiratory capacity can occur in both restrictive and obstructive lung diseases, and IC measurements are used to assess disease severity and response to treatment. The IC is an important component of total lung capacity and provides information about inspiratory muscle function and chest wall mechanics. 2500 mL subtracts the tidal volume instead of adding it, and 4000 to 4500 mL would need the expiratory reserve volume added as well, which turns the sum into vital capacity rather than inspiratory capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inspiratory capacity = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume, about 3500 mL in a young man; it is the most a person can inhale starting from the normal end-expiratory level. COMLEX: Because TLC = FRC + IC, the spirometric IC is the piece added to a helium-dilution FRC to obtain total lung capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive vs. restrictive patterns on the flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q05_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist explains that functional residual capacity is the volume of air remaining in the lungs at the end of normal passive expiration. FRC is composed of which two lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume","B":"Tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume","C":"Inspiratory reserve volume and residual volume","D":"Residual volume and vital capacity","E":"Expiratory reserve volume and residual volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Functional residual capacity is the volume of air that remains in the lungs at the end of normal expiration and equals the expiratory reserve volume plus the residual volume. FRC = ERV + RV. In healthy adults, FRC is approximately 2300 mL. The FRC represents the resting volume of the lung-chest wall system where the inward elastic recoil of the lungs is exactly balanced by the outward elastic recoil of the chest wall. FRC is an important buffer that prevents wide fluctuations in blood gas concentrations between breaths and provides a reservoir of air for continuous gas exchange. FRC cannot be measured by simple spirometry and requires techniques such as helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Functional residual capacity = ERV + RV (about 2300 mL), the air left after a normal expiration; First Aid describes it as the volume where the chest wall's outward pull balances the lung's inward recoil. COMLEX: At FRC pulmonary vascular resistance is at its minimum, and obstructive disease raises FRC through air trapping (First Aid).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — pressures (pleural, alveolar, transpulmonary) that set the resting lung volume","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q06_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has the following pulmonary function test results: tidal volume = 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume = 2500 mL, and expiratory reserve volume = 1000 mL. What is her vital capacity?","choices":{"A":"3000 mL","B":"3500 mL","C":"4000 mL","D":"4500 mL","E":"5000 mL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vital capacity is the maximum amount of air a person can expel from the lungs after first filling the lungs to their maximum extent and then expiring to the maximum extent. VC equals the sum of inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume, and expiratory reserve volume. VC = IRV + TV + ERV = 2500 mL + 500 mL + 1000 mL = 4000 mL. Vital capacity is one of the most important measurements in pulmonary function testing because it provides a simple assessment of overall lung function. VC is reduced in both restrictive lung diseases and severe obstructive diseases. It varies with body size, sex, age, and physical condition. Normal VC in adult males is approximately 4600 mL and in adult females approximately 3100 mL. Serial measurements of VC are useful for monitoring disease progression and response to therapy. 3000 mL is only IRV + TV (the inspiratory capacity), 3500 mL is IRV + ERV with the tidal volume left out, and 4500 and 5000 mL are more than the three given volumes add up to.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vital capacity = IRV + tidal volume + ERV (= IC + ERV), the most air that can be expelled after a maximal inspiration, about 4600 mL in a young man and 3100 mL in a woman. COMLEX: In restrictive disease First Aid notes FVC and TLC fall, with a normal or raised FEV1/FVC ratio.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC and the FEV1/FVC ratio in airway obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q07_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is explaining why standard spirometry alone cannot measure functional residual capacity or residual volume. What is the fundamental limitation of spirometry that prevents direct measurement of these volumes?","choices":{"A":"The spirometer cannot detect airflow at very low velocities","B":"Residual volume and FRC require maximal expiratory effort that exceeds spirometer capacity","C":"Spirometry can only measure volumes that can be voluntarily moved in or out of the lungs","D":"The spirometer tubing adds too much dead space for accurate measurements","E":"Spirometry requires the patient to breathe pure oxygen which alters FRC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Standard spirometry measures volumes based on the movement of air into and out of the lungs through voluntary breathing maneuvers. However, residual volume is the air that remains in the lungs even after maximal expiration and cannot be expelled voluntarily. Since this air never leaves the lungs, it cannot pass through the spirometer to be measured. Similarly, functional residual capacity includes residual volume and therefore cannot be directly measured by spirometry alone. This is why supplementary techniques such as the helium dilution method, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography are necessary. These techniques can determine the total volume of gas in the lungs, including the volume that cannot be voluntarily expired, allowing calculation of RV, FRC, and subsequently total lung capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A spirometer records only air moved in and out of the mouth, so residual volume, and every capacity containing it (FRC, TLC), cannot be measured by spirometry; RV is about half of FRC. COMLEX: First Aid's flow-volume table shows why this matters: obstructive disease raises RV, FRC and TLC, and these changes need an indirect method to detect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive vs. restrictive disease on the flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q08_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"A patient performs a helium dilution test to measure functional residual capacity. The test involves breathing a known concentration of helium in a closed system until equilibration occurs. Which principle allows helium dilution to measure FRC when spirometry cannot?","choices":{"A":"Helium is lighter than nitrogen and diffuses more rapidly into all lung spaces","B":"Helium equilibrates throughout all gas in the lungs including residual volume","C":"Helium causes bronchodilation allowing access to previously closed airways","D":"Helium directly binds to alveolar walls allowing volumetric calculation","E":"Helium dissolves in blood and can be measured in pulmonary capillaries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The helium dilution method works on the principle of gas dilution and equilibration. The patient breathes from a spirometer containing a known concentration of helium in a closed system. Helium is an inert gas that is not significantly absorbed by the blood or tissues. As the patient breathes, helium equilibrates throughout all the gas in the lungs, including the residual volume that cannot be expelled and measured by standard spirometry. By measuring the dilution of helium concentration after equilibration, the total lung volume including FRC can be calculated using the principle that the initial concentration × initial volume = final concentration × final volume. This technique allows measurement of RV and FRC, which can then be used to calculate total lung capacity by adding inspiratory capacity measured by spirometry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In helium dilution the subject breathes from a spirometer with a known helium concentration starting at end-expiration; the gas in the lungs (the FRC) dilutes the helium, and FRC = (initial/final He concentration − 1) × initial spirometer volume. COMLEX: Once FRC is known, RV = FRC − ERV and TLC = FRC + IC.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — mechanism of ventilation that mixes inspired gas with resident lung gas","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q09_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician orders a helium dilution study to determine total lung capacity in a patient with suspected emphysema. After measuring FRC by helium dilution, which additional spirometric measurement must be obtained to calculate TLC?","choices":{"A":"Tidal volume","B":"Inspiratory capacity","C":"Expiratory reserve volume","D":"Vital capacity","E":"Residual volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Total lung capacity is the maximum volume of air the lungs can contain and equals functional residual capacity plus inspiratory capacity. TLC = FRC + IC. The helium dilution method measures FRC, which includes the residual volume that cannot be measured by spirometry alone. However, inspiratory capacity can be easily and directly measured by spirometry as the maximal inhalation from resting end-expiration. By adding the helium-measured FRC to the spirometry-measured IC, the total lung capacity can be calculated. This combined approach is necessary because spirometry alone cannot measure volumes that include residual volume such as FRC and TLC, while the helium dilution method measures the total gas volume in the lungs at functional residual capacity. Understanding these relationships is essential for proper interpretation of pulmonary function tests and for distinguishing restrictive from obstructive lung diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Helium dilution yields FRC, so total lung capacity is FRC plus the spirometric inspiratory capacity (TLC = FRC + IC), and residual volume is FRC minus ERV. COMLEX: In emphysema First Aid lists increased TLC, FRC and RV from air trapping, which is why the measurement is ordered when emphysema is suspected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema and their pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q10_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy adult at rest has a tidal volume of 500 mL and a respiratory rate of 12 breaths per minute. What is the minute respiratory volume?","choices":{"A":"3000 mL/min","B":"4000 mL/min","C":"5000 mL/min","D":"7000 mL/min","E":"6000 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Minute respiratory volume, also called minute ventilation, is the total amount of air moved into and out of the lungs per minute. It is calculated by multiplying tidal volume by respiratory rate. Minute ventilation = TV × Respiratory Rate = 500 mL × 12 breaths/min = 6000 mL/min or 6 L/min. This is the normal minute ventilation for an adult at rest. Minute ventilation must be adequate to meet the metabolic demands of the body and maintain normal arterial blood gas concentrations. During exercise, minute ventilation can increase dramatically to 100 L/min or more by increasing both tidal volume and respiratory rate. Measurement of minute ventilation is important in assessing overall respiratory function and is essential for setting ventilator parameters in mechanically ventilated patients. 3000 to 5000 mL/min would need fewer breaths or a smaller tidal volume than given, and 7000 mL/min would need more; Guyton gives about 6 L/min as the normal resting value for 500 mL at 12 breaths/min.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minute respiratory volume = tidal volume × respiratory rate, about 500 mL × 12/min = 6 L/min at rest. COMLEX: First Aid gives a normal respiratory rate of 12–20 breaths/min, so a resting rate well outside this range is an early vital-sign clue to respiratory disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — alveolar ventilation and the effect of dead space on homeostasis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q11_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A patient performing vigorous exercise increases her tidal volume from 500 mL to 2000 mL and her respiratory rate from 12 breaths per minute to 30 breaths per minute. What is her minute respiratory volume during exercise?","choices":{"A":"15,000 mL/min","B":"30,000 mL/min","C":"45,000 mL/min","D":"75,000 mL/min","E":"60,000 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During exercise, minute respiratory volume increases dramatically to meet increased oxygen demand and carbon dioxide elimination. Minute ventilation = TV × Respiratory Rate = 2000 mL × 30 breaths/min = 60,000 mL/min or 60 L/min. This represents a 10-fold increase from resting minute ventilation of approximately 6 L/min. The increase in minute ventilation during exercise is achieved through increases in both tidal volume and respiratory rate. In the average young man, pulmonary ventilation at maximal exercise is about 100–110 L/min, against a maximal breathing capacity of 150–170 L/min (Guyton Ch 85). The ability to increase minute ventilation is limited by maximal tidal volume, which is constrained by vital capacity, and by the maximal respiratory rate that can be sustained. Patients with lung disease may have impaired ability to increase minute ventilation during exercise, leading to dyspnea and exercise limitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In exercise both tidal volume and rate rise; tidal volume can approach the vital capacity and rate can reach 40–50/min, so minute volume can exceed 200 L/min, more than 30 times resting. COMLEX: First Aid notes that with exercise the rise in cardiac output dilates apical capillaries, bringing apical V/Q toward 1 as ventilation climbs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 11 — interplay of chemical and nervous factors controlling ventilation in exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q12_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist is monitoring a patient on mechanical ventilation. The ventilator settings show a tidal volume of 450 mL and a respiratory rate of 16 breaths per minute. However, the patient is also taking spontaneous breaths at a rate of 4 additional breaths per minute with an average volume of 300 mL per breath. What is the patient's total minute respiratory volume?","choices":{"A":"6000 mL/min","B":"7200 mL/min","C":"9600 mL/min","D":"8400 mL/min","E":"10,800 mL/min"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Total minute respiratory volume includes both mandatory ventilator breaths and spontaneous patient breaths. Ventilator minute ventilation = 450 mL × 16 breaths/min = 7200 mL/min. Spontaneous minute ventilation = 300 mL × 4 breaths/min = 1200 mL/min. Total minute ventilation = 7200 + 1200 = 8400 mL/min. In patients on mechanical ventilation, it is important to calculate total minute ventilation including both machine-delivered and spontaneous breaths to ensure adequate ventilation and appropriate CO2 elimination. This calculation is particularly important in ventilator modes that allow spontaneous breathing such as SIMV or pressure support ventilation. Monitoring total minute ventilation helps guide weaning from mechanical ventilation and assesses the patient's work of breathing. 6000 mL/min falls short of that sum and 9600 mL/min exceeds it, so neither matches 7200 mL/min of ventilator breaths plus 1200 mL/min of spontaneous breaths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Total minute volume is the sum of every breath's volume over a minute, so mandatory ventilator breaths and spontaneous breaths are added separately (volume × rate for each). COMLEX: Spontaneous breaths with small tidal volumes add little alveolar ventilation, because dead space (about 150 mL) is subtracted from every breath.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — alveolar ventilation, VA = rate × (VT − VD)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q13_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"During an asthma attack, a patient receives an injection of epinephrine, which has strong sympathetic effects. Epinephrine primarily acts on beta-adrenergic receptors in the bronchioles. What is the expected effect on bronchiole diameter?","choices":{"A":"Marked constriction due to smooth muscle contraction","B":"No change because bronchioles lack smooth muscle","C":"Initial constriction followed by delayed dilation","D":"Alternating constriction and dilation causing bronchospasm","E":"Dilation due to smooth muscle relaxation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic stimulation via norepinephrine and epinephrine released by sympathetic nerve stimulation or from the adrenal medullae causes bronchiole dilation. These hormones, especially epinephrine because of its greater stimulation of beta-adrenergic receptors, cause dilation of the bronchial tree. This bronchodilation occurs because sympathetic stimulation relaxes the smooth muscle in the bronchiolar walls. The bronchodilating effect is particularly important during exercise or stress when increased airflow is needed to meet metabolic demands. Beta-2 agonist medications such as albuterol mimic this sympathetic effect and are used therapeutically to treat bronchoconstriction in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease by stimulating beta-adrenergic receptors and causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation and airway dilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epinephrine and norepinephrine released into the blood by the adrenal medullae dilate the bronchial tree, epinephrine more so because of its greater β-adrenergic stimulation; direct sympathetic innervation of the bronchioles is weak. COMLEX: Inhaled short-acting β2-agonists (albuterol) relax bronchial smooth muscle for acute symptoms; First Aid lists tremor and arrhythmia as adverse effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — drug classes used for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q14_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"A patient inhales irritating smoke, triggering a parasympathetic reflex. Parasympathetic nerve fibers from the vagus nerves penetrate the lung parenchyma and secrete which neurotransmitter that causes bronchiole constriction?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Epinephrine","C":"Dopamine","D":"Acetylcholine","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic nerve fibers derived from the vagus nerves penetrate the lung parenchyma and secrete acetylcholine. When activated, acetylcholine causes mild to moderate constriction of the bronchioles. This parasympathetic constrictor reflex often occurs when the respiratory passages are irritated by foreign matter, dust, cigarette smoke, or bronchial infection. The parasympathetic system provides a protective mechanism by constricting airways when noxious stimuli are detected, helping to limit exposure of the deeper lung tissues to harmful substances. However, excessive parasympathetic activation can contribute to pathological bronchoconstriction. Anticholinergic medications such as ipratropium bromide block acetylcholine receptors and are used therapeutically to prevent parasympathetic-mediated bronchoconstriction in conditions such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and asthma. Norepinephrine and epinephrine are the sympathetic side and dilate the bronchioles (mostly as adrenal hormones in the blood, since few sympathetic fibers reach the central lung), and dopamine and serotonin are not the transmitter released by vagal fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A few vagal parasympathetic fibers enter the lung and release acetylcholine, causing mild to moderate bronchiolar constriction; they can be triggered reflexly by noxious gases, dust, smoke or infection irritating the airway epithelium. COMLEX: Muscarinic antagonists (ipratropium, tiotropium) block this pathway and are used in asthma and COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — adverse effects and safety of the major asthma/COPD drug classes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q15_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that direct control of bronchioles by sympathetic nerve fibers is relatively weak compared to systemic sympathetic effects. Why is direct sympathetic neural control of bronchioles limited?","choices":{"A":"Bronchioles completely lack beta-adrenergic receptors","B":"Few sympathetic nerve fibers penetrate to the deeper portions of the lung","C":"The vagus nerve blocks sympathetic nerve transmission to bronchioles","D":"Bronchiolar smooth muscle is unresponsive to catecholamines","E":"Sympathetic ganglia are too far from the lungs for effective neurotransmission"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Direct control of the bronchioles by sympathetic nerve fibers is relatively weak because few of these fibers penetrate to the deeper portions of the lung. However, the bronchial tree is very much exposed to norepinephrine and epinephrine released into the blood by sympathetic stimulation of the adrenal gland medullae. Both these hormones, especially epinephrine because of its greater stimulation of beta-adrenergic receptors, cause dilation of the bronchial tree. This means that sympathetic effects on the bronchioles are mediated more by circulating catecholamines from the adrenal medulla than by direct neural innervation. This hormonal mechanism allows for widespread bronchodilation during stress or exercise when sympathoadrenal activation occurs, even though direct sympathetic innervation of bronchioles is sparse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Direct sympathetic control of the bronchioles is weak because few sympathetic fibers penetrate to the central portions of the lung; the airways respond instead to circulating epinephrine and norepinephrine from the adrenal medulla. COMLEX: The OMM course targets sympathetic tone through the chain ganglia beside the rib heads; rib raising under 30 seconds is traditionally stimulatory and over 90 seconds inhibitory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — therapeutic rationale for β2-agonists in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q16_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with allergic asthma inhales pollen, triggering an allergic reaction. Mast cells in the lungs release histamine and slow reactive substance of anaphylaxis. What effect do these substances have on the airways?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilation and increased airflow","B":"Bronchoconstriction and narrowing of airways","C":"No effect because airways lack receptors for these mediators","D":"Initial bronchodilation followed by delayed constriction","E":"Stabilization of airway diameter preventing changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Several substances formed in the lungs are often active in causing bronchiolar constriction. Two of the most important of these are histamine and slow reactive substance of anaphylaxis. Both these substances are released in the lung tissues by mast cells during allergic reactions, especially those caused by pollen in the air. These play key roles in causing airway obstruction in allergic asthma, especially those caused by pollen. Histamine causes constriction of bronchiolar smooth muscle, and slow reactive substance of anaphylaxis, which is a mixture of leukotrienes, is even more potent. The release of these local secretory factors during allergic reactions explains the acute bronchoconstriction seen in allergic asthma attacks. Antihistamines and leukotriene modifiers are used therapeutically to block the effects of these mediators and prevent or treat allergic broncho constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mast cells release histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis in allergic reactions, both of which constrict bronchioles; Guyton singles out slow-reacting substance as especially important in allergic asthma. COMLEX: First Aid frames allergic asthma as a type I hypersensitivity reaction with reversible bronchoconstriction, Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals in the sputum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast cell immunologic process that drives an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q17_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is studying the same irritants that cause parasympathetic constrictor reflexes in the airways. These irritants also act directly on lung tissues to initiate local reactions that cause bronchiolar constriction. Which statement best describes this dual mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Irritants trigger both parasympathetic reflexes and direct local non-nervous tissue reactions","B":"Irritants cause only neural reflexes with no direct tissue effects","C":"Irritants cause only direct tissue effects with no neural component","D":"The direct tissue effects occur only after neural reflexes are exhausted","E":"Neural and local effects always oppose each other causing no net change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The same irritants that cause parasympathetic constrictor reflexes of the airways, such as smoke, dust, cigarette smoke, and so on, also act directly on the lung tissues to initiate local, non-nervous reactions that cause obstructive constriction of the airways. This dual mechanism provides both rapid neural responses and sustained local inflammatory responses to noxious stimuli. The parasympathetic reflex provides immediate protection by causing reflex bronchoconstriction when irritants are detected, while the direct local tissue reactions involve release of inflammatory mediators such as histamine and leukotrienes that can cause more prolonged bronchoconstriction. This explains why airway obstruction in conditions such as asthma and chronic bronchitis can be both rapid in onset due to neural reflexes and persistent due to ongoing local inflammatory responses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The irritants that trigger vagal constrictor reflexes (smoke, dust, sulfur dioxide, acidic smog components) also act directly on lung tissue to start local, non-nervous reactions that constrict the airways. COMLEX: First Aid lists allergens, viral URIs and stress as common asthma triggers, and a methacholine challenge can support the diagnosis by provoking this hyperresponsive constriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — etiology and pathogenesis of the different asthma phenotypes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q18_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with pollen allergy develops acute bronchospasm. The physician explains that mast cells have released substances that cause the bronchioles to constrict. Mast cells release several mediators during allergic reactions, but two are most important for causing airway obstruction. Which two mediators are these?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine and norepinephrine","B":"Epinephrine and dopamine","C":"Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide","D":"Histamine and leukotrienes (SRS-A)","E":"Serotonin and substance P"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two of the most important local secretory factors causing bronchiolar constriction are histamine and slow reactive substance of anaphylaxis. Both these substances are released in the lung tissues by mast cells during allergic reactions, especially those caused by pollen in the air. Histamine causes rapid constriction of bronchiolar smooth muscle. Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) is a mixture of leukotrienes that is even more potent and causes sustained bronchoconstriction. These substances play key roles in causing airway obstruction in allergic asthma. Pharmacological treatments for allergic asthma include antihistamines to block histamine receptors and leukotriene modifiers such as montelukast to block the effects of slow reactive substance of anaphylaxis. Understanding these mediators is essential for appropriate treatment of allergic airway diseases. Acetylcholine and norepinephrine are nerve transmitters, not mast cell mediators, and norepinephrine and epinephrine dilate bronchioles; epinephrine and dopamine are not mast cell products; nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide relax smooth muscle rather than constrict it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (the leukotrienes) are the two main local mediators of bronchiolar constriction released by mast cells in allergy. COMLEX: First Aid's drug targets follow from this: montelukast and zafirlukast block CysLT1 leukotriene receptors (useful in aspirin- and exercise-induced asthma), and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — antileukotrienes among the asthma drug classes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q19_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient aspirates a small food particle that irritates the larynx, triggering the cough reflex. After up to 2.5 liters of air are rapidly inspired, the epiglottis and vocal cords close tightly to entrap the air. The abdominal muscles then contract forcefully. What pressure can develop in the lungs during this phase of the cough?","choices":{"A":"Roughly 10 to 15 mm Hg","B":"About 25 mm Hg","C":"About 50 mm Hg","D":"100 mm Hg or more","E":"Less than 5 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During the cough reflex, after air is entrapped by closure of the epiglottis and vocal cords, the abdominal muscles contract forcefully, pushing against the diaphragm while other expiratory muscles such as the internal intercostals also contract forcefully. This causes the pressure in the lungs to rise rapidly to as much as 100 mm Hg or more. This high intrapulmonary pressure is essential for the explosive nature of the cough. The vocal cords and epiglottis then suddenly open widely, causing an explosive outward flow of air. The rapidly moving air usually carries with it any foreign matter that is present in the bronchi or trachea. Importantly, the strong compression of the lungs collapses the bronchi and trachea by causing their noncartilaginous parts to invaginate inward, so the exploding air actually passes through bronchial and tracheal slits. The rapidly moving air usually carries with it foreign matter present in the airways, making the cough reflex an important protective mechanism. Pressures of 10 to 50 mm Hg fall far short of the 100 mm Hg or more that Guyton describes for this phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In a cough, up to 2.5 L is inspired, the epiglottis and vocal cords close, and forceful contraction of abdominal muscles and internal intercostals raises lung pressure to 100 mm Hg or more. COMLEX: First Aid lists altered mental status with a weakened cough or poor glottic closure as a risk factor for aspiration pneumonia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — host defenses against pulmonary infection, including the cough reflex","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q20_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains the sequence of events in the cough reflex to a medical student. After the initial inspiration and building of high pressure with closed glottis, what happens that creates the explosive expulsion of air?","choices":{"A":"The diaphragm suddenly contracts causing forced expiration","B":"The vocal cords and epiglottis suddenly open widely","C":"The bronchioles dilate allowing increased airflow","D":"The alveoli actively contract expelling their air","E":"Negative pleural pressure suddenly becomes positive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After pressure builds up in the lungs to 100 mm Hg or more with the glottis closed, the vocal cords and epiglottis suddenly open widely, so that air under this high pressure in the lungs explodes outward. Sometimes this air is expelled at velocities ranging from 75 to 100 miles per hour. Importantly, the strong compression of the lungs collapses the bronchi and trachea by causing their noncartilaginous parts to invaginate inward, so the exploding air actually passes through bronchial and tracheal slits. The rapidly moving air usually carries with it any foreign matter that is present in the bronchi or trachea. The explosive nature of the cough is essential for its protective function in clearing the airways of foreign material, excess mucus, and debris, making it one of the most important respiratory defense mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After pressure builds behind the closed glottis, the vocal cords and epiglottis open suddenly and air explodes out, sometimes at 75–100 miles/hr; the compressed bronchi and trachea invaginate into slits that speed the airflow. COMLEX: The cough's afferent limb runs mainly in the vagus nerves to the medulla, and the larynx and carina are the most sensitive trigger sites.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — opioid antitussives that suppress the cough reflex","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q21_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has an irritant in the nasal passages triggering the sneeze reflex. The sneeze reflex is similar to the cough reflex, but differs in the triggering stimulus and the pathway of air expulsion. How does the sneeze reflex differ from the cough reflex?","choices":{"A":"The sneeze reflex is triggered by irritation in nasal passages and air passes rapidly through the nose","B":"The sneeze reflex produces lower pressures than the cough reflex","C":"The sneeze reflex involves only inspiration without forceful expiration","D":"The sneeze reflex keeps the glottis open throughout the entire reflex","E":"The sneeze reflex is voluntary while the cough reflex is involuntary"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The sneeze reflex is very much like the cough reflex, except that it applies to the nasal passageways instead of the lower respiratory passages. The initiating stimulus of the sneeze reflex is irritation in the nasal passageways rather than the larynx, trachea, or bronchi. The afferent impulses pass in the fifth cranial nerve to the medulla, where the reflex is triggered. A series of reactions similar to those for the cough reflex takes place, but with one difference: the uvula is depressed, so that large amounts of air pass rapidly through the nose as well as the mouth, thus helping clear the nasal passages of foreign matter. The sneeze reflex is an important protective mechanism for the upper respiratory tract, just as the cough reflex protects the lower respiratory tract.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The sneeze reflex works like a cough but starts with irritation in the nasal passages, its afferents run in cranial nerve V to the medulla, and the uvula is depressed so the blast of air leaves through the nose. COMLEX: The OMM course targets the trigeminal system and suboccipital region when treating upper respiratory infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — innate and mucociliary defenses of the respiratory tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q22_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes pulmonary function testing. During quiet breathing at rest, she inhales and exhales 450 mL of air with each breath. After a normal expiration, she is asked to exhale as forcefully as possible and expels an additional 1,000 mL of air. Which of the following best describes the relationship between these measured volumes?","choices":{"A":"The first measurement represents functional residual capacity, and the second represents residual volume","B":"The first measurement represents tidal volume, and the second represents expiratory reserve volume","C":"The first measurement represents inspiratory reserve volume, and the second represents vital capacity","D":"The first measurement represents vital capacity, and the second represents total lung capacity","E":"The first measurement represents expiratory reserve volume, and the second represents tidal volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tidal volume is the volume of air moved during normal quiet breathing, typically around 500 mL at rest. The patient's 450 mL measurement during quiet breathing represents tidal volume. Expiratory reserve volume is the additional volume that can be forcefully expired after a normal tidal expiration, typically around 1,100 mL. The 1,000 mL expelled after normal expiration represents expiratory reserve volume. FRC then RV is incorrect because FRC and RV are capacities/volumes that cannot be measured by simple spirometry. IRV then VC is incorrect because IRV is the volume that can be inspired beyond normal tidal inspiration. VC then TLC and ERV then tidal volume incorrectly identify the measurements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tidal volume is the air of a quiet breath (about 500 mL); expiratory reserve volume is the extra air forced out after a normal expiration (about 1100 mL in men, 700 mL in women). COMLEX: A forced expiration below FRC recruits the abdominal muscles, so patients with weak expiratory muscles have a small ERV and a weak cough.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — anatomy and mechanics of inspiration and expiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q23_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy, nonsmoking 23-year-old woman of average build has pulmonary function testing before joining a research study. Her results are tidal volume 400 mL, inspiratory reserve volume 1,900 mL, expiratory reserve volume 700 mL, and residual volume 1,100 mL. The technician flags every value as low because the laboratory's posted reference card lists 500, 3,000, 1,100, and 1,200 mL. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Restrictive disease shrinking all four volumes","B":"Air trapping from early obstructive disease","C":"Submaximal effort during the forced maneuvers","D":"Normal results for a woman of her age and size","E":"Respiratory muscle weakness limiting the reserves"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton's table of average volumes gives young adult men tidal volume 500, inspiratory reserve 3,000, expiratory reserve 1,100, and residual volume 1,200 mL, and young adult women 400, 1,900, 700, and 1,100 mL, which are exactly her values; most volumes and capacities run about 20% to 30% lower in women than in men, and they also vary with size, fitness, age, and altitude. The reference card is a male average, so her results are normal for her. Restrictive disease is not supported in a healthy woman whose values match the female averages. Air trapping from obstruction would raise residual volume, not leave it at the female average. Submaximal effort and respiratory muscle weakness would depress the effort-dependent reserves selectively, not reproduce the published female averages across all four volumes, including the residual volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's average volumes differ by sex: young men about 500/3000/1100/1200 mL and young women about 400/1900/700/1100 mL (tidal, IRV, ERV, RV), and most volumes run 20–30% lower in women. COMLEX: First Aid's obstructive and restrictive criteria use percent of predicted (FEV1 and FVC > 80% predicted normal), so values must be compared with sex- and size-matched references.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC and FEV1/FVC as obstruction worsens","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q24_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Lung volumes — TV, IRV, ERV, RV (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has difficulty exhaling air from his lungs. After performing a maximal forced expiration during pulmonary function testing, a significant volume of air remains in his lungs that cannot be expelled. This remaining volume is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Expiratory reserve volume","B":"Functional residual capacity","C":"Residual volume","D":"Tidal volume","E":"Inspiratory capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Residual volume is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after maximal forced expiration, typically around 1,200 mL. This air remains in the lungs and cannot be expelled voluntarily. RV is increased in obstructive diseases like COPD due to air trapping. Expiratory reserve volume is incorrect because ERV is the volume that CAN be expired beyond normal expiration. Functional residual capacity is a capacity that includes RV plus ERV. Tidal volume and inspiratory capacity refer to other lung volumes/capacities that do not describe the air remaining after maximal expiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Residual volume is the air left after the most forceful expiration, about 1200 mL in a young man; it cannot be measured by spirometry. COMLEX: In obstructive disease, premature airway closure at high lung volumes traps air, so First Aid lists RV, FRC and TLC all increased.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — clinical features of the major obstructive pulmonary diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q25_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old athlete undergoes pulmonary function testing. Her measurements show a tidal volume of 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume of 3,200 mL, expiratory reserve volume of 1,100 mL, and residual volume of 1,200 mL. What is her inspiratory capacity?","choices":{"A":"1,600 mL","B":"2,300 mL","C":"3,700 mL","D":"4,800 mL","E":"6,000 mL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inspiratory capacity is calculated as IC = TV + IRV. In this patient: IC = 500 mL + 3,200 mL = 3,700 mL. This represents the maximum volume of air that can be inspired starting from the resting expiratory level. 1,600 mL would be FRC minus VC, which is not a meaningful calculation. 2,300 mL represents only FRC calculation. 4,800 mL represents vital capacity. 6,000 mL represents total lung capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inspiratory capacity = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume; residual volume and ERV are not part of it. COMLEX: Athletes and larger people have larger lung volumes than small or asthenic people, so a big IC is not by itself abnormal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — exercise and the body's rising O2 demand","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q26_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary physiology instructor writes the four lung capacities on the board and asks the class to identify the one pair of measurements whose sum equals total lung capacity. Which pair is correct?","choices":{"A":"Inspiratory capacity plus tidal volume","B":"Vital capacity plus the residual volume","C":"Functional residual capacity plus ERV","D":"Inspiratory capacity plus residual volume","E":"Vital capacity plus expiratory reserve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton defines total lung capacity (about 5,800 mL) as the maximum volume to which the lungs can be expanded, equal to the vital capacity plus the residual volume. Vital capacity (inspiratory reserve volume plus tidal volume plus expiratory reserve volume) covers everything that can be moved, and residual volume is the air that stays behind after the most forceful expiration. Inspiratory capacity plus tidal volume counts the tidal volume twice and omits both expiratory reserve and residual volume. Functional residual capacity plus expiratory reserve volume counts the expiratory reserve twice and omits the inspiratory capacity. Inspiratory capacity plus residual volume leaves out the expiratory reserve volume. Vital capacity plus expiratory reserve counts the expiratory reserve twice and leaves out the residual volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Total lung capacity (about 5800 mL) is the most the lungs can hold; TLC = VC + RV, and equivalently TLC = IC + FRC. COMLEX: Because RV is part of TLC, TLC is reported from helium dilution or another indirect method, not from spirometry alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — clinical analysis of restrictive disease, defined by a reduced TLC","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q27_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Lung capacities — IC, FRC, VC, TLC (definitions and calculations)","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has the following pulmonary function test results: tidal volume 450 mL, inspiratory reserve volume 2,700 mL, expiratory reserve volume 1,000 mL, and residual volume 1,100 mL. What is her total lung capacity?","choices":{"A":"3,150 mL","B":"4,150 mL","C":"4,600 mL","D":"5,250 mL","E":"6,000 mL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Total lung capacity is the sum of all four lung volumes: TLC = TV + IRV + ERV + RV. In this patient: TLC = 450 + 2,700 + 1,000 + 1,100 = 5,250 mL. This represents the maximum volume of air the lungs can hold. Alternatively, TLC can be calculated as IC + FRC or VC + RV. 4,150 mL represents vital capacity only. 3,150 mL represents IC only. 4,600 mL and 6,000 mL represent incorrect calculations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Total lung capacity is the sum of all four volumes (TV + IRV + ERV + RV). COMLEX: First Aid notes restrictive lung disease lowers TLC and FVC, while obstructive disease raises TLC through air trapping.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — major restrictive lung diseases that reduce lung volumes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q28_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"A technician performing a helium dilution study is distracted and connects the patient to the helium-filled spirometer at the end of a maximal inspiration instead of at the end of a normal expiration. The rest of the procedure and the dilution formula are applied correctly. Which volume does the calculated result now represent?","choices":{"A":"Total lung capacity","B":"Functional residual capacity","C":"Residual volume","D":"Vital capacity","E":"Inspiratory capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Guyton's helium dilution method the person expires normally and then immediately begins breathing from the spirometer, so the gas already in the lungs at that moment, the functional residual capacity, is what dilutes the helium. The formula measures whatever volume of gas is in the lungs at the moment of connection. Connecting at the end of a maximal inspiration means the helium is diluted by the lungs at maximum expansion, which is total lung capacity. Functional residual capacity is what the test returns only when the connection is made at the end of a normal expiration. Residual volume would require connection after a maximal expiration, and in the standard test it is derived as functional residual capacity minus expiratory reserve volume. Vital capacity and inspiratory capacity are volumes that are breathed in or out and are measured by ordinary spirometry, not by gas dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Helium dilution measures whatever gas is in the lungs at the moment the subject connects to the spirometer; connecting at end of normal expiration gives FRC, so connecting at full inspiration would measure TLC. COMLEX: Correct technique matters clinically, because FRC and RV change markedly in lung disease and are the values the test is ordered to find.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — mechanism of pulmonary ventilation and lung volume change","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q29_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton explains that the spirometer cannot measure functional residual capacity directly because part of it can never be expired into the device. In a healthy young adult, approximately what fraction of the functional residual capacity does this unexpirable volume make up?","choices":{"A":"About one-tenth","B":"About one-quarter","C":"About one-half","D":"About three-quarters","E":"Essentially all of it"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that the air in the residual volume cannot be expired into the spirometer and that this volume constitutes about half of the functional residual capacity; with average young adult male values, residual volume is about 1,200 mL of an FRC of about 2,300 mL. That is why FRC must be measured indirectly by helium dilution and residual volume is then obtained by subtracting expiratory reserve volume. One-tenth and one-quarter understate the residual volume, which is roughly equal to the expiratory reserve volume of about 1,100 mL. Three-quarters and essentially all of it would leave almost no expiratory reserve volume, the part of FRC that can be forcibly expired and measured by spirometry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spirometer cannot measure FRC because the residual volume, about half of FRC (about 1200 of 2300 mL), can never be expired into it. COMLEX: First Aid restates the rule: RV and any capacity that includes RV cannot be measured by spirometry.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — maximum expiratory flow and why airways close before the lung empties","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q30_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Spirometry limitations; helium dilution method for FRC","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist performs helium dilution testing on a patient. The initial spirometer volume is 3,000 mL with 10% helium. After equilibration, the helium concentration measures 6%. The expiratory reserve volume measured by spirometry is 1,200 mL. What is the patient's residual volume?","choices":{"A":"800 mL","B":"1,000 mL","C":"1,200 mL","D":"1,800 mL","E":"3,000 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using the dilution equation: C1V1 = C2V2, where 0.10 × 3,000 = 0.06 × V2. Solving: 300 = 0.06 × V2, so V2 = 5,000 mL total volume. The FRC is the patient's lung volume at the start, which equals total volume minus initial spirometer volume: FRC = 5,000 - 3,000 = 2,000 mL. Since FRC = ERV + RV, then RV = FRC - ERV = 2,000 - 1,200 = 800 mL. This is slightly below normal RV, but represents the calculation from the given values. The other options represent incorrect calculations or misidentification of volumes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Helium is conserved, so initial concentration × spirometer volume = final concentration × (spirometer volume + FRC); after solving for FRC, RV = FRC − ERV. COMLEX: An RV well above about 1200 mL in a young adult points to air trapping, as in obstructive lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — obstructive pulmonary diseases with air trapping","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q31_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old man found unresponsive after an opioid overdose is breathing only 3 times per minute with shallow breaths. The resident asks how low minute respiratory volume can fall before survival, even for a short period, becomes impossible. According to Guyton, a person can live for a short time with a minute respiratory volume as low as approximately which value?","choices":{"A":"0.2 L/min","B":"4.5 L/min","C":"3.0 L/min","D":"1.5 L/min","E":"0.5 L/min"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton notes that minute respiratory volume, tidal volume times respiratory rate, averages about 6 L/min at rest, and that a person can live for a short period with a minute respiratory volume as low as 1.5 L/min and a respiratory rate of only 2 to 4 breaths per minute. Values of 0.2 and 0.5 L/min fall well below that stated floor. Values of 3.0 and 4.5 L/min are reduced from the normal 6 L/min but are well above the lowest volume Guyton gives for short-term survival.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton notes a person can live for a short time with a minute volume as low as 1.5 L/min and a rate of only 2–4 breaths/min, against a normal 6 L/min. COMLEX: First Aid lists CNS depression from opiate overdose as a cause of hypoventilation, with a normal A-a gradient and a raised PaCO2; naloxone reverses opioid overdose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — the medullary centers that generate respiratory rhythm","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q32_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old male rower performs a 15-second maximal voluntary ventilation test and reaches about 200 L/min, more than 30 times his resting value. His coach asks what level he could keep up for longer than 1 minute. Which estimate is most consistent with Guyton?","choices":{"A":"About 100 to 130 L/min","B":"About 200 L/min","C":"About 20 to 30 L/min","D":"About 50 to 60 L/min","E":"About 160 to 180 L/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that most people cannot sustain more than one-half to two-thirds of their maximal values for longer than 1 minute; the maximum, reached with a rate of 40 to 50 per minute and a depth near the vital capacity, can exceed 200 L/min, more than 30 times normal. Half to two-thirds of 200 L/min is about 100 to 130 L/min. A full 200 L/min is the brief peak, not a sustained value. 160 to 180 L/min exceeds two-thirds of the peak. 20 to 30 L/min and 50 to 60 L/min are far below what a trained young man can sustain under Guyton's one-half to two-thirds rule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maximal voluntary ventilation can exceed 200 L/min, but most people cannot sustain more than one-half to two-thirds of their maximal values for longer than 1 minute. COMLEX: Guyton names the energy cost of breathing itself as a major limit on exercise intensity, since it can rise 50-fold.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 12 — neurogenic control of ventilation during exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q33_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Minute respiratory volume (minute ventilation)","batch":1,"stem":"A patient in respiratory distress has rapid shallow breathing with a tidal volume of 200 mL and respiratory rate of 40 breaths per minute. Another patient has slow deep breathing with a tidal volume of 800 mL and respiratory rate of 10 breaths per minute. How do their minute ventilations compare?","choices":{"A":"The first patient has minute ventilation of 5,000 mL/min and the second has 8,000 mL/min","B":"The first patient has minute ventilation of 8,000 mL/min and the second has 8,000 mL/min","C":"The first patient has minute ventilation of 8,000 mL/min and the second has 10,000 mL/min","D":"The first patient has minute ventilation of 10,000 mL/min and the second has 5,000 mL/min","E":"The first patient has minute ventilation of 200 mL/min and the second has 800 mL/min"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First patient: Minute ventilation = 200 mL × 40 breaths/min = 8,000 mL/min. Second patient: Minute ventilation = 800 mL × 10 breaths/min = 8,000 mL/min. Both patients have the same minute ventilation despite very different breathing patterns. However, the first patient's shallow breathing is less efficient because a larger proportion of each breath ventilates dead space rather than alveoli, resulting in less effective gas exchange despite equal minute ventilation. The other options contain calculation errors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minute volume (VT × rate) can be identical in rapid-shallow and slow-deep breathing, but alveolar ventilation differs, because the roughly 150 mL of dead space is subtracted from every breath. COMLEX: First Aid ties breathing pattern to disease: restrictive patients take fast, shallow breaths and obstructive patients slower, deeper breaths to minimize work.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — alveolar ventilation and dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q34_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"A patient experiencing an asthma attack receives an injection of epinephrine. The medication causes bronchodilation through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Blocking muscarinic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle","B":"Stimulating beta-adrenergic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle","C":"Directly inhibiting mast cell degranulation in the airways","D":"Increasing parasympathetic tone to relax bronchiolar smooth muscle","E":"Stimulating alpha-adrenergic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Epinephrine causes bronchodilation by stimulating beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle, leading to relaxation and increased airway diameter. This represents sympathetic nervous system activation. The bronchioles have relatively sparse direct sympathetic innervation, but they are highly responsive to circulating epinephrine and norepinephrine. Muscarinic blockade describes the mechanism of anticholinergic drugs like ipratropium. Mast cell inhibition describes mast cell stabilizers like cromolyn. Increased parasympathetic tone is incorrect because increased parasympathetic tone causes bronchoconstriction, not dilation. Alpha-adrenergic stimulation is incorrect because alpha-adrenergic stimulation would cause constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epinephrine dilates the bronchial tree mainly through β-adrenergic receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle; it reaches the airways in the blood from the adrenal medulla, because sympathetic nerve supply to the bronchioles is sparse. COMLEX: Selective inhaled β2-agonists give the same bronchodilation for acute asthma; First Aid notes albuterol is the acute-symptom drug.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — current asthma and COPD treatment guidelines","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q35_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with asthma is already wheezing when she walks into a smoky room, and her bronchoconstriction suddenly worsens. Her physician explains that irritation of the airway epithelium has triggered a vagal reflex superimposed on her disease. Which drug acts by blocking the transmitter of these vagal fibers and can relax her airways enough to relieve part of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Timolol","B":"Phenylephrine","C":"Atropine","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Bethanechol"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton notes that the parasympathetic fibers that penetrate the lung secrete acetylcholine and cause mild to moderate bronchiolar constriction; when asthma has already narrowed the airways, superimposed parasympathetic stimulation, often reflexly triggered by smoke, dust, noxious gases, or infection, worsens it, and drugs that block acetylcholine such as atropine can relax the passages enough to relieve the obstruction. Timolol, a nonselective β-blocker, blocks β-adrenergic receptors and so removes the bronchodilating action of circulating epinephrine, which worsens bronchospasm. Phenylephrine is an α1 agonist with no bronchodilating action. Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and bethanechol is a muscarinic agonist, so both would increase cholinergic constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vagal fibers to the lung release acetylcholine and cause mild to moderate bronchiolar constriction; in asthma, superimposed parasympathetic stimulation worsens the obstruction, and blocking acetylcholine (atropine) can relieve part of it. COMLEX: Ipratropium and tiotropium are the inhaled muscarinic antagonists in current use for asthma and COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — therapeutic goals of asthma and COPD pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q36_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — SNS vs. PNS effects on bronchiole diameter","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is studying autonomic control of bronchiolar smooth muscle in isolated lung tissue. Application of norepinephrine causes bronchodilation, while application of acetylcholine causes bronchoconstriction. Which statement best explains the difference in direct sympathetic versus parasympathetic innervation of bronchioles?","choices":{"A":"Both sympathetic and parasympathetic systems provide equal direct neural innervation to bronchioles","B":"Parasympathetic innervation is extensive while direct sympathetic neural innervation is limited, though bronchioles respond to circulating catecholamines","C":"Sympathetic innervation is extensive while parasympathetic innervation is absent in normal bronchioles","D":"Neither system provides any innervation; all effects are mediated by circulating hormones","E":"Both systems innervate only the large airways, not the bronchioles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bronchioles receive significant direct parasympathetic innervation from the vagus nerve, with acetylcholine release causing bronchoconstriction via muscarinic receptors. In contrast, direct sympathetic neural innervation of bronchioles is quite limited. However, bronchiolar smooth muscle is highly responsive to circulating epinephrine and norepinephrine, which cause bronchodilation via beta-2 adrenergic receptors. This explains why sympathetic effects on bronchioles are mediated more by circulating catecholamines than by direct neural input. Equal dual innervation and absent parasympathetic innervation incorrectly describe the innervation patterns. No innervation at all and large-airway-only innervation are incorrect because parasympathetic innervation is definitely present and significant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parasympathetic (vagal) fibers penetrate the lung and constrict bronchioles via acetylcholine, while sympathetic fibers rarely reach the central lung, so bronchodilation depends on circulating catecholamines. COMLEX: First Aid summarizes asthma therapy as targeting two pathways, inflammation and parasympathetic tone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — pharmacological classes for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q37_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"In healthy lungs the greatest resistance to airflow lies in the larger bronchi near the trachea, not in the terminal bronchioles. During an allergic asthma attack, with mast-cell mediators acting on the airway walls, the smaller bronchioles become the main site of resistance. Which feature of the smaller bronchioles best explains this shift?","choices":{"A":"Their walls hold more cartilage than the bronchi","B":"They are far fewer in number than the large bronchi","C":"Their walls contain no smooth muscle at all","D":"Each carries a large share of the airflow","E":"Their small caliber lets them occlude easily"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton explains that normal resistance is highest in some of the larger bronchioles and bronchi near the trachea because there are relatively few of them compared with roughly 65,000 parallel terminal bronchioles, each carrying only a minute share of the flow. In disease the smaller bronchioles take over because of their small size: they are easily occluded by contraction of the smooth muscle in their walls, by edema in the walls, or by mucus collecting in their lumens, all of which follow release of histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis from mast cells. Cartilage plates become less extensive in later generations and are gone in the bronchioles, so they have less cartilage, not more. The bronchioles are far more numerous than the large bronchi, which is why they offer little resistance in health. Bronchiolar walls are almost entirely smooth muscle, the very tissue that contracts in asthma. Each bronchiole carries only a minute share of the total airflow, the reason its resistance is negligible in health.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normally resistance is highest in the larger bronchi near the trachea, because there are few of them compared with about 65,000 parallel terminal bronchioles; in disease the small bronchioles take over because they are easily occluded by muscle contraction, wall edema or mucus. COMLEX: Less than 1 cm H2O pressure drives quiet breathing in health, so even modest narrowing of many small airways raises the work of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — morphologic airway findings in status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q38_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is studying the pathophysiology of asthma and learns about various local factors that affect bronchiolar tone. The student reads that leukotrienes are particularly important in asthma because they cause prolonged bronchoconstriction. Leukotrienes are also known by which other name?","choices":{"A":"Fast-reacting substance of anaphylaxis","B":"Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis","C":"Immediate hypersensitivity factor","D":"Bradykinin","E":"Prostaglandin E2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leukotrienes are also called slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis because they produce more gradual but sustained bronchoconstriction compared to histamine. They are synthesized from arachidonic acid by mast cells, eosinophils, and other inflammatory cells. Leukotriene receptor antagonists (like montelukast) and leukotriene synthesis inhibitors (like zileuton) are used to treat asthma. Fast-reacting substance of anaphylaxis would better describe histamine, which acts rapidly. Immediate hypersensitivity factor is not a standard term. Bradykinin is a different inflammatory mediator. Prostaglandin E2 is a prostaglandin with different effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis is the leukotriene mixture released by mast cells; with histamine it is a key local constrictor of bronchioles in allergic asthma. COMLEX: First Aid links leukotriene overproduction to aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (asthma, nasal polyps, COX-inhibitor sensitivity: Samter's triad).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2 response that drives allergic asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q39_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Local secretory factors affecting bronchiole constriction","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmaceutical company is developing a new medication for asthma that works by preventing mast cell degranulation in the airways. By preventing mast cell degranulation, this medication would primarily reduce bronchoconstriction caused by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Reduced circulating epinephrine levels","B":"Enhanced parasympathetic acetylcholine release","C":"Decreased local release of histamine and leukotrienes","D":"Inhibited sympathetic nervous system activation","E":"Reduced surfactant production in alveoli"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Preventing mast cell degranulation would reduce the local release of bronchoconstrictive mediators, primarily histamine and leukotrienes (slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis). Mast cells are the major source of these local secretory factors in the airways, and their release is a key mechanism in asthma and allergic bronchoconstriction. This is the mechanism of mast cell stabilizers like cromolyn sodium. Reduced circulating epinephrine levels and inhibited sympathetic activation relate to sympathetic effects, which cause bronchodilation. Enhanced parasympathetic acetylcholine release relates to parasympathetic effects but is not affected by mast cell stabilization. Reduced surfactant production is unrelated to mast cell function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Blocking mast-cell degranulation prevents release of histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis, the mediators that constrict bronchioles in allergic asthma. COMLEX: Cromolyn is First Aid's mast-cell stabilizer; it prevents acute asthma symptoms but is rarely used.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — mast cell–mediated immunologic process in an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q40_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man accidentally inhales dust particles while cleaning his garage. The particles irritate his trachea, triggering a protective reflex. Which sequence of events best describes the cough reflex?","choices":{"A":"Rapid inhalation, vocal cords open, forceful expiration at low velocity","B":"Rapid inhalation, glottis closes, pressure builds to 100 mm Hg or more, explosive expulsion at high velocity","C":"Slow deep inhalation, glottis opens widely, gentle sustained expiration","D":"Rapid inhalation, bronchioles constrict, gradual expiration over several seconds","E":"Brief breath hold, glottis opens slowly, air expelled through nose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cough reflex involves: (1) rapid inspiration, (2) closure of the glottis and vocal cords, (3) forceful contraction of abdominal and respiratory muscles building pressure up to 100 mm Hg or higher, and (4) sudden opening of the glottis causing explosive expulsion of air at velocities of 75-100 mph. This high-velocity air flow can dislodge foreign material or mucus from the respiratory tract. Expiration with vocal cords open is incorrect because the vocal cords close, not open. Slow inhalation with gentle expiration describes normal breathing, not coughing. Gradual expiration with bronchoconstriction does not describe the explosive nature of cough. Breath hold with nasal expulsion describes components more similar to sneezing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cough sequence: rapid inspiration of up to 2.5 L, closure of epiglottis and vocal cords, forceful expiratory-muscle contraction raising lung pressure to 100 mm Hg or more, then sudden opening so air explodes outward and carries foreign matter. COMLEX: Corrosive gases such as sulfur dioxide or chlorine can trigger cough from as far down as the terminal bronchioles and alveoli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — tracheal epithelium and mucociliary clearance that work with the cough","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q41_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an impaired cough reflex due to muscle weakness. The physician explains that an effective cough requires the ability to generate high intrathoracic pressure. What intrathoracic pressure is typically achieved during the compressive phase of a normal cough?","choices":{"A":"10-20 mm Hg","B":"30-40 mm Hg","C":"50-70 mm Hg","D":"100 mm Hg or greater","E":"200-300 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During the compressive phase of coughing, intrathoracic pressure can reach 100 mm Hg or more due to forceful contraction of expiratory muscles against a closed glottis. This high pressure is essential for generating the explosive airflow needed to clear the airways effectively. When the glottis suddenly opens, the pressure differential creates air velocities of 75-100 mph. Patients with muscle weakness, like those with COPD or neuromuscular disease, may not generate adequate pressure for effective coughing. 10-20 mm Hg, 30-40 mm Hg, and 50-70 mm Hg represent pressures too low for effective cough. 200-300 mm Hg exceeds typical cough pressures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An effective cough needs forceful contraction of the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals against a closed glottis to raise lung pressure to about 100 mm Hg or more. COMLEX: The OMM course notes COPD hyperinflation shortens the respiratory muscles and raises their resting tone, reducing their efficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — clinical features of COPD (pink puffers and blue bloaters)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl7_ex1_q42_b1","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Cough reflex and sneeze reflex — mechanism and protective importance","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about protective airway reflexes. The student notes that both cough and sneeze reflexes involve forceful expulsion of air, but they differ in their initiating stimuli and the pathway of air expulsion. Which statement best describes the key difference between these reflexes?","choices":{"A":"Cough starts with laryngeal or tracheal irritation and exits the mouth; sneeze starts in the nose (CN V) and exits the nose","B":"Cough is triggered by nasal irritation via CN V, while sneeze is triggered by pharyngeal irritation via CN IX","C":"Cough involves lower intrathoracic pressures than sneeze","D":"Cough is a voluntary action while sneeze is entirely involuntary","E":"Cough expels air through the nose while sneeze expels air through the mouth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cough reflex is initiated by irritation of the larynx, trachea, or bronchi, and results in forceful expulsion of air primarily through the mouth to clear the lower airways. The sneeze reflex is initiated by irritation of the nasal passages, transmitted via the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V), and involves expulsion of air through both the nose and mouth, though predominantly through the nose, to clear the upper airways. Both reflexes involve similar high pressures and velocities but differ in their triggers and expulsion pathways. CN V cough versus CN IX sneeze reverses the triggers (the sneeze afferent is CN V, from the nose). Lower cough pressures is incorrect; both generate similar high pressures. Voluntary cough versus involuntary sneeze is incorrect; while cough can be voluntarily initiated, the reflex itself is involuntary. Nasal cough versus oral sneeze reverses the expulsion routes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cough is triggered from the larynx, trachea and bronchi with vagal afferents; sneeze is triggered in the nasal passages with trigeminal (CN V) afferents and the uvula depressed so air goes out the nose. COMLEX: First Aid lists altered mental status, with a weakened cough reflex or glottic closure, as a risk factor for aspiration pneumonia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — the medullary ventral respiratory group and its expiratory drive","isHighYield":false}],"9":[{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is reviewing the anatomy of the pulmonary circulation. The pulmonary artery extends from the apex of the right ventricle and divides into right and left main branches. Approximately how far beyond the apex of the right ventricle does the pulmonary artery extend before dividing?","choices":{"A":"5 centimeters","B":"10 centimeters","C":"15 centimeters","D":"1 centimeter","E":"3 centimeters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pulmonary artery extends only 5 centimeters beyond the apex of the right ventricle and then divides into right and left main branches that supply blood to the two respective lungs. This short length is characteristic of the pulmonary circulation and contrasts with the much longer course of the aorta in the systemic circulation. Understanding this anatomical relationship is important for interpreting chest imaging and understanding the hemodynamic properties of the pulmonary circulation, as the short distance means minimal resistance to flow from the right ventricle to the lung parenchyma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pulmonary trunk runs only about 5 cm beyond the apex of the right ventricle before dividing into right and left main branches; its branches are short and wide, and the pulmonary veins are short too, emptying straight into the left atrium. COMLEX: Because the pulmonary arteries carry all the blood returning to the right heart, an embolus lodged there leaves lung that is ventilated but not perfused (Moore).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism and infarction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is comparing the compliance of pulmonary arteries to that of systemic arteries. The pulmonary arterial tree has a compliance that averages almost 7 mL per mm Hg. How does this compliance compare quantitatively to that of the entire systemic arterial tree?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary arterial compliance is about one-tenth that of the systemic arterial tree","B":"Pulmonary arterial compliance is about one-half that of the systemic arterial tree","C":"Pulmonary arterial compliance is about twice that of the systemic arterial tree","D":"Pulmonary arterial compliance is about ten times that of the systemic arterial tree","E":"Pulmonary arterial compliance is equal to that of the systemic arterial tree"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The large compliance of the pulmonary arterial tree, averaging almost 7 mL/mm Hg, is similar to that of the entire systemic arterial tree. This large compliance allows the pulmonary arteries to accommodate the stroke volume output of the right ventricle with minimal increase in pulmonary arterial pressure. The pulmonary artery normally has a wall thickness one-third that of the aorta, and all the pulmonary arteries, even the smaller arteries and arterioles, have larger diameters than their counterpart systemic arteries. This feature, combined with the fact that the vessels are thin and distensible, gives the pulmonary arterial tree its large compliance, which is essential for the low-pressure, high-flow characteristics of the pulmonary circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary arteries have about one-third the wall thickness of the aorta and larger diameters than their systemic counterparts, so the pulmonary arterial tree's compliance (almost 7 mL/mm Hg) matches that of the whole systemic arterial tree and absorbs the right ventricle's stroke volume. COMLEX: First Aid describes the pulmonary circulation as a low-resistance, high-compliance system; pulmonary hypertension is a mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mm Hg at rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — causes, mechanisms and classification of pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with mitral stenosis develops acute left-sided heart failure. Blood begins to dam up in the left atrium, causing the pulmonary blood volume to increase. Under physiological conditions, the quantity of blood in the lungs can vary within what range relative to normal values?","choices":{"A":"From one-quarter normal to one-half normal","B":"From one-half normal to equal to normal","C":"From equal to normal to one and one-half times normal","D":"From one-half normal to twice normal","E":"From twice normal to four times normal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under various physiological and pathological conditions, the quantity of blood in the lungs can vary from as little as half-normal up to twice normal. The blood volume of the lungs is normally about 450 mL, about 9% of the total blood volume of the entire circulatory system. Approximately 70 mL of this pulmonary blood volume is in the pulmonary capillaries, and the remainder is divided about equally between the pulmonary arteries and veins. The ability of the pulmonary circulation to accommodate variable blood volumes is important in several clinical situations. For example, loss of blood from the systemic circulation by hemorrhage can be partly compensated for by the automatic shift of blood from the lungs into the systemic vessels. In this patient with mitral stenosis, increased resistance to blood flow through the mitral valve causes blood to dam up in the pulmonary circulation, potentially increasing the pulmonary blood volume as much as 100% and causing large increases in the pulmonary vascular pressures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lung blood volume (about 450 mL, 9% of the total) can range from half normal to twice normal; left heart failure or mitral stenosis or regurgitation dams blood back and can raise it by as much as 100%. COMLEX: Because the systemic circulation holds about nine times the pulmonary volume, such a shift affects the lungs greatly but the systemic circulation only mildly, so pulmonary congestion and edema dominate the picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 10 — sequence of changes in the pulmonary circulation as left atrial pressure rises","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology instructor is teaching about the lung's dual circulation. Blood flows to the lungs through small bronchial arteries that originate from the systemic circulation. What percentage of the total cardiac output do these bronchial arteries supply?","choices":{"A":"Less than 1%","B":"1% to 2%","C":"5% to 7%","D":"10% to 15%","E":"20% to 25%"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Blood also flows to the lungs through small bronchial arteries that originate from the systemic circulation, amounting to 1% to 2% of the total cardiac output. This bronchial arterial blood is oxygenated blood, in contrast to the partially deoxygenated blood in the pulmonary arteries. The bronchial arteries supply the supporting tissues of the lungs, including the connective tissue, septa, and large and small bronchi. After this bronchial and arterial blood passes through the supporting tissues, it empties into the pulmonary veins and enters the left atrium rather than passing back to the right atrium. Therefore, the flow into the left atrium and left ventricular output are about 1% to 2% greater than that of the right ventricular output. This dual circulation is functionally important because it allows the metabolic needs of the bronchial and supporting tissues to be met by fully oxygenated systemic arterial blood while the main pulmonary circulation handles gas exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation and carry 1–2% of cardiac output as oxygenated blood to the lung's supporting tissues (connective tissue, septa, large and small bronchi). COMLEX: Moore notes about 95% of hemoptysis comes from bronchial artery branches, with bronchitis, lung cancer, pneumonia, bronchiectasis, pulmonary embolism and tuberculosis as common causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, a common source of bronchial-artery hemoptysis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is comparing the two components of the lung's blood supply. Which statement best describes the fundamental difference in the role and characteristics of the bronchial circulation compared to the pulmonary circulation?","choices":{"A":"The bronchial circulation feeds supporting tissues with oxygenated blood, then drains to the left atrium","B":"The bronchial circulation receives all venous return from the body for oxygenation","C":"The bronchial circulation operates at lower pressure than the pulmonary circulation","D":"The bronchial circulation handles a larger volume of blood per minute than the pulmonary circulation","E":"The bronchial circulation carries deoxygenated blood at high pressure to the alveoli for gas exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lung has two circulations with distinct roles: a high-pressure, low-flow circulation and a low-pressure, high-flow circulation. The high-pressure, low-flow circulation is the bronchial circulation, which supplies systemic arterial blood to the trachea, bronchial tree including the terminal bronchioles, supporting tissues of the lung, and outer coats of the pulmonary arteries and veins. The bronchial arteries, which are branches of the thoracic aorta, supply most of this systemic arterial blood at a pressure that is only slightly lower than the aortic pressure. Importantly, after perfusing the supporting tissues with oxygenated blood, the bronchial venous drainage empties into the pulmonary veins and enters the left atrium, not the right atrium. This is in contrast to the low-pressure, high-flow pulmonary circulation that supplies venous blood from all parts of the body to the alveolar capillaries for gas exchange and returns oxygenated blood to the left atrium via the pulmonary veins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lung has two circulations: a high-pressure, low-flow bronchial circulation (branches of the thoracic aorta) nourishing airways, supporting tissue and vessel adventitia, and a low-pressure, high-flow pulmonary circulation carrying mixed venous blood to the alveolar capillaries. COMLEX: Most bronchial blood returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, so a little desaturated blood normally mixes into oxygenated pulmonary venous return.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic shunt, to which bronchial venous drainage contributes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"A cardiologist is evaluating a patient and notes that the left ventricular cardiac output is slightly greater than the right ventricular output. Which physiological mechanism best explains this normal finding?","choices":{"A":"Bronchial venous blood drains into pulmonary veins, not the right atrium","B":"The left ventricle is more muscular and generates higher stroke volume","C":"Coronary sinus blood drains into the left atrium, not the right atrium","D":"Pulmonary arteriovenous shunts direct blood from right to left circulation","E":"The left atrium receives blood from both pulmonary veins and superior vena cava"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bronchial arteries supply 1% to 2% of the total cardiac output to the supporting tissues of the lungs. After this bronchial and arterial blood passes through the supporting tissues, it empties into the pulmonary veins and enters the left atrium rather than passing back to the right atrium. Therefore, the flow into the left atrium and left ventricular output are about 1% to 2% greater than that of the right ventricular output. This is a normal physiological finding and should not be interpreted as pathological. Costanzo (Ch 5) adds that a small portion of coronary venous blood drains directly into the left ventricle through the thebesian veins, but the coronary sinus, which carries most coronary venous blood, empties into the right atrium, not the left atrium; the bronchial drainage is the exception Guyton uses to explain the extra left ventricular output. This unique drainage pattern means that the left heart handles slightly more blood volume than the right heart, though this small difference has minimal hemodynamic significance under normal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After supplying the lung's supporting tissues, bronchial blood empties into the pulmonary veins and the left atrium rather than the right atrium, so left atrial inflow and left ventricular output are about 1–2% greater than right ventricular output. COMLEX: Guyton (Ch 41) calls this about 2% bronchial flow shunt flow; its venous admixture (PO2 about 40 mm Hg) is why aortic PO2 sits below the 104 mm Hg of pulmonary capillary blood even in healthy lungs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic shunt vs. physiologic dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"A pathology resident is studying a histological section of lung tissue and identifies lymph vessels. Where do the lymph vessels in the lung begin, and what is their primary course through the lung tissue?","choices":{"A":"Lymph vessels begin in the alveolar walls and course along the pulmonary veins","B":"Lymph vessels begin in the pulmonary capillaries and drain into mediastinal nodes","C":"Lymph vessels begin in the visceral pleura and course toward the bronchi","D":"Lymph vessels begin around the terminal bronchioles and course to the hilum","E":"Lymph vessels begin in the interlobular septa and drain into axillary lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymph vessels are present in all the supportive tissues of the lung, beginning in the connective tissue spaces that surround the terminal bronchioles, coursing to the hilum of the lung, and then emptying mainly into the right thoracic lymph duct. This anatomical arrangement is crucial for removing excess fluid and protein from the lung tissues. Particulate matter entering the alveoli is partly removed by these lymph vessels, and plasma protein leaking from the lung capillaries is also removed from the lung tissues by the lymphatic system, thereby helping to prevent pulmonary edema. The strategic location of lymphatic vessels starting at the level of terminal bronchioles ensures effective drainage of the interstitial spaces while maintaining the delicate gas exchange surface of the alveoli free from lymphatic vessels that could interfere with diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary lymphatics lie in all supportive tissues of the lung, starting in the connective tissue around the terminal bronchioles, running to the hilum and draining mainly into the right thoracic lymph duct. COMLEX: The OMM course treats the thoracic inlet as a key region for lymphatic drainage, and respiration itself draws lymph centrally toward the veins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics and edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with congestive heart failure has elevated left atrial pressure. The physician explains that the lymphatic system helps protect against pulmonary edema. Which mechanism best describes how pulmonary lymphatics prevent fluid accumulation in the alveoli?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatics remove particulate matter and plasma proteins from lung tissues","B":"Lymphatics actively pump fluid from alveoli back into the bloodstream","C":"Lymphatics generate positive pressure to push fluid into pulmonary veins","D":"Lymphatics constrict in response to elevated capillary pressure","E":"Lymphatics secrete surfactant to reduce alveolar surface tension"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lymph vessels present in all the supportive tissues of the lung play a critical role in maintaining fluid balance and preventing pulmonary edema. Particulate matter entering the alveoli is partly removed by these lymph vessels, and plasma protein leaking from the lung capillaries is also removed from the lung tissues, thereby helping to prevent pulmonary edema. The lymphatics effectively drain excess interstitial fluid and maintain the negative pressure in the interstitial spaces. When extra fluid appears in the alveoli, it is sucked mechanically into the lung interstitium through the small openings between the alveolar epithelial cells, and this excess fluid is then carried away through the pulmonary lymphatics. This system becomes increasingly important when capillary pressure rises, as occurs in heart failure, helping to delay the development of overt pulmonary edema until capillary pressure exceeds a critical threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lymphatics keep the lung dry by removing particulate matter that reaches the alveoli and plasma protein that leaks from the capillaries, along with the slight continual filtrate from a mean filtration pressure of about +1 mm Hg. COMLEX: Lymphatic pump techniques in OMM aim to enhance this lymphatic return as an adjunct in pulmonary disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — Starling forces in the pulmonary interstitium and how edema develops","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"During a pulmonary pathophysiology lecture, a professor discusses the role of lymphatics in chronic pulmonary conditions. In patients with chronically elevated pulmonary capillary pressure, such as those with chronic mitral stenosis, what adaptive change occurs in the pulmonary lymphatic system?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic vessels atrophy and decrease in number","B":"Lymphatic smooth muscle becomes fibrotic and nonfunctional","C":"Lymphatic valves become incompetent leading to retrograde flow","D":"Lymphatic endothelium becomes impermeable to proteins","E":"Lymph vessels expand, carrying up to 10 times more fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When pulmonary capillary pressure remains elevated chronically for at least 2 weeks, the lungs become even more resistant to pulmonary edema because the lymph vessels expand greatly, increasing their capability of carrying fluid away from the interstitial spaces perhaps as much as 10-fold. This adaptive response is clinically significant. Therefore, in patients with chronic mitral stenosis, pulmonary capillary pressures of 40 to 45 mm Hg have been measured without the development of lethal pulmonary edema. This remarkable adaptation allows patients to tolerate much higher pulmonary pressures than would be possible acutely. However, this protective mechanism has its limits, and once the capacity of even the expanded lymphatic system is exceeded, pulmonary edema will develop. This explains why some patients with chronic heart failure can maintain relatively stable respiratory status despite severely elevated pulmonary pressures, but then decompensate rapidly when additional stress pushes them beyond this adaptive capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When pulmonary capillary pressure stays high for at least 2 weeks, lymph vessels expand and can carry perhaps 10 times more fluid, so chronic mitral stenosis can tolerate capillary pressures of 40–45 mm Hg without lethal edema. COMLEX: By contrast, acute left heart failure with capillary pressure near 50 mm Hg can kill from pulmonary edema in under 30 minutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — categories of pulmonary edema and their clinical causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"A cardiology fellow is reviewing right heart catheterization data. The normal systolic pressure in the right ventricle is reported as 25 mm Hg. How does this compare quantitatively to the systolic pressure in the left ventricle?","choices":{"A":"Right ventricular systolic pressure is equal to left ventricular systolic pressure","B":"Right ventricular systolic pressure is one-half that of the left ventricle","C":"Right ventricular systolic pressure is one-tenth that of the left ventricle","D":"Right ventricular systolic pressure is two-thirds that of the left ventricle","E":"Right ventricular systolic pressure is one-fifth that of the left ventricle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The normal systolic pressure in the right ventricle averages about 25 mm Hg, a value that is only one-fifth that for the left ventricle. This fundamental difference reflects the much lower resistance of the pulmonary circulation compared to the systemic circulation. The diastolic pressure in the right ventricle averages about 0 to 1 mm Hg, which is essentially atmospheric pressure. These pressure values are critical for understanding normal cardiopulmonary physiology and for interpreting hemodynamic data from right heart catheterization. The low pressures in the right heart and pulmonary circulation allow for efficient gas exchange while minimizing the work required by the right ventricle and reducing the risk of pulmonary edema. Elevations in right ventricular systolic pressure suggest increased pulmonary vascular resistance or downstream obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Right ventricular systolic pressure averages about 25 mm Hg, one-fifth that of the left ventricle, and its diastolic pressure is about 0–1 mm Hg. COMLEX: First Aid notes that pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular pressure, producing right ventricular hypertrophy (a parasternal heave) and eventually right heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension and its effect on the right heart","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"During a hemodynamics tutorial, a medical student learns about pulmonary artery pressures. In a normal adult, the systolic pulmonary arterial pressure is about 25 mm Hg and the diastolic pressure is about 8 mm Hg. What is the mean pulmonary arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"8 mm Hg","B":"15 mm Hg","C":"20 mm Hg","D":"25 mm Hg","E":"30 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The systolic pulmonary arterial pressure normally averages about 25 mm Hg in the human being, the diastolic pulmonary arterial pressure is about 8 mm Hg, and the mean pulmonary arterial pressure is 15 mm Hg. These values contrast dramatically with the much higher aortic pressure curve, where systolic pressure averages about 120 mm Hg and diastolic about 80 mm Hg. The mean pulmonary arterial pressure is calculated as approximately diastolic pressure plus one-third of the pulse pressure. Understanding these normal values is essential for clinical practice, and pulmonary hypertension is defined by a raised resting mean pulmonary arterial pressure: > 20 mm Hg in First Aid 2025, ≥ 25 mm Hg in Robbins Ch 15 (the course text). The low mean pressure in the pulmonary circulation allows the right ventricle to pump the same cardiac output as the left ventricle against a far lower pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25 mm Hg systolic, 8 mm Hg diastolic and 15 mm Hg mean, far below aortic pressure. COMLEX: First Aid defines pulmonary hypertension as a resting mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mm Hg, only a few mm Hg above this normal mean.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension causes and classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher is measuring pressures throughout the pulmonary circulation using micro-catheters. The mean pulmonary capillary pressure is estimated to be about 7 mm Hg. Between which two other measured pressures in the pulmonary circulation must the pulmonary capillary pressure lie?","choices":{"A":"Between right ventricular pressure and pulmonary arterial pressure","B":"Between pulmonary arterial pressure and left atrial pressure","C":"Between left atrial pressure and left ventricular pressure","D":"Between right atrial pressure and right ventricular pressure","E":"Between bronchial arterial pressure and pulmonary venous pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mean pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mm Hg. This value is probably nearly correct because the mean left atrial pressure is about 2 mm Hg, and the mean pulmonary arterial pressure is only 15 mm Hg, so the mean pulmonary capillary pressure must lie somewhere between these two values. The importance of this low capillary pressure is discussed in detail later in the chapter in relation to fluid exchange functions of the pulmonary capillaries. The low pulmonary capillary pressure, only about 7 mm Hg, is critical for preventing pulmonary edema. In contrast, functional capillary pressure in many peripheral tissues is about 17 to 25 mm Hg. This pressure gradient from the pulmonary artery through the capillaries to the left atrium drives blood flow through the lungs, and the low capillary pressure is achieved because the pulmonary vasculature has such low resistance and high compliance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mean pulmonary capillary pressure is estimated at about 7 mm Hg, which must lie between the mean pulmonary arterial pressure (15 mm Hg) and the mean left atrial pressure (about 2 mm Hg). COMLEX: First Aid's PVR formula uses the same gradient: PVR = (pulmonary artery pressure − left atrial pressure) / cardiac output, with wedge pressure standing in for left atrial pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 11 — significance of pulmonary capillary pressure for gas exchange and fluid balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist is examining cross-sections of the pulmonary artery and aorta under the microscope. How does the wall thickness of the pulmonary artery compare to that of the aorta?","choices":{"A":"The pulmonary artery wall is about one-tenth the thickness of the aorta","B":"The pulmonary artery wall is about one-fifth the thickness of the aorta","C":"The pulmonary artery wall is about one-half the thickness of the aorta","D":"The pulmonary artery wall is equal in thickness to the aorta","E":"The pulmonary artery wall is about one-third the thickness of the aorta"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pulmonary artery normally has a wall thickness one-third that of the aorta. This structural difference reflects the fundamental functional differences between the pulmonary and systemic circulations. The pulmonary circulation operates at much lower pressures than the systemic circulation, so the pulmonary artery does not require the thick muscular wall needed by the aorta to withstand systemic arterial pressures. All the pulmonary arteries, even the smaller arteries and arterioles, have larger diameters than their counterpart systemic arteries. This feature, combined with the fact that the vessels are thin and distensible, gives the pulmonary arterial tree a large compliance. This high compliance allows the pulmonary arteries to accommodate the stroke volume output of the right ventricle with minimal increase in pressure, which is essential for the low-pressure, high-flow characteristics of the pulmonary circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pulmonary artery wall is about one-third as thick as the aorta's, matching the much lower pressures it carries. COMLEX: First Aid lists medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis and plexiform lesions of the pulmonary arteries as the vascular changes of pulmonary hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — vascular changes in pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes exercise stress testing. As her cardiac output increases from 5 L/min at rest to 20 L/min during maximal exercise, the pulmonary vessels accommodate this increased blood flow. Which structural characteristic of pulmonary vessels primarily allows them to accommodate such large increases in blood flow without major increases in pulmonary arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"Thick smooth muscle walls that actively dilate","B":"High vascular resistance that redistributes flow","C":"Rigid structure that maintains constant diameter","D":"Thin walls and large diameters providing high compliance","E":"Multiple sphincters that regulate flow distribution"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pulmonary artery normally has a wall thickness one-third that of the aorta. All the pulmonary arteries, even the smaller arteries and arterioles, have larger diameters than their counterpart systemic arteries. This feature, combined with the fact that the vessels are thin and distensible, gives the pulmonary arterial tree a large compliance, averaging almost 7 mL/mm Hg, which is similar to that of the entire systemic arterial tree. This large compliance allows the pulmonary arteries to accommodate the stroke volume output of the right ventricle with minimal pressure increase. During heavy exercise, blood flow through the lungs may increase 4-fold to 7-fold. This extra flow is accommodated in the lungs in three ways: by increasing the number of open capillaries, by distending all the capillaries and increasing the rate of flow through each capillary more than 2-fold, and by increasing the pulmonary arterial pressure. The first two changes decrease pulmonary vascular resistance so much that the pulmonary arterial pressure rises very little, even during maximum exercise. The vessels are not thick or muscular: the pulmonary artery wall is one-third as thick as the aorta's, and the extra flow is taken up passively by opening and distending vessels rather than by active muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thin, distensible walls and large diameters give the pulmonary vessels high compliance; in heavy exercise flow can rise 4- to 7-fold, met by recruiting up to 3 times more open capillaries and distending all of them, so pulmonary artery pressure rises very little. COMLEX: This low-pressure accommodation conserves right-heart energy and prevents exercise-induced pulmonary edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — pulmonary blood flow during heavy exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about pulmonary vascular resistance. For adequate aeration of the blood, blood must be distributed to the lung segments where the alveoli are best oxygenated. Which mechanism accomplishes this distribution?","choices":{"A":"Slowing of flow at low pressures to maximize oxygen extraction","B":"Constriction of vessels adjacent to alveoli with low PO₂","C":"Dilation of vessels adjacent to alveoli with low PO₂","D":"Turbulent flow that improves alveolar gas mixing","E":"Passive distension of vessels as pulmonary pressure rises"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Blood is directed to the best-aerated segments by hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: when alveolar O₂ falls below normal, the adjacent vessels constrict, the opposite of the response in systemic vessels, so blood flows through better-aerated areas instead (Guyton Ch 39). This is an automatic control system that distributes flow in proportion to alveolar PO₂. Dilation of vessels next to poorly oxygenated alveoli would send blood the wrong way. Passive distension with rising pressure lowers resistance and recruits vessels as flow rises, but it does not choose well-oxygenated segments. Slowing of flow and turbulence play no role in matching perfusion to ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When alveolar O2 falls below about 70% of normal (PO2 < 73 mm Hg), the adjacent vessels constrict, raising local resistance up to 5-fold and diverting blood to better-ventilated alveoli; systemic vessels do the opposite and dilate. COMLEX: First Aid warns that chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction can lead to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale, as in COPD, obstructive sleep apnea or high-altitude living.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — the V/Q ratio and its effect on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is teaching medical students about the bronchial circulation. Blood in the bronchial arteries has which oxygenation status, and what is the source of these vessels?","choices":{"A":"Deoxygenated blood from the pulmonary artery","B":"Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle","C":"Partially oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins","D":"Oxygenated blood from the thoracic aorta","E":"Mixed oxygenated and deoxygenated blood from coronary sinus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Blood flows to the lungs through small bronchial arteries that originate from the systemic circulation, specifically from branches of the thoracic aorta. This bronchial arterial blood is oxygenated blood, in contrast to the partially deoxygenated blood in the pulmonary arteries. The bronchial arteries supply the supporting tissues of the lungs, including the connective tissue, septa, and large and small bronchi. The bronchial circulation provides nutritive blood flow to the airways and supporting structures of the lung, which require oxygenated blood for their metabolic needs. This is functionally important because these structures do not participate in gas exchange and therefore cannot obtain oxygen from the air in the airways. The bronchial arteries supply systemic arterial blood at a pressure that is only slightly lower than the aortic pressure, amounting to 1% to 2% of the total cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial arteries are branches of the thoracic aorta carrying oxygenated systemic blood at a pressure only slightly below aortic pressure; Moore adds that the left bronchial arteries usually arise directly from the aorta, while the right one often comes off a posterior intercostal artery. COMLEX: Because of this near-aortic pressure, bleeding from bronchial artery branches (about 95% of hemoptysis) can be brisk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — why the dual blood supply makes most pulmonary emboli not infarct","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic bronchitis has increased metabolic demands in his bronchial supporting tissues. The bronchial arteries supply oxygenated blood to specific lung structures. Which structures are primarily supplied by the bronchial circulation?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar epithelium and respiratory membrane","B":"Pulmonary capillaries and alveolar septa","C":"Connective tissue, septa, and large and small bronchi","D":"Alveolar ducts and alveolar sacs","E":"Type I and Type II pneumocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The bronchial arterial blood supplies the supporting tissues of the lungs, including the connective tissue, septa, and large and small bronchi. After this bronchial and arterial blood passes through the supporting tissues, it empties into the pulmonary veins and enters the left atrium rather than passing back to the right atrium. The bronchial circulation does not supply the alveolar structures directly involved in gas exchange, as these receive their blood supply from the pulmonary circulation. The separation of nutritive blood flow to supporting structures from gas exchange blood flow to alveolar capillaries is an important functional distinction. The supporting tissues of the lungs, including the bronchial walls, require oxygenated blood for their metabolic needs, and this is provided by the bronchial arteries originating from the systemic circulation at systemic arterial pressure. The alveolar epithelium and respiratory membrane, type I and type II pneumocytes, the pulmonary capillaries in the alveolar walls, and the alveolar ducts and sacs are all gas-exchange structures served by the pulmonary circulation (Guyton Ch 39).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial arterial blood supplies the lung's supporting tissues: connective tissue, septa, and large and small bronchi, plus (per Moore) the visceral pleura and structures of the lung root. COMLEX: Chronic airway disease such as chronic bronchitis is among Moore's common causes of hemoptysis from these vessels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — chronic bronchitis among the major obstructive diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton describes the bronchial arteries as the lung's high-pressure, low-flow circulation, delivering systemic arterial blood to the trachea, the bronchial tree down to the terminal bronchioles, and the supporting tissues of the lung. Which other structure depends on this circulation for its systemic arterial supply?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar walls of the gas-exchange units","B":"Parietal pleura lining the chest wall","C":"Outer coat of pulmonary arteries and veins","D":"Endothelium lining the alveolar capillary bed","E":"Myocardium forming the left atrial wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton lists the outer coats (adventitia) of the pulmonary arteries and veins among the tissues supplied by the high-pressure, low-flow bronchial circulation, along with the trachea, the bronchial tree including the terminal bronchioles, and the supporting tissues of the lung. The alveolar walls and the endothelium of the alveolar capillary bed belong to the low-pressure, high-flow pulmonary circulation; Pawlina likewise states that the bronchial arteries supply all lung tissue other than the alveoli. The parietal pleura is supplied by the arteries of the thoracic wall, whereas the bronchial arteries reach the visceral pleura (Moore). The left atrial myocardium is supplied by the coronary circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton lists the outer coats (adventitia) of the pulmonary arteries and veins, along with the trachea, bronchial tree to the terminal bronchioles and lung supporting tissues, as supplied by the high-pressure, low-flow bronchial circulation. COMLEX: The alveolar walls are not on this list; they are nourished by the pulmonary capillary blood they exchange gas with.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — terminal bronchioles, the end of the bronchial arteries' airway territory","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q19_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist is studying the effects of gravity on pulmonary blood flow. In an upright adult standing at rest, the lowest point in the lungs is normally about 30 cm below the highest point. What is the approximate pressure difference in mm Hg between the top and bottom of the lungs due to hydrostatic pressure?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg","B":"10 mm Hg","C":"15 mm Hg","D":"23 mm Hg","E":"30 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the upright adult, the lowest point in the lungs is normally about 30 cm below the highest point, which represents a pressure difference of 23 mm Hg. Hydrostatic pressure, that is, the weight of the blood in the blood vessels, causes a corresponding effect in the lungs. In the upright adult, the pulmonary arterial pressure in the uppermost portion of the lung of a standing person is about 15 mm Hg less than the pulmonary arterial pressure at the level of the heart, and the pressure in the lowest portion of the lungs is about 8 mm Hg higher. About 15 mm Hg of this 23 mm Hg pressure difference is above the heart and 8 mm Hg below. Such pressure differences have profound effects on blood flow through the different areas of the lungs. This gravitational effect means that at rest, blood flow at the bottom of the lung is about 5 times as great as in the top of the lung, which has important implications for ventilation-perfusion matching and gas exchange efficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In an upright adult the lung base is about 30 cm below the apex, a hydrostatic difference of 23 mm Hg: apical arterial pressure is about 15 mm Hg below heart level and basal pressure about 8 mm Hg above. COMLEX: First Aid notes both ventilation and perfusion are greater at the base than the apex, with V/Q about 3 at the apex and 0.6 at the base.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — regional V/Q ratio and alveolar gas pressures","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q20_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"During a pulmonary physiology lecture, a professor explains how the lung can be divided into three zones based on blood flow patterns. In Zone 2 of the lung, which pressure relationship determines that blood flow is intermittent, occurring only during systole?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar pressure exceeds both arterial and venous pressures throughout the cardiac cycle","B":"Arterial pressure is always greater than alveolar pressure which is always greater than venous pressure","C":"Venous pressure exceeds alveolar pressure which exceeds arterial pressure during diastole","D":"Systolic arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure but diastolic arterial pressure does not","E":"Capillary pressure equals alveolar pressure creating continuous laminar flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zone 2 has intermittent blood flow only during the peaks of pulmonary arterial pressure because the systolic pressure is then greater than the alveolar air pressure, but the diastolic pressure is less than the alveolar air pressure. The capillaries in the alveolar walls are distended by the blood pressure inside them but simultaneously are compressed by the alveolar air pressure on their outsides. Therefore, whenever the lung alveolar air pressure becomes greater than the capillary blood pressure, the capillaries close, and there is no blood flow. Zone 2 blood flow begins in normal lungs about 10 cm above the midlevel of the heart and extends from there to the top of the lungs. Under different normal and pathological lung conditions, one may find multiple zones of pulmonary blood flow, and understanding these zones is critical for interpreting blood flow distribution in the lungs and for understanding conditions such as pulmonary embolism or positive pressure ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In zone 2, systolic arterial pressure exceeds alveolar air pressure but diastolic pressure does not, so capillaries open only at pressure peaks; normally this zone begins about 10 cm above mid-heart and runs to the apex. COMLEX: First Aid's zone notation is Pa > PA > Pv for zone 2, where flow depends on the arterial–alveolar difference.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — how regional V/Q differences alter alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q21_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about regional blood flow distribution in the lungs. When a person is standing upright at rest, blood flow at the bottom of the lung is approximately how many times greater than blood flow at the top of the lung?","choices":{"A":"Equal blood flow throughout","B":"2 times greater","C":"5 times greater","D":"10 times greater","E":"20 times greater"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hydrostatic pressure effect demonstrates that blood flow in all parts of the lung increases during exercise. Guyton's measurements of blood flow at different levels of the upright lung show that at rest the blood flow is very low at the top of the lungs but about 5 times as great at the bottom. Most of the flow is through the bottom of the lung. This uneven distribution of blood flow has important implications for gas exchange efficiency. During exercise, the increase in pulmonary vascular pressures causes more uniform distribution of blood flow throughout the lung. A major reason for increased blood flow is that the pulmonary vascular pressures rise enough during exercise to convert the lung apices from a zone 2 pattern to a zone 3 pattern of flow, allowing continuous rather than intermittent blood flow through the apical capillaries. Understanding this gravitational effect on blood flow is important for interpreting ventilation-perfusion relationships and for understanding why certain pathologies preferentially affect different regions of the lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At rest in the upright position, blood flow per unit of lung at the base is about 5 times that at the top, because hydrostatic pressure adds to arterial pressure low in the lung and subtracts from it at the apex. COMLEX: First Aid notes organisms that thrive in high O2, such as TB, flourish in the high-V/Q apex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 10 — late-stage tuberculosis and its effects on lung function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q22_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is explaining the three zones of pulmonary blood flow to a resident. In Zone 1 of the lung, what is the characteristic pressure relationship that results in no blood flow during any portion of the cardiac cycle?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar air pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure and venous pressure","B":"Pulmonary arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure exceeds venous pressure","C":"Venous pressure exceeds alveolar pressure exceeds arterial pressure","D":"Capillary pressure equals alveolar pressure creating equilibrium","E":"Arterial pressure equals venous pressure and both exceed alveolar pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 1 has no blood flow during all portions of the cardiac cycle because the local alveolar capillary pressure in that area of the lung never rises higher than the alveolar air pressure during any part of the cardiac cycle. This occurs when the pulmonary systolic arterial pressure is too low or the alveolar pressure is too high to allow flow. Zone 1 blood flow normally occurs only under abnormal conditions. For example, if an upright person is breathing against a positive air pressure so that the intraalveolar air pressure is at least 10 mm Hg greater than normal but the pulmonary systolic blood pressure is normal, one would expect zone 1 blood flow in the lung apices. Another case in which zone 1 blood flow occurs is in an upright person whose pulmonary systolic arterial pressure is exceedingly low, as might occur after severe blood loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zone 1 has no flow at any point in the cardiac cycle because local capillary pressure never exceeds alveolar air pressure; it occurs only when pulmonary systolic pressure is too low or alveolar pressure too high. COMLEX: Normal lungs have only zones 2 and 3; zone 1 adds alveolar dead space, and First Aid writes it as PA ≥ Pa > Pv.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space and its implications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q23_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"During an ICU consultation, a critical care physician discusses a mechanically ventilated patient receiving positive end-expiratory pressure therapy. The physician explains that excessive positive pressure ventilation can convert normally perfused lung regions into which zone of pulmonary blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Zone 1, creating alveolar dead space","B":"Zone 2, improving ventilation-perfusion matching","C":"Zone 3, increasing capillary recruitment","D":"Zone 4, causing pulmonary edema","E":"All zones equally, maintaining uniform perfusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 1 blood flow, which means no blood flow at any time during the cardiac cycle, normally occurs only under abnormal conditions. For example, if an upright person is breathing against a positive air pressure so that the intraalveolar air pressure is at least 10 mm Hg greater than normal but the pulmonary systolic blood pressure is normal, one would expect zone 1 blood flow in the lung apices. This can occur during positive pressure mechanical ventilation, especially with high levels of positive end-expiratory pressure. When alveolar pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure, the capillaries collapse and blood flow ceases, creating alveolar dead space where ventilation occurs without perfusion. This represents wasted ventilation and impairs gas exchange efficiency. Clinicians must balance the benefits of positive pressure ventilation for oxygenation and lung recruitment against the potential adverse effects of creating zone 1 conditions and increasing dead space. Monitoring dead space fraction can help optimize ventilator settings in critically ill patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Breathing against positive airway pressure that raises alveolar pressure by at least 10 mm Hg, with normal pulmonary systolic pressure, should produce zone 1 (no flow) at the upright lung apices. COMLEX: Ventilated-but-unperfused alveoli are dead space, so high positive pressure can raise dead space as well as risk barotrauma, which First Aid lists as a cause of secondary pneumothorax.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space in lung disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q24_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology professor is teaching about Zone 3 blood flow in the lungs. In the lower regions of the lungs, from about 10 cm above the level of the heart all the way to the bottom of the lungs, the pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole remains greater than the alveolar air pressure. What is the characteristic blood flow pattern in Zone 3?","choices":{"A":"No blood flow occurs at any point in the cardiac cycle","B":"Blood flow occurs only during systole but ceases during diastole","C":"Blood flow is continuous throughout the entire cardiac cycle","D":"Blood flow alternates between forward and retrograde directions","E":"Blood flow is turbulent with unpredictable variations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Zone 3 has continuous blood flow because the alveolar capillary pressure remains greater than alveolar air pressure during the entire cardiac cycle. In the lower regions of the lungs, from about 10 cm above the level of the heart all the way to the bottom of the lungs, the pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole remains greater than the 0 alveolar air pressure. Therefore, continuous flow occurs through the alveolar capillaries, or zone 3 blood flow. Normally, the lungs have only zones 2 and 3 blood flow: zone 2 in the apices and zone 3 in all the lower areas. Zone 3 represents the most efficient region for gas exchange because blood flows continuously through the capillaries, maximizing the time available for oxygen uptake and carbon dioxide elimination. Also, when a person is lying down, no part of the lung is more than a few centimeters above the level of the heart, so in this case, blood flow in a normal person is entirely zone 3 blood flow, including the lung apices. This positional change in blood flow distribution is one reason why patients with certain lung diseases may have different symptoms when lying down versus standing up.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Below about 10 cm above heart level, pulmonary arterial pressure stays above the zero alveolar pressure in both systole and diastole, so capillary flow is continuous (zone 3). COMLEX: First Aid writes zone 3 as Pa > Pv > PA; this is where most perfusion goes, giving the lung base a low V/Q of about 0.6.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — V/Q ratio and its effects on alveolar gases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q25_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is studying the pulmonary circulation and notes that the pulmonary artery extends only 5 centimeters beyond the apex of the right ventricle before dividing. What is the primary anatomical destination of these two main branches?","choices":{"A":"The right and left main bronchi for bronchial supply","B":"The superior and inferior pulmonary lobes bilaterally","C":"The right and left lungs respectively","D":"The anterior and posterior pulmonary segments","E":"The apical and basilar zones of each lung"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pulmonary artery extends only about 5 centimeters beyond the right ventricle before dividing into right and left main branches that supply blood to the two respective lungs. This short length before branching is characteristic of the pulmonary circulation. Main bronchi incorrectly refers to the bronchial tree. Superior and inferior lobes incorrectly describes lobar divisions. Anterior and posterior segments and apical and basilar zones describe anatomical segments but not the main arterial branching pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pulmonary trunk divides about 5 cm beyond the right ventricular apex into right and left main pulmonary arteries, one to each lung. COMLEX: First Aid's RALS rule places the pulmonary artery anterior to the bronchus at the right hilum and superior to it at the left hilum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — development of the bronchi and bronchopulmonary segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q26_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old patient undergoes pulmonary function testing. The physician explains that the lung receives blood from two distinct circulatory systems. Which statement best characterizes these two systems?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary: high-pressure, low-flow; bronchial: low-pressure, high-flow","B":"Pulmonary: oxygenated blood to lung tissue; bronchial: venous blood for gas exchange","C":"Pulmonary: low-pressure, high-flow for gas exchange; bronchial: high-pressure, low-flow","D":"Both carry mixed venous blood from the right ventricle at low pressure","E":"Bronchial flow equals pulmonary flow, each about half the cardiac output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton describes the lung as having two circulations: a low-pressure, high-flow pulmonary circulation that carries the whole cardiac output of mixed venous blood to the alveolar capillaries for gas exchange, and a high-pressure, low-flow bronchial circulation, a branch of the systemic arteries, that carries oxygenated blood (about 1–2% of cardiac output) to the supporting tissues — connective tissue, septa and bronchi (Ch 39). Reversing the pressure–flow labels, or the roles of the two circulations, gives the distractors. Only the pulmonary circulation carries mixed venous blood from the right ventricle, and bronchial flow is a tiny fraction of pulmonary flow, not half the cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pulmonary circulation is low-pressure and high-flow, carrying the whole cardiac output of mixed venous blood to the alveolar capillaries; the bronchial circulation is high-pressure and low-flow, carrying oxygenated aortic blood to airways and supporting tissue. COMLEX: First Aid calculates pulmonary vascular resistance as (pulmonary artery pressure − left atrial pressure) / cardiac output.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — step-by-step O2 and CO2 exchange in the pulmonary capillaries","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q27_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulatory system","batch":1,"stem":"During a physiology lecture, a student learns that approximately 70 mL of the total pulmonary blood volume of 450 mL resides in a specific location. Where is this blood primarily located?","choices":{"A":"The pulmonary veins returning to left atrium","B":"The pulmonary capillaries where gas exchange occurs","C":"The bronchial arteries supplying lung tissues","D":"The right ventricular outflow tract region","E":"The left atrial chamber receiving blood"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Of the approximately 450 mL total blood volume in the lungs, approximately 70 mL resides in the pulmonary capillaries where gas exchange occurs. The remainder is divided roughly equally between pulmonary arteries and veins. This relatively small capillary volume is sufficient because blood flows as a \"sheet of flow.\" The right ventricular outflow tract is part of the heart and is not counted in the roughly 450 mL lung blood volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Of the roughly 450 mL in the lungs, only about 70 mL is in the pulmonary capillaries; the rest is split about equally between arteries and veins, and capillary blood flows through the alveolar walls as a \"sheet of flow.\" COMLEX: First Aid lists the reduced alveolar–capillary surface area of emphysema as a cause of a low diffusing capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — structure and function of the respiratory membrane","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q28_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"During dissection of the root of the right lung, a surgeon identifies the right bronchial vein. Guyton states that most bronchial blood empties into the pulmonary veins, but the bronchial veins proper also carry part of it away. Into which vessel does the right bronchial vein drain?","choices":{"A":"Right pulmonary vein","B":"Azygos vein","C":"Accessory hemiazygos vein","D":"Superior vena cava","E":"Brachiocephalic vein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Moore describes the bronchial veins as draining only part of the bronchial arterial blood, mainly that distributed to the proximal part of the lung root; the right bronchial vein drains into the azygos vein, and the left into the accessory hemiazygos vein or the left superior intercostal vein. The rest, from the visceral pleura, the peripheral lung, and the distal root, is drained by the pulmonary veins, which is the pathway Guyton emphasizes and the reason left ventricular output slightly exceeds right ventricular output. Pawlina similarly notes that bronchial veins drain only the connective tissue of the hilar region. A right pulmonary vein carries the larger, distal share of bronchial blood but does not receive the bronchial vein itself. The accessory hemiazygos vein receives the left bronchial vein, not the right. The superior vena cava and the brachiocephalic vein are not the named termination of the right bronchial vein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Moore says bronchial veins drain only part of the bronchial arterial blood, mainly from the proximal lung root; the right bronchial vein drains into the azygos vein and the left into the accessory hemiazygos or left superior intercostal vein. COMLEX: Guyton's point stands: most bronchial blood still returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, so left ventricular output slightly exceeds right.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic shunt","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q29_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old professional trumpet player holds a long, forceful note, blowing out so hard that high pressure builds up in his lungs. Normal pulmonary blood volume is about 450 mL. What happens to the blood in his pulmonary circulation during the note?","choices":{"A":"Nearly all 450 mL empties into the systemic veins","B":"Pulmonary volume doubles as the vessels distend","C":"About 1 L moves from systemic veins into the lungs","D":"Pulmonary volume stays fixed near 450 mL","E":"Up to about 250 mL shifts into systemic vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes the lungs as a blood reservoir whose volume can vary from about half normal to twice normal; when a person blows out so hard that high pressure builds in the lungs, as in playing a trumpet, as much as 250 mL of blood can be expelled from the pulmonary circulation into the systemic circulation. Emptying nearly all 450 mL overstates the shift; the lungs fall at most to about half their normal volume. Doubling of pulmonary volume and a 1-L shift into the lungs describe blood damming up in the pulmonary circulation, as in left heart failure or mitral stenosis, not raised intrathoracic pressure. A fixed pulmonary volume ignores the reservoir function, which also lets blood shift from the lungs into the systemic vessels to offset hemorrhage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lungs act as a blood reservoir: blowing hard against high pulmonary pressure, as with a trumpet, can push as much as 250 mL of blood into the systemic circulation, and hemorrhage is partly offset by a similar shift. COMLEX: The same high intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return to the chest, a mechanism First Aid describes for tension pneumothorax.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — alveolar and pleural pressures during expiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q30_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Dual circulation — bronchial (high-pressure/low-flow) vs. pulmonary (low-pressure/high-flow)","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old patient with pulmonary hypertension asks why the bronchial vessels are important. Which function is specifically served by the bronchial circulation but not the pulmonary circulation?","choices":{"A":"Facilitating oxygen exchange at alveolar-capillary membrane","B":"Supplying nutrients to the septa and bronchi","C":"Removing carbon dioxide from systemic venous blood","D":"Distributing blood flow according to ventilation ratios","E":"Accommodating increased cardiac output during exercise"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bronchial circulation specifically supplies oxygenated blood and nutrients to the supporting structures including connective tissue, septa, and bronchi. These tissues require oxygen and nutrients for metabolic needs that cannot be met by deoxygenated blood in pulmonary circulation. Taking up the extra cardiac output of exercise is a job of the pulmonary circulation, which opens and distends its capillaries; the bronchial supply is only about 1% to 2% of cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The bronchial circulation, not the pulmonary, supplies oxygenated blood and nutrients to the lung's supporting tissues (connective tissue, septa, bronchi) at nearly aortic pressure. COMLEX: Robbins explains that with adequate cardiovascular function this bronchial supply sustains the parenchyma after a pulmonary embolus, so hemorrhage may occur without infarction; only about 10% of emboli cause infarcts, more often in heart or lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — pulmonary embolism and infarction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q31_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist is examining lung tissue and observes lymph vessels coursing through the supportive tissues. These lymph vessels begin in specific locations and empty into a major lymphatic duct. Where do pulmonary lymph vessels primarily drain?","choices":{"A":"The thoracic duct on the left side exclusively","B":"The azygos vein bilaterally for venous return","C":"The right thoracic lymph duct primarily","D":"The superior vena cava directly for drainage","E":"The left subclavian vein directly without ducts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lymph vessels in the lungs are present in all supportive tissues, beginning in connective tissue spaces surrounding terminal bronchioles and coursing to the hilum. They then empty mainly into the right thoracic lymph duct. This drainage pathway is important for removing particulate matter and plasma proteins. Guyton routes lung lymph to the hilum and then mainly into the right thoracic lymph duct, not straight into the azygos vein or the left subclavian vein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary lymphatics run from the connective tissue around the terminal bronchioles to the hilum and empty mainly into the right thoracic lymph duct. COMLEX: Moore notes lymph from the lungs carries phagocytes that move inhaled carbon into the supporting connective tissue and the lymph nodes draining the lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — coal workers' pneumoconiosis and other dust diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q32_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old patient develops pulmonary edema. The physician explains that the lymphatic system plays a protective role. Which function is performed by the pulmonary lymphatic system to help prevent pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Increasing hydrostatic pressure in pulmonary capillaries","B":"Removing plasma protein from the lung interstitium","C":"Elevating colloid osmotic pressure of interstitial fluid","D":"Constricting pulmonary arterioles to decrease capillary flow","E":"Secreting surfactant to reduce alveolar surface tension"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pulmonary lymphatic system removes plasma proteins that leak from lung capillaries back to circulation, thereby helping prevent pulmonary edema. By removing these proteins, lymphatics help maintain a favorable osmotic gradient that opposes fluid accumulation in lung tissues. Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar epithelial cells, not by lymphatics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lymphatics remove the plasma protein that leaks from lung capillaries, keeping interstitial colloid osmotic pressure in check and helping prevent pulmonary edema; they also pump away the small continual filtrate. COMLEX: Any factor that impedes pulmonary lymphatic function and lets interstitial pressure turn positive tends to flood the interstitium and alveoli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — major categories of pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q33_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Role of the pulmonary lymphatic system","batch":1,"stem":"During a histology lab, a student examines lung tissue from a retired coal miner and notes dust-laden cells in the connective tissue around the terminal bronchioles. Which statement correctly describes how particulate matter that reaches the alveoli is partly removed?","choices":{"A":"Particles are directly absorbed into alveolar capillaries","B":"Particles are swept out by cilia lining the alveolar walls","C":"Particles are carried away by lymph vessels in the interstitium","D":"Particles are phagocytosed by pneumocytes and secreted","E":"Particles are transported across pleural membrane to pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lymph vessels in the connective tissue spaces around the terminal bronchioles drain the lung interstitium, and particulate matter entering the alveoli is partly removed by way of these channels (Guyton Ch 39); macrophages that phagocytose particles in the alveoli migrate into the interstitium and enter the lymphatics. Alveolar walls have no cilia — mucociliary clearance works only in the conducting airways. Particles are not absorbed directly into alveolar capillaries, pneumocytes do not phagocytose and secrete them, and they are not transported across the pleura.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Particulate matter reaching the alveoli is partly removed by lymph vessels in the interstitium, which begin around the terminal bronchioles. COMLEX: Guyton notes coal miners often develop terminal bronchiolar disease from settled 1–5 µm dust particles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — coal workers' pneumoconiosis and other pneumoconioses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q34_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"During right heart catheterization, pullback tracings show a right ventricular pressure of 25/0–1 mm Hg and a pulmonary arterial pressure of 25/8 mm Hg. Systolic pressures are essentially equal in the two chambers. Why does pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure stay near 8 mm Hg while right ventricular diastolic pressure falls to near zero?","choices":{"A":"Tricuspid closure seals the artery off from the ventricle","B":"Pulmonic closure, then slow arterial runoff through lungs","C":"Left atrial pressure is transmitted back into the artery","D":"Hypoxic vasoconstriction holds arterial tone in diastole","E":"The pulmonary artery wall is stiffer than the aortic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton explains that during systole pulmonary arterial pressure is essentially equal to right ventricular pressure, but after the pulmonary valve closes at the end of systole the ventricular pressure falls precipitously while pulmonary arterial pressure falls more slowly as blood flows on through the lungs, leaving a diastolic pressure of about 8 mm Hg against a ventricular diastolic pressure of about 0 to 1 mm Hg. The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and ventricle, not between the ventricle and the artery. Left atrial pressure averages only about 2 mm Hg, too low to hold the artery at 8 mm Hg. Hypoxic vasoconstriction is a local response to low alveolar PO₂, not the cause of normal diastolic pressure. The pulmonary artery wall is about one-third as thick as the aorta and highly distensible, not stiffer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In systole pulmonary arterial pressure equals right ventricular pressure, but once the pulmonary valve closes, ventricular pressure falls almost to zero while arterial pressure falls slowly as blood runs off through the lungs, leaving a diastolic pressure of about 8 mm Hg. COMLEX: The same arterial runoff explains why heart-level diastolic pressure (8 mm Hg) cannot lift blood 15 mm Hg to the apex, producing apical zone 2 flow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 11 — pulmonary capillary pressure and its significance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q35_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"During a cardiology rotation, a student learns about pulmonary capillary pressure. What is the approximate mean pressure in the pulmonary capillaries?","choices":{"A":"2 mm Hg","B":"7 mm Hg","C":"15 mm Hg","D":"25 mm Hg","E":"40 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mean pulmonary capillary pressure is approximately 7 mm Hg, lying between the mean pulmonary arterial pressure of about 15 mm Hg and the left atrial pressure of about 2 mm Hg. This low capillary pressure is crucial for preventing pulmonary edema because it minimizes fluid filtration out of capillaries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mean pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mm Hg, much lower than the 17–25 mm Hg functional capillary pressure in many peripheral tissues. COMLEX: Capillary pressure must rise to about plasma colloid osmotic pressure (about 28 mm Hg) before significant edema forms, an acute safety factor of about 21 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — interstitial fluid dynamics and pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q36_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Pressure values — RV, pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries, left atrium","batch":1,"stem":"A physician measures a patient's pulmonary wedge pressure to estimate left atrial pressure. The wedge pressure is approximately 5 mm Hg. What is the approximate actual left atrial pressure?","choices":{"A":"Equal to wedge pressure at 5 mm Hg","B":"About 5 to 7 mm Hg below wedge pressure","C":"About 2 to 3 mm Hg below wedge pressure","D":"About 10 mm Hg below wedge pressure","E":"About 10 mm Hg above wedge pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pulmonary wedge pressure is measured by inserting a catheter through a peripheral vein into the right atrium, through the pulmonary artery, into a small branch until it wedges tightly. This measured wedge pressure is usually only 2 to 3 mm Hg higher than the actual left atrial pressure because of direct connection between small pulmonary arteries and pulmonary capillaries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A catheter wedged in a small pulmonary artery branch stops flow, so the measured wedge pressure (about 5 mm Hg) reflects the capillaries and is usually only 2–3 mm Hg above left atrial pressure. COMLEX: Wedge pressure therefore tracks changes in pulmonary capillary and left atrial pressure in congestive heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 10 — changes in the pulmonary circulation as left atrial pressure rises","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q37_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A segment of lung behind a partially obstructed bronchus is poorly ventilated, and its alveolar PO₂ falls below about 70% of normal. The small arteries and arterioles next to these alveoli constrict and divert blood to better-ventilated regions. Which event in the pulmonary vascular smooth muscle is a proposed mechanism for this response?","choices":{"A":"Opening of K⁺ channels that hyperpolarize the muscle cells","B":"Increased release of nitric oxide from the lung tissue","C":"Closure of O₂-sensitive K⁺ channels, depolarizing the cell","D":"β₂-receptor activation that raises smooth-muscle cAMP","E":"Local adenosine release that relaxes the arteriolar wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton notes that the mechanisms of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction are not completely understood, but low O₂ may inhibit oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in the pulmonary vascular smooth muscle; the resulting depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and calcium influx constricts the small arteries and arterioles. Low O₂ may also increase release of, or sensitivity to, endothelin and reactive oxygen species and decrease release of nitric oxide. The response begins when alveolar PO₂ falls below about 70% of normal (under 73 mm Hg). Opening K⁺ channels would hyperpolarize the cell and relax it. Increased nitric oxide release is the opposite of the proposed change. β₂-receptor activation and adenosine release relax vascular smooth muscle, the dilator response that hypoxia produces in systemic vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoxic vasoconstriction may come from low O2 inhibiting O2-sensitive K+ channels in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle; depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and Ca2+ influx constricts small arteries and arterioles. Low O2 may also raise endothelin or reactive oxygen species and lower nitric oxide release. COMLEX: Pulmonary hypertension drugs work on these pathways: endothelin receptor antagonists (bosentan), PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil) and prostacyclin analogs (First Aid).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — nitric oxide and medications for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q38_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is studying the compliance of pulmonary vessels. The pulmonary arterial tree has a compliance of approximately 7 mL/mm Hg. This compliance is significantly different from that of the systemic arterial tree. Which structural feature primarily explains the high compliance of pulmonary vessels?","choices":{"A":"Thick muscular walls with extensive smooth muscle layers","B":"Narrow lumens with high resistance to blood flow","C":"Short lengths with minimal branching patterns","D":"Large diameters, thin walls, and high distensibility","E":"Rigid walls that maintain constant vascular pressure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The large compliance of pulmonary circulation (about 7 mL/mm Hg) is explained by structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels: they have large diameters relative to systemic vessels, thin walls (one-third thickness), and are highly distensible. These features allow pulmonary vessels to accommodate large increases in blood volume with minimal pressure increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High pulmonary vascular compliance (almost 7 mL/mm Hg) comes from larger diameters than systemic vessels, walls one-third as thick, and distensibility; under most conditions the vessels act as distensible tubes that widen with rising pressure. COMLEX: This lets lung blood volume swing from half to twice normal, absorbing a shift of blood in left heart failure before pressures rise steeply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — pulmonary blood flow during heavy exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q39_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Structural characteristics of pulmonary vessels vs. systemic arteries","batch":1,"stem":"A cardiovascular physiologist notes that pulmonary arterioles have larger diameters than their systemic counterparts. What is the functional significance of this anatomical difference?","choices":{"A":"It increases pulmonary vascular resistance to flow","B":"It prevents blood flow from occurring during diastole","C":"It increases pulmonary arterial pressure at rest","D":"It limits cardiac output capacity during exercise","E":"It allows greater blood flow with low pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The larger diameter of pulmonary arterioles compared to systemic arterioles allows much lower resistance to blood flow, enabling entire cardiac output to flow through lungs with minimal pressure drop. This is essential for efficient gas exchange and allows pulmonary circulation to accommodate large increases in cardiac output during exercise without substantial increases in pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Larger-diameter pulmonary arterioles and arteries give low resistance (resistance falls with the fourth power of radius), so the whole cardiac output passes through the lungs with a mean driving pressure of only about 13 mm Hg (15 − 2). COMLEX: First Aid's resistance formula, R = 8ηl/πr⁴, shows why small changes in vessel radius in pulmonary vascular disease raise resistance sharply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — mechanisms of pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q40_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"A student learns that bronchial arteries originate from the systemic circulation. What is the oxygenation status of blood in the bronchial arteries?","choices":{"A":"Fully deoxygenated similar to pulmonary arterial blood","B":"Partially oxygenated with approximately 50% saturation","C":"Fully oxygenated similar to systemic arterial blood","D":"Mixed venous blood with highly variable saturation","E":"Completely deoxygenated with zero oxygen content"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bronchial arteries carry fully oxygenated blood because they originate from systemic circulation (branches of thoracic aorta). This oxygenated blood supplies metabolic needs of lung's supporting structures including connective tissue, septa, and bronchi. In contrast, pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood for gas exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial arteries carry fully oxygenated systemic blood (they arise from the thoracic aorta), unlike the partially deoxygenated blood of the pulmonary arteries. COMLEX: Moore notes the single right bronchial artery often arises from a posterior intercostal artery rather than directly from the aorta, which matters when planning bronchial artery embolization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — partial pressure gradients for O2 and CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q41_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with long-standing bronchiectasis coughs up about 300 mL of bright red blood over 2 hours. Bronchial artery embolization is planned. Which vessels are the usual source of bleeding in hemoptysis, and at what pressure do they carry blood?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary arteries, at systemic arterial pressure","B":"Pulmonary veins, at left atrial pressure","C":"Bronchial arteries, at nearly aortic pressure","D":"Pulmonary capillaries, at about 7 mm Hg","E":"Bronchial veins, at azygos venous pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Moore notes that in about 95% of cases of hemoptysis the bleeding comes from branches of the bronchial arteries, with bronchiectasis among the common causes. Guyton describes the bronchial arteries as branches of the thoracic aorta that deliver blood at a pressure only slightly lower than aortic pressure, which is why their bleeding is brisk and why embolizing them controls it. The pulmonary arteries carry blood at about 25/8 mm Hg, not systemic pressure, and are not the usual source. The pulmonary veins run at left atrial pressure, about 2 mm Hg, and the pulmonary capillaries at about 7 mm Hg; both are low-pressure beds. The bronchial veins drain only the proximal root of the lung into the azygos system and are not the usual source of hemoptysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Moore traces about 95% of hemoptysis to branches of the bronchial arteries, which Guyton describes as thoracic aortic branches delivering blood at a pressure only slightly below aortic pressure. COMLEX: Moore lists bronchitis, lung cancer, pneumonia, bronchiectasis, pulmonary embolism and tuberculosis as the commonest causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis: pathogenesis, morphology and clinical features","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q42_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Bronchial vessel role in oxygenation — systemic origin, drainage pathway","batch":1,"stem":"A histology student traces the finest branches of the bronchial arteries in a lung section and finds that they open into vessels of the pulmonary circulation. At about what level along the airway tree do the bronchial and pulmonary circulations anastomose?","choices":{"A":"At the hilum, within the walls of the main bronchi","B":"In the tracheal wall just above the carina","C":"In the interlobular septa around the lobar bronchi","D":"Where conducting airways meet respiratory airways","E":"In the parietal pleura lining the chest wall"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pawlina states that the finest branches of the bronchial arterial tree open into the pulmonary capillaries, so the two circulations anastomose at about the junction between the conducting and respiratory passages; Moore adds that the bronchial arteries supply the airways as far distally as the respiratory bronchioles and anastomose with pulmonary artery branches in the walls of the bronchioles and in the visceral pleura. The main bronchi at the hilum, the tracheal wall above the carina, and the tissue around the lobar bronchi are proximal conducting structures supplied by the bronchial arteries without joining the pulmonary bed. The parietal pleura is supplied by the arteries of the thoracic wall, not by the bronchial arteries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pawlina says the finest bronchial arterial branches open into pulmonary capillaries, so the two circulations anastomose at about the junction of the conducting and respiratory passages; bronchial veins drain only the hilar connective tissue. COMLEX: Robbins notes this bronchial supply sustains the lung parenchyma after a pulmonary embolus in a patient with adequate cardiovascular function, so hemorrhage may occur without infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — bronchioles at the conducting–respiratory junction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q43_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"A physiologist measures pulmonary blood flow at different vertical levels in an upright person at rest. At the apex of the lung, the blood flow is very low. What is the primary explanation for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar air pressure exceeds capillary pressure during diastole","B":"Pulmonary arterial pressure is maximally elevated at apex","C":"Cardiac output is insufficient to perfuse apical regions","D":"Bronchial circulation dominates flow in apical regions","E":"Left atrial pressure is abnormally elevated causing backup"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a normal upright lung the apex is zone 2. Apical arterial pressure is about 15 mm Hg lower than at heart level, so systolic pressure still exceeds alveolar air pressure (0) but diastolic pressure does not; capillaries open only in systole, and apical flow is low and intermittent (Guyton Ch 39: normal lungs have only zones 2 and 3). Zone 1 (no flow at any time) appears only in abnormal states such as severe blood loss or breathing against high positive airway pressure. Pulmonary arterial pressure is lowest, not highest, at the apex; cardiac output is not the limiting factor; bronchial flow is a small nutritive supply everywhere; and left atrial pressure is normal in this person.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the upright lung the apex is zone 2: apical systolic pressure (about 25 − 15 = 10 mm Hg) exceeds alveolar pressure, but diastolic pressure (8 mm Hg at heart level) cannot overcome the 15 mm Hg hydrostatic gradient, so apical flow stops in diastole. COMLEX: First Aid notes that in exercise, rising cardiac output dilates apical capillaries and apical V/Q approaches 1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — V/Q ratio and alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q44_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"In an upright person at rest, the upper region of the lung (the apex) experiences intermittent blood flow. During which phase of the cardiac cycle does blood flow occur in this zone?","choices":{"A":"Throughout both systole and diastole continuously","B":"Only during systole when arterial pressure peaks","C":"Only during diastole when arterial pressure falls","D":"Only during the isovolumetric contraction phase briefly","E":"Blood flow never occurs in this upper region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In an upright person the upper region of the lung (the apex) is zone 2, where flow is intermittent (Guyton Ch 39: zone 2 begins about 10 cm above the midlevel of the heart and extends to the top of the lungs). During systole, arterial pressure rises high enough to exceed alveolar air pressure, so blood flows; during diastole arterial pressure falls below alveolar pressure and flow stops. Continuous flow in both phases is zone 3 (the lower lung), no flow at all is zone 1, and flow is not confined to diastole or to the brief isovolumetric phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zone 2 flow is intermittent: capillaries open in systole, when arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure, and close in diastole, when it does not; in normal lungs this begins about 10 cm above mid-heart and extends to the apex. COMLEX: First Aid gives apical V/Q as about 3 (wasted ventilation), which is why the apex contributes most of the alveolar dead space in a healthy lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q45_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Hydrostatic pressure gradients → pulmonary blood flow; concept of lung zones 1/2/3","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is lying supine in bed. How does body position affect the distribution of pulmonary blood flow zones?","choices":{"A":"Zone 1 becomes more extensive throughout all lungs","B":"Zone 2 becomes the dominant pattern in all regions","C":"Blood flow ceases completely in posterior lung regions","D":"Hydrostatic effects are amplified versus upright position","E":"Zone 3 blood flow pattern occurs in most lungs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When a person is lying down, no part of lung is more than a few centimeters above heart level. In this position, blood flow in a normal person is entirely Zone 3 blood flow, with continuous flow throughout cardiac cycle because pulmonary arterial pressure remains greater than alveolar air pressure at all times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lying down, no part of the lung is more than a few centimeters above the heart, so in a normal person the whole lung, apices included, has continuous zone 3 flow. COMLEX: First Aid notes aspiration pneumonia favors the right upper lobe in a recumbent patient and the right lower lobe when upright, because of bronchial anatomy and gravity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 2 — pneumonia classification, including aspiration pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q46_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about the three zones of pulmonary blood flow in an upright person. Which pressure relationship correctly defines Zone 3 blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar pressure > Arterial pressure > Venous pressure","B":"Arterial pressure > Alveolar pressure > Venous pressure","C":"Venous pressure > Arterial pressure > Alveolar pressure","D":"Arterial pressure > Venous pressure > Alveolar pressure","E":"Alveolar pressure > Venous pressure > Arterial pressure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zone 3 blood flow, which occurs in lower regions of lung in upright person, is characterized by pressure relationship: PA > PV > PALV (arterial pressure > venous pressure > alveolar pressure). In this zone, both arterial and venous pressures remain greater than alveolar pressure throughout cardiac cycle, resulting in continuous blood flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zone 3 is defined by arterial > venous > alveolar pressure, so capillaries stay open through the whole cardiac cycle; this covers the lung from about 10 cm above heart level to the base. COMLEX: First Aid notes the base, with the most perfusion, has the lowest V/Q (about 0.6, wasted perfusion).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — V/Q ratio and its effect on alveolar gases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q47_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"During heavy exercise, a person's pulmonary blood flow increases substantially. What happens to the distribution of blood flow zones in the lungs?","choices":{"A":"Zone 1 regions expand significantly throughout all lungs","B":"Zone 2 becomes the dominant pattern in all regions","C":"All zones remain in the same distribution as rest","D":"Zone 1 develops in lung bases from increased flow","E":"Zone 3 pattern extends upward, reducing Zone 2"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During heavy exercise, pulmonary vascular pressures rise enough to convert lung apices from Zone 2 pattern (intermittent flow) into Zone 3 pattern (continuous flow). This occurs because increased cardiac output and pulmonary arterial pressure cause pulmonary arterial pressure to exceed alveolar air pressure even at lung apices, making blood flow more uniform. The zones therefore do not stay in their resting distribution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In exercise pulmonary vascular pressures rise enough to convert the apices from zone 2 to zone 3, so blood flow increases in all parts of the lung. COMLEX: Extra flow is met mainly by recruiting and distending capillaries, keeping pulmonary artery pressure nearly unchanged and protecting against edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — pulmonary blood flow during heavy exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl9_ex1_q48_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological differences in pressures and blood flow among zones 1/2/3 (upright)","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with severe blood loss develops hypotension. The physician is concerned about Zone 1 blood flow developing in the lungs. At the apex of the lung in an upright position, which condition would most likely create Zone 1 physiology?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary systolic arterial pressure exceeding 25 mm Hg","B":"Left atrial pressure rising above 15 mm Hg significantly","C":"Alveolar air pressure decreasing below 5 mm Hg level","D":"Pulmonary capillary pressure rising above 30 mm Hg","E":"Heart-level pulmonary systolic pressure below about 10 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Zone 1 (no flow) occurs when apical capillary pressure never exceeds alveolar air pressure, which is about 0 mm Hg. Apical arterial pressure is about 15 mm Hg below heart-level pressure, so if heart-level pulmonary systolic pressure falls to about 10 mm Hg after severe hemorrhage, apical systolic pressure is below 0 and the apical capillaries never open (Guyton Ch 39: zone 1 appears when pulmonary systolic pressure is exceedingly low, as might occur after severe blood loss). A higher systolic arterial pressure, a raised left atrial pressure, or a raised capillary pressure all favor flow, and a fall in alveolar pressure would also favor flow rather than prevent it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zone 1 appears at the upright apex when apical capillary pressure can never exceed alveolar pressure (0), as when pulmonary systolic pressure is exceedingly low after severe blood loss (apical pressure is about 15 mm Hg below heart level). COMLEX: Guyton notes the lungs also act as a reservoir in hemorrhage, shifting blood into the systemic vessels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space and how it arises","isHighYield":false}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q01_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old athlete exercises vigorously on a treadmill, increasing cardiac output from 5 L/min to 25 L/min. During this 5-fold increase in cardiac output, what happens to pulmonary arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"It increases by 10-fold to accommodate the flow","B":"It decreases due to vasodilation in exercising muscles","C":"It remains nearly unchanged despite increased flow","D":"It increases proportionally by 5-fold to match flow","E":"It fluctuates wildly between systole and diastole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During heavy exercise, blood flow through the lungs may increase 4-fold to 7-fold, yet pulmonary arterial pressure rises very little, even during maximum exercise. This occurs because the pulmonary circulation accommodates increased flow through three mechanisms: increasing the number of open capillaries (up to 3-fold), distending all capillaries and increasing flow rate through each (more than 2-fold), and increasing pulmonary arterial pressure slightly. The first two mechanisms decrease pulmonary vascular resistance so much that pulmonary arterial pressure rises minimally. Nothing in this response makes the pressure swing wildly; normal pulmonary arterial pressure is about 25/8 mm Hg with a mean of 15 mm Hg, and it rises only slightly even at maximum exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In heavy exercise lung blood flow can rise 4- to 7-fold, yet mean pulmonary artery pressure rises very little because recruiting and distending capillaries lowers pulmonary vascular resistance. COMLEX: First Aid's PVR = (pulmonary artery − left atrial pressure)/cardiac output shows the arithmetic: a large rise in flow with an almost fixed gradient means resistance has fallen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — thin-walled, large-diameter pulmonary vessels and their high compliance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q02_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student is studying how the pulmonary circulation accommodates increased cardiac output during exercise. Which mechanism is NOT used by the pulmonary circulation to accommodate this increased blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Increasing the number of open capillaries up to 3-fold","B":"Distending all capillaries and increasing flow through each","C":"Constricting pulmonary arterioles to increase pressure","D":"Slightly increasing pulmonary arterial pressure overall","E":"Decreasing pulmonary vascular resistance substantially"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pulmonary circulation accommodates increased blood flow during exercise through three main mechanisms: opening previously closed capillaries (up to 3-fold increase), distending all capillaries and increasing flow rate through each (more than 2-fold), and slightly increasing pulmonary arterial pressure. These mechanisms work together to decrease, not increase, pulmonary vascular resistance. Constricting pulmonary arterioles would increase resistance and pressure, which is counterproductive. The pulmonary circulation is designed to accommodate large flow increases with minimal pressure elevation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extra exercise flow is handled by opening more capillaries (up to 3-fold), distending all capillaries so flow through each rises more than 2-fold, and only then a small rise in pulmonary artery pressure; arteriolar constriction is not part of it. COMLEX: Constriction of pulmonary vessels is instead the response to low alveolar PO2, which diverts blood away from poorly ventilated regions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — structure of pulmonary vessels and hypoxic vasoconstriction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q03_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"During maximum exercise, a healthy person's pulmonary blood flow increases from 5 L/min at rest to 30 L/min. By approximately how much can the number of open pulmonary capillaries increase to accommodate this flow?","choices":{"A":"No increase in open capillaries occurs","B":"Approximately 50% increase in open capillaries","C":"Approximately 2-fold increase in open capillaries","D":"Approximately 3-fold increase in open capillaries","E":"Approximately 10-fold increase in open capillaries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During heavy exercise, the number of open capillaries can increase by as much as 3-fold. Many capillaries are normally collapsed at rest and open up when pulmonary arterial pressure increases even slightly during exercise. This recruitment of previously closed capillaries is one of the three major mechanisms by which the pulmonary circulation accommodates increased cardiac output without large increases in pulmonary arterial pressure. The other mechanisms are distension of all capillaries and slight increases in pulmonary arterial pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Capillary recruitment can increase the number of open pulmonary capillaries as much as 3-fold in heavy exercise, while distension raises flow through each more than 2-fold. COMLEX: Recruitment also expands the surface for gas exchange, which First Aid's diffusion equation (rate ∝ area × gradient / thickness) shows raises O2 uptake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q04_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with mitral stenosis develops increased left atrial pressure that rises from 10 mm Hg to 20 mm Hg. What is the effect on pulmonary circulatory function?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary arterial pressure decreases proportionally","B":"Pulmonary venous pressure remains completely unchanged","C":"Pulmonary arterial pressure increases by similar amount","D":"Right ventricular pressure decreases substantially","E":"Bronchial circulation pressure increases dramatically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once left atrial pressure is above 7 or 8 mm Hg, further increases in left atrial pressure cause almost equally great increases in pulmonary arterial pressure, which adds a load to the right heart (Guyton Ch 39). Rises up to about 7 mm Hg are absorbed by distension of the pulmonary venules and opening of more capillaries and have little effect. Here left atrial pressure rises 10 mm Hg, entirely above the threshold, so pulmonary arterial pressure rises by about 10 mm Hg. Pulmonary arterial and right ventricular pressures rise rather than fall, pulmonary venous pressure rises with the left atrium, and the bronchial circulation is not the site of the change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Left atrial pressure rises up to about 7 mm Hg are absorbed by venular distension and capillary recruitment, but above 7–8 mm Hg each further rise raises pulmonary artery pressure almost equally, adding load to the right heart. COMLEX: First Aid classifies pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease (systolic or diastolic dysfunction, valvular disease) as group 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension causes and classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q05_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old patient with left-sided heart failure has a left atrial pressure of 25 mm Hg. At what left atrial pressure does pulmonary edema typically begin to develop?","choices":{"A":"When left atrial pressure exceeds 5 mm Hg","B":"When left atrial pressure exceeds 10 mm Hg","C":"When left atrial pressure exceeds 15 mm Hg","D":"When left atrial pressure exceeds 23 mm Hg","E":"When left atrial pressure exceeds 40 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulmonary edema typically begins when left atrial pressure rises above approximately 23 mm Hg: in Guyton's experiments this raised pulmonary capillary pressure above about 25 mm Hg, the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of those animals, and fluid then leaked into the interstitial spaces and eventually into the alveoli. The pulmonary capillary pressure must rise from the normal level of 7 mm Hg to more than 28 mm Hg to cause substantial pulmonary edema. This gives an acute safety factor against pulmonary edema of 21 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In Guyton's experiments fluid began to collect in the lungs once left atrial pressure exceeded about 23 mm Hg, which pushed capillary pressure above about 25 mm Hg, the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of those animals. COMLEX: Robbins lists left-sided heart failure as the common cause of hemodynamic (increased hydrostatic pressure) pulmonary edema, which produces heavy, wet lungs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — categories of pulmonary edema and their etiologies","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q06_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A physician monitors a patient's left atrial pressure, which has increased to 15 mm Hg. What is the expected effect on pulmonary capillary pressure?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary capillary pressure remains at baseline 7 mm Hg","B":"Pulmonary capillary pressure decreases below baseline","C":"Pulmonary capillary pressure increases by approximately 1 mm Hg","D":"Pulmonary capillary pressure increases to approximately 15 mm Hg","E":"Pulmonary capillary pressure increases to approximately 30 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normally mean pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mm Hg with a left atrial pressure of about 2 mm Hg. Rises in left atrial pressure up to about 7 mm Hg are absorbed by venular distension and capillary recruitment, but any increase above 7 or 8 mm Hg raises capillary pressure almost equally (Guyton Ch 39). A left atrial pressure of 15 mm Hg is about 7 mm Hg above the threshold, so capillary pressure rises from about 7 to about 14–15 mm Hg (at high left atrial pressures capillary pressure runs 1–2 mm Hg above left atrial pressure). It does not stay at baseline, fall, or rise by only 1 mm Hg, and 30 mm Hg would require a left atrial pressure near 28–30 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Once left atrial pressure is above about 7–8 mm Hg, pulmonary capillary pressure rises almost as much as left atrial pressure does, running 1–2 mm Hg above it at high values. COMLEX: That close coupling is why pulmonary wedge pressure, normally only 2–3 mm Hg above left atrial pressure, is used to follow capillary pressure in heart failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — pressure values across the pulmonary circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q07_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A cardiology fellow is learning about pulmonary interstitial pressure. What is the approximate normal interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure in the lungs?","choices":{"A":"Positive pressure of +5 mm Hg","B":"Zero pressure equal to atmospheric","C":"Positive pressure of +2 mm Hg","D":"Negative pressure of -20 mm Hg","E":"Negative pressure of -8 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The interstitial fluid pressure in the lung is slightly more negative than that in peripheral subcutaneous tissue. This pressure has been measured in two ways: by a micropipette inserted into the pulmonary interstitium, giving a value of about -5 mm Hg, and by measuring the absorption pressure of fluid from the alveoli, giving a value of about -8 mm Hg. This negative pressure helps pull the lungs against the chest wall and contributes to keeping the alveoli relatively dry by opposing fluid filtration from the capillaries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lung interstitial fluid pressure is slightly more negative than in subcutaneous tissue: about −5 mm Hg by micropipette and about −8 mm Hg by alveolar fluid absorption pressure. COMLEX: This suction, maintained by the capillaries and lymphatics, pulls any fluid that appears in the alveoli back into the interstitium so the alveoli stay \"dry.\"","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — pulmonary lymphatics and fluid removal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q08_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student asks why low pulmonary capillary pressure is important for lung function. What is the primary significance of the normally low pulmonary capillary pressure?","choices":{"A":"It prevents fluid filtration into lung interstitium","B":"It maximizes oxygen diffusion across alveolar membranes","C":"It increases blood transit time through capillaries","D":"It enhances carbon dioxide removal from blood","E":"It maintains high pulmonary vascular resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The low pulmonary capillary pressure (approximately 7 mm Hg) is crucial for preventing pulmonary edema by minimizing fluid filtration out of the capillaries into the pulmonary interstitium. This low pressure, combined with negative interstitial pressure and lymphatic drainage, normally keeps the alveoli \"dry\" except for a small amount of fluid that seeps from the epithelium to keep alveolar surfaces moist. If capillary pressure rises significantly above the plasma colloid osmotic pressure, pulmonary edema develops. CO2 unloading depends on the PCO2 difference across the membrane and CO2's high diffusion coefficient, not on capillary hydrostatic pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A mean pulmonary capillary pressure of about 7 mm Hg keeps outward filtration small: the outward forces (29 mm Hg) barely exceed the inward plasma oncotic pressure (28 mm Hg), and lymphatics remove the slight net filtrate. COMLEX: First Aid's transudate causes for the pleural space follow the same Starling logic: raised hydrostatic pressure (heart failure) or reduced oncotic pressure (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 7 — disease states, such as pulmonary edema, that impair gas diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q09_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher is studying Starling forces in the pulmonary capillaries. The normal mean pulmonary capillary pressure of 7 mm Hg is significantly lower than what value in peripheral tissues?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral capillary pressure of 5 to 10 mm Hg","B":"Peripheral capillary pressure of 10 to 15 mm Hg","C":"Peripheral capillary pressure of 30 to 35 mm Hg","D":"Peripheral capillary pressure of 17 to 25 mm Hg","E":"Peripheral capillary pressure of 40 to 50 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pulmonary capillary pressure is low, about 7 mm Hg, in comparison with a considerably higher functional capillary pressure in peripheral tissues of about 17 to 25 mm Hg. This lower pressure in pulmonary capillaries is essential for preventing fluid accumulation in the lungs. The high compliance and low resistance of the pulmonary circulation allow for this low capillary pressure despite receiving the entire cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary capillary pressure (about 7 mm Hg) is far lower than the 17–25 mm Hg functional capillary pressure of many peripheral tissues, while lung interstitial colloid osmotic pressure (about 14 mm Hg) is more than double that of peripheral tissues. COMLEX: Because the alveolar epithelium tears with any positive interstitial pressure, keeping capillary pressure low is the lung's main protection against alveolar flooding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — pressures in the pulmonary circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q10_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"A pulmonary physiologist measures the time that blood spends in the pulmonary capillaries at rest. What is the approximate normal transit time for blood through the pulmonary capillaries?","choices":{"A":"0.1 seconds","B":"0.3 seconds","C":"2.0 seconds","D":"5.0 seconds","E":"0.8 seconds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When cardiac output is normal, blood passes through the pulmonary capillaries in about 0.8 second (Guyton Ch 39). This transit time can shorten to as little as 0.3 second when cardiac output increases during exercise. The 0.8-second transit time at rest is sufficient for complete oxygenation and carbon dioxide removal. The shortened time during exercise is still adequate because additional capillaries open up to accommodate increased flow, and the blood still becomes fully oxygenated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At normal cardiac output blood spends about 0.8 second in the pulmonary capillaries, shortening to as little as 0.3 second with high output. COMLEX: Guyton (Ch 41) adds that resting blood is nearly saturated with O2 after about one-third of the capillary, a reserve that protects oxygenation when transit shortens.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 6 — capillary transit time and its effect on O2 and CO2 diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q11_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"During heavy exercise, a patient's cardiac output increases substantially. What happens to the transit time of blood through the pulmonary capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Transit time increases to 2 seconds for better oxygenation","B":"Transit time remains constant at 0.8 seconds always","C":"Transit time increases to 5 seconds to match demand","D":"Transit time becomes variable and unpredictable","E":"Transit time decreases to as little as 0.3 seconds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When cardiac output increases during heavy exercise, blood transit time through the pulmonary capillaries can shorten to as little as 0.3 second. Despite this shortened time, blood still becomes adequately oxygenated because additional capillaries open up and distend, and because the blood-gas barrier is extremely thin and efficient. This demonstrates the remarkable reserve capacity of the pulmonary circulation for gas exchange even under conditions of greatly increased blood flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With high cardiac output, pulmonary capillary transit time can fall from about 0.8 to as little as 0.3 second, and it would fall further if normally collapsed capillaries did not open to share the flow. COMLEX: First Aid notes O2 is perfusion-limited in health but diffusion-limited in emphysema or fibrosis, where diffusion problems cause hypoxia earlier than hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q12_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student asks how the shortened capillary transit time during exercise affects gas exchange efficiency. What compensatory mechanism allows adequate gas exchange despite reduced transit time?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar oxygen concentration increases dramatically","B":"Hemoglobin affinity for oxygen decreases substantially","C":"Additional capillaries open up to increase surface area","D":"Blood viscosity decreases to enhance diffusion","E":"Alveolar ventilation decreases to match perfusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Despite the shortened transit time during exercise (from about 0.8 seconds to as little as 0.3 seconds), gas exchange remains adequate because additional capillaries that are normally collapsed open up, greatly increasing the surface area available for gas exchange. Additionally, all capillaries distend, increasing the rate of flow through each capillary. These mechanisms, combined with the extreme thinness and efficiency of the alveolar-capillary membrane, ensure complete oxygenation even with the shortened contact time. Blood viscosity is not one of the factors that set gas diffusion through the respiratory membrane (thickness, surface area, diffusion coefficient, and partial pressure difference).","boardPrep":"Step 1: When flow rises, normally collapsed capillaries open, so the shortening of transit time is much smaller than it would be and the gas-exchange surface grows; blood is still oxygenated within a fraction of a second. COMLEX: In First Aid's diffusion equation area is in the numerator, which is why loss of capillary bed (as in emphysema) limits this reserve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — factors (surface area, thickness) affecting gas diffusion rate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q13_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"A physician is reviewing the Starling forces that govern fluid exchange in the pulmonary capillaries. According to the balance of forces, what is the net filtration pressure at the pulmonary capillary membrane?","choices":{"A":"+1 mm Hg favoring filtration out of capillaries","B":"-5 mm Hg favoring absorption into capillaries","C":"+10 mm Hg favoring filtration out of capillaries","D":"+20 mm Hg favoring filtration out of capillaries","E":"-10 mm Hg favoring absorption into capillaries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The balance of Starling forces at the pulmonary capillary membrane creates a mean filtration pressure of +1 mm Hg, which slightly favors filtration of fluid out of the capillaries. Forces favoring filtration total 29 mm Hg (capillary hydrostatic pressure 7 mm Hg + interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure 14 mm Hg + negative interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure 8 mm Hg). The inward force is plasma colloid osmotic pressure of 28 mm Hg. This yields a net outward force of +1 mm Hg. This slight continual filtration is pumped back to circulation through the pulmonary lymphatic system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Outward forces at the lung capillary are capillary pressure 7, interstitial colloid osmotic pressure 14 and negative interstitial pressure 8 (total 29 mm Hg) against plasma colloid osmotic pressure 28 mm Hg, a mean filtration pressure of +1 mm Hg. COMLEX: This small, continual filtrate is pumped back by pulmonary lymphatics, which the OMM course supports with thoracic inlet and lymphatic pump techniques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — the lymphatic system of the lungs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q14_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old patient with left-sided heart failure develops pulmonary edema. Experimental studies show that pulmonary capillary pressure must rise to exceed what critical value before significant pulmonary edema develops?","choices":{"A":"The plasma colloid osmotic pressure value","B":"The interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure value","C":"The alveolar air pressure value","D":"The pulmonary arterial pressure value","E":"The right ventricular systolic pressure value"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pulmonary capillary pressure normally must rise to a value at least equal to the colloid osmotic pressure of the plasma (approximately 28 mm Hg) before significant pulmonary edema will occur. When left atrial pressure rises above 23 mm Hg, causing pulmonary capillary pressure to rise above 25 mm Hg, fluid begins to accumulate in the lungs. As soon as left atrial pressure rises above this critical level, fluid accumulates even more rapidly with further increases in capillary pressure because the plasma colloid osmotic pressure during these experiments was equal to this 25 mm Hg critical level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary capillary pressure must rise to at least plasma colloid osmotic pressure before significant edema forms; with a normal plasma value of 28 mm Hg and capillary pressure of 7 mm Hg, the acute safety factor is about 21 mm Hg. COMLEX: Robbins lists low oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminemia, nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, protein-losing enteropathy) as a less common cause of hemodynamic edema, because it lowers this threshold.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — categories and causes of pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q15_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with acute pulmonary edema. The physician explains that two primary mechanisms can cause pulmonary edema. Which of the following is a correct cause of pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Decreased capillary permeability from infections","B":"Left-sided heart failure increasing capillary pressure","C":"Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure","D":"Excessive lymphatic drainage from lung tissue","E":"Decreased alveolar surface tension from surfactant"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most common causes of pulmonary edema are: (1) left-sided heart failure or mitral valve disease, with consequent great increases in pulmonary venous pressure and pulmonary capillary pressure, causing flooding of interstitial spaces and alveoli; and (2) damage to pulmonary blood capillary membranes caused by infections such as pneumonia or by breathing noxious substances such as chlorine gas or sulfur dioxide gas. Each of these mechanisms causes rapid leakage of plasma proteins and fluid into interstitial spaces and alveoli. Lymphatic drainage protects against edema by carrying away leaked fluid and protein, so more of it would not cause edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's two most common causes of pulmonary edema are left heart failure or mitral valve disease (high venous and capillary pressure) and capillary membrane damage from infection such as pneumonia or noxious gases such as chlorine or sulfur dioxide. COMLEX: Robbins makes the same split: hemodynamic (cardiogenic) edema vs. edema from alveolar wall (microvascular or epithelial) injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — ARDS, the most severe form of microvascular-injury edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q16_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student is learning about pleural pressure. What is the normal pressure within the pleural fluid space?","choices":{"A":"Positive pressure of approximately +5 mm Hg","B":"Zero pressure equal to atmospheric pressure","C":"Positive pressure of approximately +10 mm Hg","D":"Negative pressure of approximately -20 to -25 mm Hg","E":"Negative pressure of approximately -4 to -7 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A negative force is always required on the outside of the lungs to keep them expanded. This force is provided by negative pressure in the pleural space. Because the normal collapse tendency of the lungs is about -4 mm Hg, the pleural fluid pressure must always be at least as negative as -4 mm Hg to keep the lungs expanded. Actual measurements have shown that the pressure is usually about -7 mm Hg, which is a few millimeters of mercury more negative than the collapse pressure of the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because the lung's collapse tendency is about −4 mm Hg, pleural fluid pressure must be at least that negative; measured values are usually about −7 mm Hg, a few mm Hg beyond the collapse pressure. COMLEX: Guyton's Ch 38 gives pleural pressure in cm H2O instead (about −5 at the start of inspiration to −7.5 at its end), so check units before comparing numbers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — pleural and alveolar pressures that drive airflow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q17_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A physician asks a student to explain why negative pleural pressure is necessary. What is the primary function of the negative pressure in the pleural space?","choices":{"A":"It keeps the normal lungs pulled against the chest wall","B":"It facilitates gas exchange across alveolar membranes","C":"It increases pulmonary vascular resistance","D":"It enhances lymphatic drainage from lung tissue","E":"It maintains proper bronchiole diameter during breathing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The negativity of the pleural fluid pressure keeps the normal lungs pulled against the parietal pleura of the chest cavity, except for an extremely thin layer of mucoid fluid that acts as a lubricant to allow the lungs to slide back and forth within the pleural cavity during breathing. The basic cause of the negative pressure is pumping of fluid from the space by the lymphatics, which also provides the basis of negative pressure found in other tissue spaces of the body. Pleural pressure does not drive gas exchange, which depends on diffusion down partial pressure gradients across the respiratory membrane, and its role is mechanical, not setting pulmonary vascular resistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Negative pleural fluid pressure keeps the lungs pulled against the parietal pleura, leaving only a thin layer of mucoid fluid that lets them slide; it is generated by lymphatics pumping fluid out of the pleural space. COMLEX: First Aid explains why thoracentesis needles go above the rib: the intercostal neurovascular bundle runs below each rib.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — pleural effusions and their causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q18_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops a pneumothorax, and air enters the pleural space. What happens to the lung when pleural pressure becomes equal to atmospheric pressure?","choices":{"A":"The lung expands maximally to fill pleural cavity","B":"The lung collapses completely due to elastic recoil","C":"The lung maintains normal size and function","D":"The lung increases perfusion to compensate","E":"The lung doubles in size from pressure equalization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax), the pleural pressure becomes equal to atmospheric pressure (zero), eliminating the negative pressure that normally keeps the lung expanded. The lung then collapses because of its natural elastic recoil tendency. The collapse tendency of the lungs is normally about -4 mm Hg, so when pleural pressure is no longer negative, nothing opposes this collapse force. This demonstrates the critical importance of maintaining negative pleural pressure for lung expansion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When air enters the pleural space, pleural pressure becomes atmospheric, nothing opposes the lung's elastic recoil, and the lung collapses. COMLEX: First Aid's findings for pneumothorax are hyperresonance, diminished breath sounds and reduced tactile fremitus on the affected side; a tension pneumothorax needs immediate needle decompression and a chest tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q19_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old patient develops a pleural effusion. The physician explains that pleural effusion is analogous to edema fluid in other tissues. Which of the following is a correct mechanism causing pleural effusion?","choices":{"A":"Increased lymphatic drainage from the pleural cavity","B":"Blockage of lymphatic drainage from pleural cavity","C":"Decreased capillary permeability in pleural membranes","D":"Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure levels","E":"Decreased hydrostatic pressure in pleural capillaries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pleural effusion is analogous to edema fluid in tissues and can be caused by the same mechanisms that cause edema in other tissues: (1) blockage of lymphatic drainage from the pleural cavity; (2) cardiac failure, which causes excessively high peripheral and pulmonary capillary pressures, leading to excessive transudation of fluid into the pleural cavity; (3) greatly reduced plasma colloid osmotic pressure, thus allowing excessive transudation of fluid; and (4) infection or any other cause of inflammation of the surfaces of the pleural cavity, which increases capillary permeability and allows rapid dumping of plasma proteins and fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton calls pleural effusion edema of the pleural cavity, with four causes: blocked lymphatic drainage, cardiac failure (high capillary pressures), greatly reduced plasma colloid osmotic pressure, and inflammation that makes capillaries leaky. COMLEX: First Aid's Light's criteria separate exudate from transudate: pleural/serum protein > 0.5, pleural/serum LDH > 0.6, or pleural LDH > two-thirds the upper limit of normal serum LDH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — transudates vs. exudates and Light's criteria","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q20_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with congestive heart failure develops bilateral pleural effusions. Which pathophysiological mechanism explains this development?","choices":{"A":"Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure reduces filtration","B":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure increases filtration","C":"Enhanced lymphatic drainage removes excess fluid","D":"Decreased capillary permeability prevents fluid accumulation","E":"Increased plasma protein concentration draws fluid inward"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cardiac failure causes excessively high peripheral and pulmonary capillary pressures, leading to excessive transudation of fluid into the pleural cavity. When right-sided or left-sided heart failure occurs, the increased venous and capillary pressures cause fluid to filter out of the capillaries at a rate that exceeds the lymphatic system's ability to remove it, resulting in pleural effusion. This is one of the most common causes of pleural effusion in clinical practice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In heart failure, high peripheral and pulmonary capillary pressures cause excessive transudation into the pleural cavity, giving a transudate. COMLEX: First Aid describes a transudate as clear, hypocellular fluid from raised hydrostatic pressure (heart failure, Na+ retention) or low oncotic pressure (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — pleural effusions: transudates, exudates and common causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q21_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with pleural effusion secondary to pneumonia. What is the mechanism by which infection causes pleural effusion?","choices":{"A":"Infection decreases capillary permeability preventing leakage","B":"Infection increases plasma colloid osmotic pressure","C":"Infection makes membranes leaky, allowing protein leakage","D":"Infection enhances lymphatic drainage capacity","E":"Infection reduces hydrostatic pressure in pleural vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infection or any other cause of inflammation of the surfaces of the pleural cavity increases permeability of the capillary membranes and allows rapid dumping of both plasma proteins and fluid into the pleural cavity. This increased permeability disrupts the normal Starling forces that regulate fluid balance, allowing proteins (which normally stay in the capillaries) to leak out. Once proteins accumulate in the pleural space, they create an osmotic force that draws even more fluid out of the capillaries, rapidly worsening the effusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Infection or any inflammation of the pleural surfaces raises capillary permeability, so plasma proteins and fluid dump rapidly into the pleural cavity, producing an exudate. COMLEX: First Aid notes exudates (cloudy, cellular) from infection such as pneumonia or tuberculosis often need drainage because of the risk of infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — complications of bacterial pneumonia, including empyema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q22_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old marathon runner increases her cardiac output from 6 L/min to 24 L/min during a race. The pulmonary circulation accommodates this increased flow through three primary mechanisms. Which of the following is NOT one of these mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Opening previously collapsed capillaries up to 3-fold","B":"Distending capillaries and increasing flow rate through each","C":"Increasing pulmonary vascular resistance substantially","D":"Slightly elevating pulmonary arterial pressure overall","E":"Recruiting additional capillary surface area for gas exchange"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pulmonary circulation accommodates increased blood flow during exercise by: (1) increasing the number of open capillaries up to 3-fold, (2) distending all capillaries and increasing flow rate through each more than 2-fold, and (3) slightly increasing pulmonary arterial pressure. These mechanisms work together to DECREASE, not increase, pulmonary vascular resistance. The first two changes decrease resistance so much that pulmonary arterial pressure rises very little even during maximum exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lungs take up exercise flow by recruiting capillaries (up to 3-fold), distending them (flow per capillary more than 2-fold) and a small rise in pulmonary artery pressure; together the first two lower pulmonary vascular resistance. COMLEX: First Aid notes the exercise rise in cardiac output dilates apical capillaries and brings apical V/Q toward 1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — zones 1, 2 and 3 and how exercise shifts them","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q23_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"During maximal exercise a healthy 24-year-old's pulmonary blood flow rises about 5-fold, yet her mean pulmonary arterial pressure rises only a few mm Hg because capillaries open and distend. According to Guyton, which pair of benefits does this ability to carry the extra flow without a large rise in pulmonary arterial pressure provide?","choices":{"A":"Saves right-heart energy and prevents lung edema","B":"Raises alveolar ventilation, lowers dead space","C":"Lengthens capillary transit and adds diffusion time","D":"Shifts the apex to zone 1 and diverts flow downward","E":"Lowers left atrial pressure and boosts LV filling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that the lungs' ability to accommodate greatly increased exercise blood flow without increasing pulmonary arterial pressure conserves the energy of the right side of the heart and prevents a major rise in pulmonary capillary pressure and the development of pulmonary edema. The flow is carried by opening more capillaries (up to about 3-fold) and distending all of them, which lowers resistance. Alveolar ventilation and dead space are set by breathing, not by pulmonary vascular resistance. Capillary transit time actually shortens in exercise, from about 0.8 to as little as 0.3 second. Exercise converts the apex from zone 2 to zone 3, increasing flow in all parts of the lung rather than creating zone 1. Left atrial pressure stays near its normal range in exercise, almost never above +6 mm Hg, and is not the benefit Guyton describes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton states that carrying a large rise in exercise flow without raising pulmonary artery pressure conserves right-heart energy and prevents a major rise in capillary pressure and pulmonary edema. COMLEX: A healthy person's left atrial pressure almost never exceeds +6 mm Hg even in the most strenuous exercise, so exercise alone does not raise capillary pressure toward the edema threshold.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — exercise and the rise in O2 demand","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q24_b2","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Pulmonary blood flow changes during heavy exercise","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old patient with pulmonary hypertension cannot recruit additional capillaries during exercise. How does this inability affect the patient's exercise capacity compared to a healthy individual?","choices":{"A":"Exercise capacity improves due to consistent blood flow patterns","B":"Exercise capacity remains unchanged from baseline resting values","C":"Exercise capacity decreases due to excessive pressure elevation","D":"Exercise capacity increases due to enhanced oxygen extraction","E":"Exercise capacity normalizes through compensatory mechanisms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In pulmonary hypertension, the ability to recruit additional capillaries and distend existing ones is impaired. During exercise, when cardiac output increases, the inability to decrease pulmonary vascular resistance through capillary recruitment causes pulmonary arterial pressure to rise excessively. This excessive pressure elevation increases right ventricular afterload, limits cardiac output augmentation, and severely restricts exercise capacity. Normal individuals accommodate increased flow with minimal pressure rise through capillary recruitment and distension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normally, recruitment and distension of capillaries lower pulmonary vascular resistance as flow rises, so pulmonary artery pressure barely changes; without that reserve, rising flow raises pressure and right ventricular load. COMLEX: First Aid notes pulmonary hypertension increases right ventricular pressure, leading to right ventricular hypertrophy and eventually right heart failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension: causes and mechanisms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q25_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with an acute anterior myocardial infarction develops acute left-sided heart failure, and his pulmonary capillary pressure climbs to about 50 mm Hg, far above his plasma colloid osmotic pressure of 28 mm Hg. How quickly can acute pulmonary edema at this level kill him?","choices":{"A":"Over several days of flooding","B":"In less than about half an hour","C":"After 2 weeks of sustained pressure","D":"Over 6 to 12 hours of leakage","E":"Never, as lymph flow rises 10-fold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton explains that when pulmonary capillary pressure rises even slightly above the safety-factor level, lethal pulmonary edema can occur within hours, or within 20 to 30 minutes if capillary pressure rises 25 to 30 mm Hg above that level; in acute left-sided heart failure, in which capillary pressure occasionally reaches 50 mm Hg, death may ensue in less than 30 minutes. Several days or 6 to 12 hours describes a much slower course than an acute rise of more than 20 mm Hg above the safety factor. Two weeks is the time over which chronic elevation lets lymph vessels expand, and that roughly 10-fold rise in lymphatic capacity protects chronic mitral stenosis, not an abrupt failure like this one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When capillary pressure rises 25–30 mm Hg above the safety-factor level, lethal pulmonary edema can develop within 20–30 minutes; in acute left heart failure, capillary pressure near 50 mm Hg can kill in under 30 minutes. COMLEX: First Aid's physical-exam table lists late inspiratory crackles and dullness over pulmonary edema, which is consolidated with fluid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — morphology of acute and chronic pulmonary congestion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q26_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student learns that left atrial pressure in a healthy person almost never rises above a certain value, even during strenuous exercise. What is this maximum normal left atrial pressure during exercise?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 2 mm Hg matching the resting value","B":"Approximately 15 mm Hg during most strenuous exercise","C":"Approximately 25 mm Hg during most strenuous exercise","D":"Approximately 40 mm Hg during most strenuous exercise","E":"Approximately 6 mm Hg during most strenuous exercise"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The left atrial pressure in a healthy person almost never rises above +6 mm Hg, even during the most strenuous exercise. These small changes in left atrial pressure have virtually no effect on pulmonary circulatory function because this merely expands the pulmonary venules and opens up more capillaries, allowing blood to continue flowing with almost equal ease from the pulmonary arteries. This demonstrates the remarkable reserve capacity of the normal pulmonary circulation. About 2 mm Hg is the usual resting left atrial pressure (normal range 1 to 5 mm Hg), so it is not the exercise ceiling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A healthy person's left atrial pressure almost never rises above +6 mm Hg even in the most strenuous exercise; such small changes just expand the venules and open more capillaries, with almost no effect on pulmonary circulatory function. COMLEX: In left heart failure, by contrast, left atrial pressure can climb from its normal 1–5 mm Hg to 40–50 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — normal pressure values in the pulmonary circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q27_b2","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Sequence of changes when left atrial pressure rises","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with worsening mitral stenosis has a left atrial pressure that has risen from 5 to 18 mm Hg. Her pulmonary arterial pressure has risen by nearly the same amount. Beyond the risk to her lungs, which chamber of her heart bears the new burden created by this change?","choices":{"A":"Left ventricle, from higher filling pressure","B":"Left atrium, from reduced pulmonary inflow","C":"Right atrium, from systemic venous pooling","D":"Right ventricle, from higher outflow pressure","E":"No chamber, since capillaries absorb the rise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton explains that once left atrial pressure rises above about 7 or 8 mm Hg, further increases cause almost equally great increases in pulmonary arterial pressure, thus causing a concomitant increased load on the right heart; the right ventricle must now eject against a higher pulmonary arterial pressure. In mitral stenosis the obstruction lies upstream of the left ventricle, so the left ventricle is not loaded by a higher filling pressure. The left atrium is distended by the pressure, but pulmonary inflow to it is not reduced. The right atrium is not the chamber facing the raised outflow pressure. Capillary opening and venular expansion absorb only the small rises up to about 7 mm Hg; above that, the rise is transmitted backward to the pulmonary artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Above a left atrial pressure of about 7–8 mm Hg, each further rise raises pulmonary artery pressure almost equally, so the right ventricle must pump against a higher afterload. COMLEX: First Aid lists valvular disease among group 2 (left heart disease) causes of pulmonary hypertension, which can lead to right ventricular hypertrophy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q28_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A histology student notes that the alveolar walls are packed with capillaries that in most places almost touch one another side by side. Guyton uses a particular phrase to describe how blood moves through the alveolar walls because of this arrangement. Which description does he use?","choices":{"A":"A series of parallel tubes","B":"A continuous sheet of flow","C":"A single capillary loop","D":"A portal system of flow","E":"A pulsatile jet of flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that the alveolar walls are lined with so many capillaries that, in most places, they almost touch one another side by side, so the capillary blood is often said to flow in the alveolar walls as a sheet of flow rather than in individual capillaries. A series of parallel tubes and a single capillary loop describe discrete vessels, the picture the sheet concept replaces. A portal system is two capillary beds in series, as in the liver or the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, not the alveolar bed. A pulsatile jet does not describe low-pressure flow at a mean capillary pressure of about 7 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar walls carry so many capillaries that in most places they almost touch, so capillary blood is said to flow in the alveolar walls as a \"sheet of flow\" rather than in individual tubes; only about 70 mL of the lungs' 450 mL is in the capillaries. COMLEX: First Aid's diffusion equation shows why this matters: a broad, thin sheet maximizes area and minimizes thickness for gas transfer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 6 — structure and function of the respiratory membrane","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q29_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A physician explains to a student why pulmonary capillary pressure must be kept low. The normal mean pulmonary capillary pressure of 7 mm Hg provides what critical safety margin against pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"It remains 10 mm Hg below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure","B":"It remains 21 mm Hg below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure","C":"It remains 5 mm Hg below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure","D":"It remains 15 mm Hg below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure","E":"It remains 30 mm Hg below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mean pulmonary capillary pressure is approximately 7 mm Hg, while the plasma colloid osmotic pressure is approximately 28 mm Hg. This provides a safety margin of about 21 mm Hg against pulmonary edema development. This is called the \"acute safety factor\" against pulmonary edema. Pulmonary capillary pressure must rise from its normal 7 mm Hg to more than 28 mm Hg before significant pulmonary edema occurs under acute conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With capillary pressure about 7 mm Hg and plasma colloid osmotic pressure about 28 mm Hg, the acute safety factor against pulmonary edema is about 21 mm Hg. COMLEX: Chronic elevation for 2 weeks or more widens it further, because lymphatics expand and can carry up to 10 times more fluid, so chronic mitral stenosis tolerates capillary pressures of 40–45 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — pulmonary lymphatics and their adaptive capacity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q30_b2","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Pulmonary capillary pressure — significance in gas exchange and fluid balance","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology student compares the Starling forces of the lung with those of peripheral subcutaneous tissue. Pulmonary capillary pressure is lower and pulmonary interstitial pressure slightly more negative. Which other force differs, with a value of about 14 mm Hg in the lung compared with less than half of that in most peripheral tissues?","choices":{"A":"Plasma colloid osmotic pressure","B":"Interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure","C":"Net mean filtration pressure","D":"Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure","E":"Alveolar air pressure at rest"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton lists the colloid osmotic pressure of the pulmonary interstitial fluid as about 14 mm Hg, compared with less than half this value in most peripheral tissues, as one of the quantitative differences of lung fluid exchange; it is one of the outward forces (7 capillary hydrostatic + 14 interstitial colloid osmotic + 8 negative interstitial pressure = 29 mm Hg). Plasma colloid osmotic pressure is about 28 mm Hg, the single inward force. Interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure is about −5 to −8 mm Hg, not +14. The mean filtration pressure is the net +1 mm Hg (29 − 28). Alveolar air pressure is 0 mm Hg at rest.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lung interstitial fluid has a colloid osmotic pressure of about 14 mm Hg, more than twice that of most peripheral tissues, and it counts as an outward (filtration-favoring) force at the lung capillary. COMLEX: Removal of leaked plasma protein by lymphatics is what keeps this value from rising and tipping the balance toward edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — lymphatic removal of protein from lung tissue","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q31_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"A pulmonary physiologist explains that normal blood transit time through pulmonary capillaries provides adequate time for gas exchange. However, certain conditions can impair oxygenation despite normal transit time. Which condition would impair oxygenation despite normal 0.8-second transit time?","choices":{"A":"Increased number of open capillaries during mild exercise","B":"Slightly elevated pulmonary arterial pressure at rest","C":"Normal cardiac output with regular respiratory rate","D":"Adequate ventilation with normal alveolar oxygen levels","E":"Thickened alveolar-capillary membrane from pulmonary fibrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Normal transit time of about 0.8 second (Guyton Ch 39) is sufficient for complete oxygenation when the alveolar-capillary membrane is normal. However, if the membrane is thickened (as in pulmonary fibrosis, pulmonary edema, or other interstitial diseases), diffusion is impaired and the normal transit time may become insufficient for complete oxygenation. This is particularly problematic during exercise when transit time shortens to 0.3 seconds. The other options describe normal or favorable conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal transit time (about 0.8 second) gives O2 time to equilibrate, but a thickened alveolar–capillary membrane slows diffusion so blood may leave the capillary before equilibrating. COMLEX: First Aid calls O2 diffusion-limited in fibrosis, which causes early hypoxemia before hypercapnia and a low DLCO.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 7 — fibrosis, edema and emphysema as causes of impaired diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q32_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"During maximal exercise, cardiac output increases from 5 L/min to 30 L/min. The capillary transit time shortens from about 0.8 seconds to 0.3 seconds. Despite this roughly 60% reduction in contact time, complete oxygenation still occurs. What is the primary reason?","choices":{"A":"Massive capillary recruitment increases total surface area available","B":"Blood hemoglobin concentration doubles during exercise periods","C":"Alveolar oxygen concentration increases dramatically during exercise","D":"Pulmonary arterial pressure increases to 100 mm Hg during exercise","E":"Respiratory rate decreases to allow more time per breath"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite the shortened transit time during exercise (from about 0.8 to 0.3 seconds), blood still becomes adequately oxygenated because the number of open capillaries increases up to 3-fold and all capillaries distend. This massive increase in total capillary surface area available for gas exchange compensates for the reduced contact time. Additionally, the alveolar-capillary membrane is extremely thin and efficient, allowing rapid gas exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In maximal exercise, transit time can drop to about 0.3 second, yet oxygenation stays complete because capillary recruitment and distension enlarge the exchange surface and the resting capillary has a large time reserve. COMLEX: Guyton (Ch 41) notes resting blood is nearly saturated after about one-third of the capillary length.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 10 — diffusing capacity at rest vs. during exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q33_b2","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange efficiency","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student asks how long it takes for oxygen to equilibrate between alveolar air and pulmonary capillary blood under normal conditions. What fraction of the total capillary transit time is required for complete oxygen equilibration?","choices":{"A":"Approximately one-tenth of the total transit time","B":"Approximately one-half of the total transit time","C":"Approximately three-fourths of the total transit time","D":"Approximately one-third of the total transit time","E":"The entire total transit time is required"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under resting conditions the blood becomes almost saturated with oxygen by the time it has passed through about one-third of the pulmonary capillary, so it normally stays in the capillaries about three times as long as needed (Guyton Ch 41). This reserve is why, during exercise, when transit time shortens from about 0.8 second to as little as 0.3 second (Guyton Ch 39), the blood can still become almost fully oxygenated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton (Ch 41) shows blood nearly reaches alveolar PO2 after about one-third of its path through the pulmonary capillary, so at rest it stays about three times longer than needed. COMLEX: First Aid's perfusion-limited O2 curve in health becomes diffusion-limited in fibrosis or emphysema, where this reserve is lost.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 8 — solubility vs. pressure gradient in O2 and CO2 diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q34_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student is studying the Starling forces governing fluid balance in pulmonary capillaries. Which force favors movement of fluid OUT of the pulmonary capillaries into the interstitium?","choices":{"A":"Plasma colloid osmotic pressure of 28 mm Hg","B":"Atmospheric pressure at sea level","C":"Alveolar air pressure of 0 mm Hg","D":"Left atrial pressure of 2 mm Hg","E":"Interstitial fluid pressure of -8 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The negative interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure of -8 mm Hg acts as a force favoring filtration OUT of the capillaries by creating a \"pulling\" effect. The forces favoring filtration include: capillary hydrostatic pressure (+7 mm Hg), interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure (+14 mm Hg), and negative interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure (+8 mm Hg when considering its effect on filtration), totaling 29 mm Hg. The plasma colloid osmotic pressure (28 mm Hg) opposes filtration, yielding a net filtration pressure of +1 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Three forces push fluid out of the pulmonary capillary: capillary hydrostatic pressure (7), interstitial colloid osmotic pressure (14) and negative interstitial fluid pressure (8), totalling 29 mm Hg; plasma colloid osmotic pressure (28) pulls it back. COMLEX: Any factor that raises filtration or impairs lymphatic drainage and drives interstitial pressure positive tends to flood the alveoli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — mechanisms and categories of pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q35_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"Rising left atrial pressure in a patient with acute heart failure pushes fluid into the pulmonary interstitium until interstitial fluid pressure rises from its normal negative value to slightly above 0 mm Hg. According to Guyton, what happens at the alveolar wall once interstitial pressure exceeds alveolar air pressure?","choices":{"A":"Lymph flow ceases, and the fluid stays in the interstitium","B":"The alveolar epithelium tears and fluid floods alveoli","C":"Capillaries constrict and filtration stops entirely","D":"Surfactant dissolves the fluid into the alveolar gas","E":"The fluid is drawn back into the capillaries by suction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that the alveolar walls are extremely thin and the alveolar epithelium is so weak that it can be ruptured by any positive pressure in the interstitial spaces greater than alveolar air pressure (above 0 mm Hg), dumping fluid from the interstitium into the alveoli. Normally a slight negative interstitial pressure keeps the alveoli dry by sucking any extra alveolar fluid into the interstitium through small openings between epithelial cells, and lymphatics carry it away. Lymph flow does not cease; it continues to pump fluid but is overwhelmed. Pulmonary capillaries do not constrict to stop filtration in response to a rising interstitial pressure. Surfactant lowers surface tension and does not dissolve fluid into gas. Suction from negative interstitial pressure pulls alveolar fluid into the interstitium, not interstitial fluid into the capillaries, and it is lost once the pressure turns positive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The alveolar epithelium is so thin and weak that any positive interstitial pressure greater than alveolar pressure (above 0 mm Hg) ruptures it, dumping interstitial fluid into the alveoli. COMLEX: Robbins notes that pulmonary congestion and edema of any cause produce heavy, wet lungs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — morphologic features of pulmonary congestion and edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q36_b2","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Pulmonary interstitial fluid dynamics — Starling forces, pulmonary edema, pleural effusions","batch":2,"stem":"A patient inhales chlorine gas, causing damage to the pulmonary capillary membranes. Which component of the Starling equilibrium is most directly affected by this membrane damage?","choices":{"A":"The capillary permeability to plasma proteins","B":"The hydrostatic pressure gradient across the membrane","C":"The lymphatic pumping capacity from lung tissue","D":"The plasma colloid osmotic pressure in capillaries","E":"The negative interstitial pressure in lung tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Damage to pulmonary blood capillary membranes caused by infections (pneumonia) or breathing noxious substances (chlorine gas, sulfur dioxide gas) increases the permeability of the capillary membranes. This allows rapid leakage of both plasma proteins and fluid into the interstitial spaces and alveoli. Once proteins leak into the interstitium, they increase interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, dramatically increasing the driving force for fluid accumulation and causing rapid pulmonary edema development. The gas injures the capillary membrane, not the lymphatics; lymphatic pumping is a separate safeguard that is overwhelmed rather than directly damaged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chlorine or sulfur dioxide gas, like pneumonia, damages pulmonary capillary membranes and raises their permeability, so plasma proteins and fluid leak rapidly into the interstitium and alveoli. COMLEX: Robbins lists inhaled gases (smoke, high oxygen concentrations) among the direct injuries causing edema from alveolar wall injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — ARDS pathogenesis from endothelial and epithelial injury","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q37_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist measures pleural pressure in a healthy subject and finds it to be -7 mm Hg. The student asks why this pressure is more negative than the lung's collapse tendency of -4 mm Hg. What explains this additional negativity?","choices":{"A":"The lymphatic system pumps more fluid than filters out","B":"The diaphragm actively creates additional negative pressure","C":"The intercostal muscles compress the pleural space","D":"The alveolar air pressure is positive during breathing","E":"The chest wall naturally expands creating more negativity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pleural fluid pressure is usually about -7 mm Hg, which is a few millimeters more negative than the collapse pressure of the lungs (-4 mm Hg). This additional negativity occurs because the lymphatic system continuously pumps fluid from the pleural space. When fluid is pumped out faster than it filters in, the pressure becomes more negative than would be expected from lung elastic recoil alone. This lymphatic pumping is the basic mechanism creating negative pressure in the pleural space and other tissue spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pleural pressure (about −7 mm Hg) is more negative than the lungs' collapse tendency (about −4 mm Hg) because lymphatics continually pump fluid out of the pleural space, the same mechanism that makes other tissue spaces negative. COMLEX: Guyton lists blockage of this lymphatic drainage first among the causes of pleural effusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — roles of the lymphatic system in the lungs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q38_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student asks what would happen if the negative pleural pressure were eliminated but no air entered the pleural space. If pleural pressure became exactly 0 mm Hg without pneumothorax, what would occur?","choices":{"A":"The lungs would expand maximally to fill the chest","B":"The lungs would maintain their current volume exactly","C":"The lungs would oscillate between expansion and collapse","D":"The lungs would increase perfusion to compensate","E":"The lungs would collapse from unopposed elastic recoil"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lungs have a continual elastic tendency to collapse; they stay expanded only because pleural pressure is negative. If pleural pressure became 0 mm Hg (atmospheric), nothing would oppose that recoil and the lungs would collapse, just as they do in pneumothorax (Guyton Ch 38: the lung collapses like a balloon whenever there is no force to keep it inflated). They would not expand, hold their volume, or oscillate, and perfusion changes cannot substitute for the lost expanding pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lungs stay expanded only because pleural pressure is at least as negative as their collapse tendency (about −4 mm Hg); at 0 mm Hg the unopposed elastic recoil would collapse them. COMLEX: Guyton (Ch 38) attributes about two-thirds of that recoil to alveolar surface tension and one-third to tissue elastic fibers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — elastic recoil, pleural pressure and lung expansion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q39_b2","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Negative pleural fluid pressure — role in maintaining lung expansion","batch":2,"stem":"A physician explains that negative pleural pressure serves multiple functions. Beyond keeping the lungs expanded, what other physiological function does negative pleural pressure facilitate?","choices":{"A":"It enhances gas exchange across alveolar membranes directly","B":"It assists venous return to the right side of the heart","C":"It increases arterial blood pressure throughout the body","D":"It stimulates surfactant production in type II pneumocytes","E":"It maintains bronchodilation during normal breathing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Negative pleural pressure, which is transmitted to the mediastinal structures including the great veins, assists venous return to the right atrium. During inspiration, pleural pressure becomes even more negative (from about −5 to −7.5 cm H₂O in quiet breathing, Guyton Ch 38), creating a greater pressure gradient that enhances venous return. This \"thoracic pump\" mechanism is an important auxiliary mechanism for venous return, particularly during deep breathing or increased respiratory effort. Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar epithelial cells; pleural pressure does not stimulate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Negative intrathoracic pressure, made more negative during inspiration (about −5 to −7.5 cm H2O in quiet breathing), helps draw blood back to the right heart. COMLEX: The OMM course adds that the thoracic pressure gradient of respiration also draws lymph centrally toward the venous circulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — mechanics of inspiration and expiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q40_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old patient with nephrotic syndrome develops bilateral pleural effusions. The patient's plasma albumin is severely reduced, causing plasma colloid osmotic pressure to fall from 28 mm Hg to 12 mm Hg. Which mechanism explains this pleural effusion development?","choices":{"A":"Increased lymphatic drainage removes excessive fluid","B":"Decreased capillary permeability prevents protein retention","C":"Low plasma colloid osmotic pressure allows excessive filtration","D":"Elevated plasma colloid osmotic pressure draws fluid inward","E":"Normal Starling forces maintain equilibrium preventing effusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Greatly reduced plasma colloid osmotic pressure, such as occurs in nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, or severe malnutrition, allows excessive transudation of fluid into the pleural cavity. When plasma colloid osmotic pressure falls, the inward force opposing filtration decreases, causing the net filtration pressure to become more positive. This results in excessive fluid accumulation that overwhelms the lymphatic drainage capacity, leading to pleural effusion. This is a classic transudate. Capillary permeability is not reduced here; increased permeability from infection or inflammation of the pleural surfaces is a separate cause of effusion that would let protein out, not hold it in.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A large fall in plasma colloid osmotic pressure lets fluid transude excessively into the pleural cavity, one of Guyton's four causes of pleural effusion. COMLEX: First Aid lists nephrotic syndrome and cirrhosis as low-oncotic-pressure causes of a transudative effusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — hemodynamic edema from decreased oncotic pressure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q41_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with advanced cancer develops a pleural effusion that contains high protein concentration. Analysis shows the effusion protein is nearly equal to plasma protein levels. What mechanism most likely caused this exudative effusion?","choices":{"A":"Congestive heart failure increasing hydrostatic pressure","B":"Decreased plasma colloid osmotic pressure from malnutrition","C":"Normal capillary permeability with intact membranes","D":"Blockage of lymphatic drainage from malignant involvement","E":"Enhanced lymphatic function removing excessive protein"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When pleural effusion contains high protein concentration approaching plasma levels, this indicates an exudative effusion typically caused by either increased capillary permeability or blocked lymphatic drainage. In cancer patients, malignant involvement of pleural surfaces and lymphatic channels commonly blocks lymphatic drainage from the pleural cavity. When lymphatics are blocked, protein that normally leaks into the pleural space cannot be removed, accumulating and creating high oncotic pressure that draws more fluid into the pleural space.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An effusion with protein close to plasma levels is an exudate, arising from leaky capillaries or blocked pleural lymphatic drainage. COMLEX: First Aid lists malignancy among exudate causes; by Light's criteria a pleural/serum protein ratio above 0.5 alone makes it an exudate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — pleural effusions, Light's criteria and common causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_ex1_q42_b2","objective":15,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 15 — Distinct mechanisms by which pleural effusions occur","batch":2,"stem":"Normally each pleural cavity holds only a few milliliters of mucoid fluid. Whenever the quantity becomes more than barely enough to begin flowing, the excess is pumped away by lymphatic vessels that open directly from the pleural cavity. Blocking these vessels causes a pleural effusion. Into which sites do these lymphatic vessels open?","choices":{"A":"Hilum, visceral pleura, and the bronchial walls","B":"Thoracic duct, azygos vein, and the hilar nodes","C":"Mediastinum, upper diaphragm, and parietal pleura","D":"Pericardium, lung apex, and the interlobar fissures","E":"Alveolar walls, bronchioles, and the hilar lymph nodes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that excess pleural fluid is pumped away by lymphatic vessels opening directly from the pleural cavity into the mediastinum, the superior surface of the diaphragm, and the lateral surfaces of the parietal pleura; this pumping also creates the negative pleural pressure, and blockage of this lymphatic drainage is the first cause of pleural effusion he lists. The hilum, the visceral pleura, and the bronchial walls describe the lung's own lymphatics, which begin around the terminal bronchioles and run to the hilum, not the pleural-cavity drainage. The thoracic duct and azygos vein are downstream channels, not the sites where pleural lymphatics open. The pericardium, apex, and fissures, and the alveolar walls and bronchioles, are not the sites Guyton names.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Excess pleural fluid drains through lymphatics that open directly from the pleural cavity into the mediastinum, the superior surface of the diaphragm and the lateral parietal pleura; this pumping also keeps the space a \"potential space\" with negative pressure. COMLEX: Robbins lists lymphatic obstruction as a rare cause of hemodynamic edema, and Guyton lists blocked pleural lymphatics as a cause of effusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — mechanisms and causes of pleural effusion","isHighYield":false}]};
